= General relativity
{wiki}

= Congruence
{disambiguate=general relativity}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

= Congruence of curves in general relativity
{synonym}

A congruence in <general relativity> is a family of curves through an open spacetime region such that each point lies on exactly one member. Its tangent field describes the family locally. A <geodesic congruence> is the special case in which every member is a <geodesic>.

= Causal structure
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

The <causal structure> of a spacetime classifies separations and tangent directions as timelike, null or spacelike through its light cones. It determines which events can communicate by causal curves. A <null curve> is one member of this broader geometric structure.

= Flat-background scalar-force theory of gravity
{parent=General relativity}
{title2=$dp^i=\partial^i\Phi\,p_jdx^j-(p^j\partial_j\Phi)dx^i$}

= Scalar-force gravity
{synonym}

This covariant scalar-force model uses the <Minkowski metric> and the displayed momentum law, with $\Box\Phi=-4\pi G T^i{}_i$. For a massive <test particle>, write $p^i=mu^i$, $u^iu_i=1$. Contracting the force law with $p_i$ gives constant <rest mass>; the acceleration is $du^i/d\tau=\partial^i\Phi-u^i(u^j\partial_j\Phi)$, independent of the mass. A static weak field obeys the <Poisson equation> and yields Newtonian acceleration $-\boldsymbol\nabla\Phi$. For a <photon>, $p\cdot dx=0$ and $dx$ is parallel to $p$, so $dp^i=-p^i d\Phi$: photon energies change but their unparametrized paths remain straight. The motion law has the same unparametrized curves as the conformal metric $e^{2\Phi}\eta$, but the field equation must not be silently identified with a different scalar-gravity theory.

= Scalar-force gravitational redshift
{parent=Flat-background scalar-force theory of gravity}
{title2=$\nu_r/\nu_e=e^{\Phi_e-\Phi_r}$}

For a <photon> in the <flat-background scalar-force theory of gravity>, $e^\Phi p^i$ is constant along its path. In a static field, energies measured by stationary laboratories therefore satisfy the displayed ratio. At first order, $\Delta\nu/\nu=-\Delta\Phi$ in units $c=1$, matching the weak-field <Pound-Rebka experiment>. This agreement does not imply light bending: a momentum change parallel to the original null tangent changes its scale, not its direction.

= Taub–NUT space
{parent=General relativity}
{c}

The single-centre Euclidean Taub–NUT geometry is a gravitational instanton with a circle fibre over three-dimensional space. Its fibre shrinks smoothly at the centre when its period is chosen appropriately. Adding flat worldvolume directions gives the local geometry of a <Kaluza-Klein monopole>.

// Target: algebra.bigb

= Near-horizon limit
{parent=General relativity}

A limit retaining the geometry near a horizon while discarding terms describing its distant asymptotic region. For a regular extremal brane throat, removing the constant from its harmonic function can yield an <anti-de Sitter spacetime> factor. Local symmetries and supersymmetries of the limiting geometry need not extend to the full original spacetime.

= Einstein-Maxwell theory
{c}
{parent=General relativity}

The Einstein-Maxwell system couples the metric to an electromagnetic field. In geometrized units its source-free equations are $G_{ab}=8\pi T_{ab}^{\mathrm{EM}}$, $\nabla_aF^{ab}=0$ and $dF=0$, with $T_{ab}^{\mathrm{EM}}=(F_{ac}F_b{}^c-\tfrac14g_{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd})/(4\pi)$. Electrovacuum need not have vanishing Ricci tensor, so a Komar integral need not be unchanged between different enclosing surfaces.

= Majumdar–Papapetrou solution
{c}
{parent=Einstein-Maxwell theory}
{title2=$ds^2=-H^{-2}dt^2+H^2d\mathbf x^2$}

The four-dimensional <Majumdar–Papapetrou solution> is a static multi-black-hole Einstein-Maxwell solution with $H=1+\sum_iM_i/|\mathbf x-\mathbf x_i|$ and positive geometrized masses $M_i$. The electromagnetic potential can be written $A=\pm(H^{-1}-1)dt$, with a common sign of charge. Its mass at infinity is $\sum_iM_i$. Each distinct centre is an extremal horizon of zero surface gravity rather than a pointlike naked metric singularity.

= Analytic extension of a Majumdar–Papapetrou horizon
{parent=Majumdar–Papapetrou solution}

Near a distinct centre of mass $M$, write $H=M/r+h+rF(r,\theta,\phi)$, where $h$ is the regular part evaluated at that centre and $F$ is analytic in local angular charts. Set $A=M+hr$, $Q=rH=A+r^2F$ and $dv=dt+(h+M/r)^2dr$. The metric becomes
$$
ds^2=-\frac{r^2}{Q^2}dv^2+2\frac{A^2}{Q^2}dv\,dr+F\frac{(Q+A)(Q^2+A^2)}{Q^2}dr^2+Q^2d\Omega_2^2.
$$
All coefficients extend analytically across $r=0$, with $Q(0)=M>0$ and two-dimensional determinant $-1$. The surface is null, its generator is $\partial_v$, and the double zero of its squared norm gives zero surface gravity. The construction uses a constant $h$ so that the coordinate differential is integrable despite the nonspherical regular part of $H$.

= Twistor theory
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

<Twistor theory> encodes the <conformal geometry> of four-dimensional spacetime using incidence geometry in a complex vector space. In the flat correspondence, a spacetime point is a two-plane in a four-dimensional <twistor space>, and hence a projective line in <projective twistor space>. Null separation becomes intersection of these lines. Choosing a spacetime signature is an additional reality condition on the complex geometry.

= Twistor correspondence
{parent=Twistor theory}
{title2=$\operatorname{Gr}(2,\mathbb C^4)\leftarrow F_{1,2}(\mathbb C^4)\rightarrow\mathbb{CP}^3$}

The <flag manifold> of a line $\ell$ contained in a two-plane $W$ has projections $(\ell,W)\mapsto W$ and $(\ell,W)\mapsto\ell$. Their fibers are respectively $\mathbb{CP}^1$ and $\mathbb{CP}^2$. This is the flat <twistor correspondence>: <complexified compactified Minkowski space> is the <Grassmannian> of two-planes in <twistor space>, while <projective twistor space> is its projective space of lines.

= Penrose-Ward correspondence
{parent=Twistor correspondence}
{c}

A <holomorphic vector bundle> on an appropriate twistor domain, trivial on its space-time incidence lines, determines a complex anti-self-dual gauge <gauge connection>. Split its patching <matrix> on each line and apply the tangent vector fields $\pi^{A'}\partial_{AA'}$. They annihilate the unsplit twistor patching <matrix>, so the two logarithmic <derivatives> of the factors agree and define a global degree-one <function> of $\pi$. It is consequently $\pi^{A'}A_{AA'}$. The inverse splitting <bundle frame> is parallel along each <alpha-plane>; commuting these parallel equations proves that the primed <gauge curvature> <two-component spinor> vanishes. Reality conditions recover unitary Euclidean <gauge fields>; Euclidean <instanton> bundles need triviality on the real <twistor lines>, not on every possible complex line.

= Abelian Ward splitting by Laurent series
{parent=Penrose-Ward correspondence}
{c}
{title2=$f_x(\zeta)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}f_n(x)\zeta^n=h_1-h_0$}

On a <twistor line> use $\pi_{0'}=\zeta,\pi_{1'}=1$ and $\omega^A=x^{A0'}\zeta+x^{A1'}$. For a degree-zero <holomorphic> patching logarithm, its <Laurent series> splits as $h_0=-\sum_{n\ge0}f_n\zeta^n$, $h_1=\sum_{n<0}f_n\zeta^n$. The <twistor incidence relation> implies $\partial_{A0'}f_n=\partial_{A1'}f_{n-1}$. Hence $(-\partial_{A0'}+\zeta\partial_{A1'})h_\alpha=\partial_{A1'}f_{-1}$ on both patches. The <gauge potential> is $A_{A0'}=-\partial_{A1'}f_{-1}$, $A_{A1'}=0$. Exponentiating the two additive factors produces the linewise <holomorphic> splitting of $e^f$.

= Contour potential for an anti-self-dual Maxwell field
{parent=Abelian Ward splitting by Laurent series}
{title2=$A_{BB'}=\frac1{2\pi i}\oint_\Gamma\frac{\iota_{B'}}{\iota_{C'}\pi^{C'}}\,f_{,B}\,\pi_{D'}d\pi^{D'}$}

The <Abelian Ward splitting by Laurent series> gives this <gauge potential> through the <Cauchy integral formula>, with $f_{,B}=\partial f/\partial\omega^B$ evaluated on the <twistor line>. The integrand has total projective degree zero. Holding the contour fixed locally and differentiating gives $F_{AA'BB'}=\epsilon_{A'B'}(2\pi i)^{-1}\oint_\Gamma f_{,AB}\,\pi_{D'}d\pi^{D'}$, using $\pi_{A'}\iota_{B'}-\pi_{B'}\iota_{A'}=\epsilon_{A'B'}(\iota_{C'}\pi^{C'})$. Its unprimed <two-component spinor> is symmetric, so the primed <gauge curvature> vanishes and the field obeys the <anti-self-dual Maxwell equations>. These signs use $\epsilon_{0'1'}=1$, $\epsilon^{0'1'}=-1$ and raising by left multiplication; other consistent raising conventions can reverse both displayed overall signs.

= Line-trivial bundles in the Ward correspondence
{parent=Penrose-Ward correspondence}
{title2=$E|_{L_x}\cong\mathcal O^{\oplus r}$}

= Line-trivial bundle in the Ward correspondence
{synonym}

On a twistor domain containing the lines $L_x$ for $x$ in a space-time domain $U$, the Ward construction uses a <holomorphic vector bundle> $E$ with $E|_{L_x}\cong\mathcal O^{\oplus r}$ for every $x\in U$. A degree-zero restriction is not sufficient: $\mathcal O(1)\oplus\mathcal O(-1)$ has degree zero but is not holomorphically trivial. A trivial restriction has $r$ independent global <holomorphic> sections; their evaluations give a linewise <bundle frame> and hence a factorization of its patching <matrix>. Nearby factors can be chosen holomorphically because $H^1(\mathbb{CP}^1,\mathcal O)^{r^2}=0$. Lines on which the splitting type is nontrivial are called jumping lines and obstruct this regular reconstruction there. For a globally regular field on affine <complexified Minkowski space> the condition is needed on all its incidence lines; on a smaller domain only those lines are required.

= Holomorphic splitting of a twistor patching matrix
{parent=Penrose-Ward correspondence}
{title2=$F=\widetilde H H^{-1}$}

A bundle trivial on a <twistor line> has a global holomorphic <bundle frame> there. Comparing it with the given two patch <bundle frames> produces invertible <matrices> $H,\widetilde H$, holomorphic on their respective affine patches, with $F=\widetilde H H^{-1}$. The obstruction to varying such a <bundle frame> locally with space-time is $H^1(\mathbb{CP}^1,\mathcal O)^{n^2}=0$, so the splitting factors can be chosen holomorphically in a neighbourhood. If two splittings differ by $H\mapsto Hg$ and $\widetilde H\mapsto\widetilde Hg$, then $g$ is holomorphic on the compact <complex projective line> and hence independent of its projective coordinate. It is the space-time gauge freedom.

= Alpha-plane
{parent=Twistor correspondence}
{title2=$A_\ell=\{W:\ell\subset W\}\cong\mathbb{CP}^2$}

An alpha-plane in the compact <twistor correspondence> is the set of two-planes containing a fixed line $\ell$ in <twistor space>. Its affine portion has tangent vectors $u^A\pi^{A'}$ for fixed primed spinor $\pi$, so any two tangent vectors are orthogonal and null. Two distinct compact alpha-planes meet at the unique two-plane spanned by their defining lines; that point can lie at conformal infinity.

= Twistor line
{parent=Twistor correspondence}
{title2=$L_x=\mathbb P(W_x)\cong\mathbb{CP}^1$}

The projective line associated to a two-plane $W_x$ representing a point of <complexified compactified Minkowski space>. Two distinct <twistor lines> intersect precisely when their spacetime points are null separated. On the Lorentzian real slice, the line lies entirely in <projective null twistor space> and parametrizes the null geodesics through the point.

= Complexified compactified Minkowski space
{parent=Twistor correspondence}
{title2=$\mathbb M_{\mathbb C}=\operatorname{Gr}(2,\mathbb C^4)$}

The four-complex-dimensional <Grassmannian> of two-planes in <twistor space>. Its <Plücker embedding> is the <Klein quadric>. An affine chart consists of graphs $W_x=\{(ix\pi,\pi)\}$. A <Lorentzian reality structure on twistor space> or an <Euclidean reality structure on twistor space> selects different four-real-dimensional compact spacetimes inside this same complex manifold.

= Twistor space
{parent=Twistor theory}
{title2=$\mathbb T\cong\mathbb C^4$}
{wiki}

In flat <twistor theory>, a twistor is a pair $Z=(\omega^A,\pi_{A'})$ of two-component spinors. The <twistor incidence relation> with a spacetime point is $\omega^A=ix^{AA'}\pi_{A'}$. The nonzero vector $Z$ and its projective class play different roles: the latter belongs to <projective twistor space>.

= Euclidean reality structure on twistor space
{c}
{parent=Twistor space}
{title2=$J^2=-1,\quad J(W)=W$}

Choose an antilinear quaternionic structure $J$ on $\mathbb C^4$. It induces a fixed-point-free involution on <projective twistor space>. A real Euclidean spacetime point is a $J$-invariant complex two-plane, equivalently a quaternionic line in $\mathbb H^2$. These form $\mathbb{HP}^1\cong S^4$, the <conformal compactification of Euclidean space>. Every projective twistor lies on the unique invariant line $\mathbb P\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb C}(Z,JZ)$, giving a fibration $\mathbb{CP}^3\to S^4$ with fiber $\mathbb{CP}^1$.

= Twistor Hermitian form
{parent=Twistor space}
{title2=$h(Z,W)=\omega^A\overline{\rho}_A+\pi_{A'}\overline{\eta}^{A'}$}

For $Z=(\omega,\pi)$ and $W=(\eta,\rho)$ the displayed <Hermitian form> has signature $(2,2)$. It is zero on every pair of vectors in $W_x=\{(ix\pi,\pi)\}$ when $x$ is Hermitian, that is, Lorentzian real. Conversely a transverse totally isotropic two-plane is such a graph with $x$ Hermitian. This gives the <Lorentzian reality structure on twistor space>.

= Lorentzian reality structure on twistor space
{c}
{parent=Twistor Hermitian form}

Real compactified Minkowski points are the two-dimensional <totally isotropic subspaces> for the signature-$(2,2)$ <twistor Hermitian form>. In a graph chart this is the usual Hermitian reality condition on $x^{AA'}$. Their <twistor lines> lie in <projective null twistor space>. On the <Klein quadric> the same real form is the projectivized null cone of a real quadratic form of signature $(2,4)$.

= Null twistor
{parent=Twistor Hermitian form}
{title2=$h(Z,Z)=0$}

A nonzero twistor null for the <twistor Hermitian form>. Its projective class determines a real <null geodesic> in compactified Minkowski space. For nonzero $\pi$, its affine real solutions of the <twistor incidence relation> have the form $x=x_0+r\overline\pi^A\pi^{A'}$, $r\in\mathbb R$. Twistors with $\pi=0$ encode generators at infinity rather than finite affine solutions.

= Projective null twistor space
{parent=Null twistor}
{title2=$PN=\{[Z]\in\mathbb{CP}^3:h(Z,Z)=0\}$}

The five-real-dimensional space of projective <null twistors>. A projective line lies entirely in it precisely when its corresponding two-plane is totally isotropic for the <twistor Hermitian form>. Such lines represent real points of <Minkowski conformal compactification>. Two independent null twistors lie on one such line exactly when $h(X,Y)=0$.

= Twistor incidence relation
{parent=Twistor space}
{title2=$\omega^A=ix^{AA'}\pi_{A'}$}

For a fixed affine spacetime point $x$, this linear relation cuts out a two-dimensional subspace $W_x=\{(ix\pi,\pi)\}$ of <twistor space>. Projectivizing gives its <twistor line>. For a fixed projective twistor with $\pi\ne0$, its solutions are $x=x_0+u^A\pi^{A'}$, a totally null affine <alpha-plane>. The compact description replaces affine solutions by the two-planes containing the fixed twistor line in the vector space.

= Projective twistor space
{parent=Twistor space}
{title2=$\mathbb P\mathbb T\cong\mathbb{CP}^3$}

The <projective space> of one-dimensional complex subspaces of <twistor space>. A point corresponds to an <alpha-plane> in <complexified compactified Minkowski space>; a projective line corresponds to a spacetime point.

= Affine projective twistor space
{parent=Projective twistor space}
{title2=$PT_{\rm aff}=\mathbb{CP}^3\setminus\mathbb{CP}^1_\infty$}

For affine <complexified Minkowski space>, the relevant twistor domain is
$$
PT_{\rm aff}=\{[\omega^A,\pi_{A'}]\in\mathbb{CP}^3:\pi\ne0\}
=\mathbb{CP}^3\setminus\mathbb{CP}^1_\infty
\cong\operatorname{Tot}(\mathcal O(1)\oplus\mathcal O(1)).
$$
Incidence can be written $\omega^A=x^{AA'}\pi_{A'}$, absorbing the conventional factor $i$ into the definition of $x$. Each space-time point gives a <twistor line> $L_x\cong\mathbb{CP}^1$. On charts $\pi_{0'}\ne0$ and $\pi_{1'}\ne0$, the fibre coordinates transform by $\omega/\pi_{1'}=(\omega/\pi_{0'})/\lambda$, with $\lambda=\pi_{1'}/\pi_{0'}$, giving the two $\mathcal O(1)$ summands. The full <projective twistor space> corresponds to <complexified compactified Minkowski space>.

= Analytic extension of a spacetime
{parent=General relativity}

= Analytic extensions of a spacetime
{synonym}

An analytic extension embeds a spacetime isometrically as a proper open region in a larger analytic <Lorentzian manifold> with a nondegenerate <metric tensor>. A divergence of coordinate components does not obstruct extension if a new chart makes all metric coefficients analytic and the determinant nonzero. For a static spherical metric with an analytic lapse $f$ and a positive simple horizon radius, ingoing coordinates replace $-f\,dt^2+dr^2/f$ by $-f\,dv^2+2\,dv\,dr$. This removes the <coordinate singularity> at $f=0$; transverse metric coefficients must also remain regular. An extension across such a horizon is distinct from extending through a <curvature singularity>.

= Two-sheeted extension of superextremal Kerr spacetime
{parent=Analytic extension of a spacetime}
{title2=$\rho^2/(r^2+a^2)+z^2/r^2=1$}

For $0<M<a$, $\Delta=(r-M)^2+a^2-M^2>0$, so no horizon blocks need adjoining. The oblate Cartesian coordinates of the <Kerr metric> satisfy the displayed relation, where $\rho^2=x^2+y^2$. Keeping only the positive root $r$ leaves a cut along the disk $z=0$, $\rho<a$. Continue $r$ through zero away from its boundary, the <Kerr ring singularity>, onto a negative-radius sheet. Equivalently cut two Cartesian sheets along the disk and join opposite banks, so $r$ changes sign continuously. The metric is analytic at the disk interior in signed oblate coordinates since $\Sigma=a^2\cos^2\theta\ne0$ there. The extension has asymptotically flat ends at $r\to+\infty$ and $r\to-\infty$; only the singular ring remains inextendible by an analytic Lorentz metric.

= Gravitational redshift
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}
{title2=$\nu_{\mathrm r}/\nu_{\mathrm e}=N_{\mathrm e}/N_{\mathrm r}$}

In a static spacetime with $g_{tt}=-N^2$, a photon has conserved <Killing energy> $E=-p_t$. A static observer with four-velocity $N^{-1}\partial_t$ measures frequency proportional to $E/N$. Hence light emitted at one lapse and received at another obeys the displayed ratio. For $N=1+\Phi/c^2+O(\Phi^2/c^4)$ it gives the weak-field shift $(\nu_{\mathrm r}-\nu_{\mathrm e})/\nu_{\mathrm e}=(\Phi_{\mathrm e}-\Phi_{\mathrm r})/c^2$. This local clock effect does not require light-ray bending.

= Outgoing pulse redshift from a radial Schwarzschild infaller
{parent=Gravitational redshift}
{title2=$\nu_\infty/\nu_{\mathrm{em}}=f/(E+\sqrt{E^2-f})$}

A radial infalling <geodesic> in <Schwarzschild spacetime> has $dt/ds=E/f$ and $dr/ds=-\sqrt{E^2-f}$, where $f=1-2M/r$. An outward <radial null geodesic> has fixed retarded time $u=t-r_*$ with $dr_*/dr=1/f$. Consequently $du/ds=(E+\sqrt{E^2-f})/f$. This factor stretches a short emitted pulse's duration, and its reciprocal multiplies the received frequency at infinity. Near horizon crossing $s_H$, it becomes $du/ds\sim4M/(s_H-s)$, including both <gravitational redshift> and the emitter's <Doppler shift>. The local short-pulse approximation requires its emitted duration to be much smaller than $s_H-s$.

// Target: general-relativity.bigb

= Pound-Rebka experiment
{parent=Gravitational redshift}
{c}
{wiki=Pound–Rebka_experiment}

The experiment compares gamma-ray emission and resonant absorption at different heights in Earth's field, using the <Mossbauer effect> to resolve the small <gravitational redshift>. Its weak-field result is the frequency shift $\Delta\nu/\nu=-gH/c^2$ for an upward ray over height $H$. Any metric theory with the corresponding static lapse predicts this leading effect; the test alone does not distinguish <general relativity> from the weak-field prediction of <Nordstrom theory of gravitation>.

= Planar warped spacetime
{parent=General relativity}
{title2=$ds^2=A(z)^2(-dt^2+dx^2+dy^2)+dz^2$}

For positive smooth $A$, this <Lorentzian manifold> has flat $(t,x,y)$ slices scaled by $A(z)^2$. Translation in each slice coordinate gives a <Killing vector field>. An <orthonormal coframe> is $e^0=A\,dt$, $e^1=A\,dx$, $e^2=A\,dy$, $e^3=dz$. Its <Levi-Civita connection> and <Ricci tensor> are controlled by $a=A'/A$ and $q=A''/A=a'+a^2$.

= Cartan curvature of a planar warped spacetime
{c}
{parent=Planar warped spacetime}
{title2=$a=A'/A,\quad q=A''/A$}

For the <orthonormal coframe> of a <planar warped spacetime>, <Cartan's first structure equation> gives $\omega^i{}_3=a e^i$ and $\omega^3{}_i=-s_i a e^i$, where $i=0,1,2$ and $(s_0,s_1,s_2)=(-1,1,1)$. All other <connection 1-forms> vanish. <Cartan's second structure equation> gives $\Theta^i{}_3=-q e^i\wedge e^3$ and $\Theta^i{}_j=-s_j a^2e^i\wedge e^j$ for $i\ne j$. Lowering the first index makes both the <connection 1-forms> and the <curvature 2-forms> antisymmetric. Contraction yields $R_{00}=q+2a^2$, $R_{11}=R_{22}=-q-2a^2$, and $R_{33}=-3q$.

= Diffeomorphism invariance of general relativity
{parent=General relativity}

Transforming a <metric tensor> and all matter fields together by the pullback of a <diffeomorphism> preserves the <Einstein field equations> and covariant matter equations. Passive coordinate changes only change the description of the fields; active pullbacks produce another representative of the geometry. The treatment of transformations acting on a boundary depends on the boundary conditions and possible charges. A <diffeomorphism> is not necessarily an <isometry> of a fixed metric.

= Timelike geodesic
{parent=General relativity}

A <timelike geodesic> has timelike tangent and vanishing covariant acceleration in an <affine parameter>. Choosing <proper time> gives $g(\dot\gamma,\dot\gamma)=-1$.

= Odd radial perturbations preserve timelike-geodesic periods
{parent=Timelike geodesic}
{title2=$T_\tau=T_t=2\pi$}

For $ds^2=(1+f(x))^2dx^2/(1+x^2)-(1+x^2)dt^2$, with smooth odd $f$ and $|f|<1$, the conserved <energy> gives $dt/d\tau=E/(1+x^2)$ and $(dx/d\tau)^2=(E^2-1-x^2)/(1+f)^2$. Both half-cycle <integrals> over $[-\sqrt{E^2-1},\sqrt{E^2-1}]$ contain an even weight times $1+f$. The odd contribution vanishes, leaving the unperturbed half-period $\pi$ in each clock. The waveforms need not be sinusoidal, but both periods are unchanged for $E>1$.

= Timelike geodesics of an inverse-time conformal metric
{parent=Timelike geodesic}
{title2=$ds^2=t^{-2}(dx^2-dt^2)$}

In a positive-time patch, proper-time first integrals give $\dot x=pt^2$ and $\dot t^2=t^2+p^2t^4$. If $p\ne0$, the unparametrized <timelike geodesics> are $t^2=(x-x_c)^2-p^{-2}$ on an appropriate branch; in the expanded quadratic form their discriminant is positive. If $p=0$, there is the separate family $x=x_c$, $t=t_*e^{\pm\tau}$. A description using only the nonvertical quadratic family omits genuine timelike geodesics.

= Coordinate singularity
{parent=General relativity}

A <coordinate singularity> is a failure of a coordinate description which disappears in a regular chart. The horizon singularity of the <Schwarzschild metric> in $(t,r)$ coordinates is removable; its $r=0$ <curvature singularity> is not.

= Areal radius
{parent=General relativity}
{title2=$r$}

In a spherically symmetric <spacetime>, the <areal radius> $r$ is defined by the area $A=4\pi r^2$ of each symmetry sphere. It is independent of which radial coordinate chart is used.

= Geometrized units
{parent=General relativity}

<Geometrized units> set $G=c=1$, allowing <mass>, length, and time to be expressed in common units. Restore $GM/c^2$ for a mass parameter in a radial <metric tensor>.

= Geometrized luminosity
{parent=Geometrized units}
{title2=$L_{\rm geom}=GL_{\rm SI}/c^5$}

A <geometrized luminosity> is dimensionless because in <geometrized units> an energy and a time have the same dimension. Starting from SI <luminosity>, multiply by $G/c^5$. The corresponding SI power unit is $c^5/G$. Radiating a fraction $f$ of a mass $M$ at constant SI <luminosity> $L$ takes $fMc^2/L$, or $f(GM/c^3)/L_{\rm geom}$ in seconds.

= Vaidya metric
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{title2=$ds^2=-(1-2M(v)/r)dv^2+2dv\,dr+r^2d\Omega^2$}

The ingoing Vaidya metric describes a spherically symmetric spacetime sourced by radial null matter, using advanced time $v$ and areal radius $r$. In geometrized units it is $ds^2=-(1-2M(v)/r)dv^2+2\,dv\,dr+r^2d\Omega^2$. A constant mass function reduces it to the <Schwarzschild metric> in ingoing null coordinates; a varying mass function changes the curvature and the radial <null geodesics>.

= Teleological response of a Vaidya event horizon
{parent=Vaidya metric}
{title2=$\rho(v)=\frac1{2M_0}\int_v^\infty e^{-(s-v)/(4M_0)}\mu(s)\,ds$}

For a small bounded mass perturbation $m(v)=M_0+\mu(v)$ that becomes constant in the future, write the <event horizon> radius as $r_H(v)=2M_0+\rho(v)$. The outgoing <radial null geodesics of the Vaidya metric> give $\rho'-\rho/(4M_0)=-\mu/(2M_0)$ to first order. Future stationarity selects the displayed solution and removes the exponentially growing homogeneous solution. If $\mu'\geq0$, integration by parts gives $\rho-2\mu=2\int_v^\infty e^{-(s-v)/(4M_0)}\mu'(s)\,ds\geq0$. Thus the <event horizon> can grow before local matter arrives, lying outside the instantaneous spherical <apparent horizon> $r=2m(v)$. This is a consequence of the <event horizon>'s global definition, not propagation of a local signal into the past.

= Radial null geodesics of the Vaidya metric
{parent=Vaidya metric}
{title2=$v=\mathrm{const},\quad dr/dv=(1-2M/r)/2$}

= Radial null geodesic of the Vaidya metric
{synonym}

The two radial <null geodesic> families are $v=\text{constant}$ and $dr/dv=(1-2M(v)/r)/2$. The first has affine radius, while the second has tangent $k=\partial_v+(f/2)\partial_r$ obeying $\nabla_kk=(M/r^2)k$, so advanced time is generally a nonaffine parameter. An <affine parameter> $\lambda$ for the outgoing family satisfies $d\log(dv/d\lambda)/dv=-M/r^2$.

= Reconstructing Vaidya mass from a radial null geodesic
{parent=Radial null geodesics of the Vaidya metric}
{title2=$M(v)=\tfrac12r_0(v)(1-2r_0^{\prime}(v))$}

An outgoing <radial null geodesic of the Vaidya metric> prescribed as $r=r_0(v)$ determines the mass along that curve by $M(v)=r_0(v)(1-2r'_0(v))/2$. This inverse relation comes from the radial <null condition>. It determines a function of advanced time wherever the prescribed curve is differentiable and has positive radius.

= Christoffel symbols of the Vaidya metric
{c}
{parent=Vaidya metric}
{title2=$\Gamma^\mu{}_{\nu\rho}$}

Writing $f=1-2M(v)/r$, the radial <Christoffel symbols> are $\Gamma^v{}_{vv}=M/r^2$, $\Gamma^r{}_{vv}=fM/r^2+M'/r$ and $\Gamma^r{}_{vr}=-M/r^2$. The angular couplings are $\Gamma^v{}_{\theta\theta}=-r$, $\Gamma^r{}_{\theta\theta}=-fr$, with $\sin^2\theta$ factors for the corresponding $\phi\phi$ terms, $\Gamma^\theta{}_{r\theta}=\Gamma^\phi{}_{r\phi}=1/r$, $\Gamma^\theta{}_{\phi\phi}=-\sin\theta\cos\theta$ and $\Gamma^\phi{}_{\theta\phi}=\cot\theta$. The lower indices are symmetric. The plus sign of $M'/r$ follows from the ingoing $+2dv\,dr$ cross term.

= Equivalence principle
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

The equivalence principle states that gravity couples universally to energy-momentum and that a freely falling local frame removes uniform gravitational acceleration at a point. Tidal curvature remains measurable over a finite region.

= Comma-to-semicolon prescription
{parent=Equivalence principle}
{title2=$\partial_a\ \longmapsto\ \nabla_a$}

= Comma goes to semicolon rule
{synonym}

The prescription replaces partial derivatives in flat-spacetime matter equations by the <Levi-Civita connection> and the <Minkowski metric> by the curved <metric tensor>. It implements minimal gravitational coupling and reproduces flat-spacetime physics at a point in a <local inertial frame>. It is not a unique rule for rearranged higher-derivative equations: commuting partial derivatives before replacement can conceal a <Riemann curvature tensor> term afterwards. The <derivative-order ambiguity in covariant Maxwell potential equations> is an explicit example. The <equivalence principle> removes uniform gravitational acceleration locally but does not erase tidal curvature over a finite experiment.

= Einstein equivalence principle
{c}
{parent=Equivalence principle}
{title2=EEP}

The <Einstein equivalence principle> combines the <weak equivalence principle> with local Lorentz invariance and local position invariance of nongravitational experiments. In a sufficiently small freely falling laboratory, their results agree with <special relativity> independently of its location and velocity. The <strong equivalence principle> extends this claim to local gravitational experiments.

= Weak equivalence principle
{parent=Equivalence principle}

Freely falling <test particles> with identical initial position and velocity follow the same trajectory independently of their material composition. The principle neglects appreciable self-gravity and nongravitational forces. The <Einstein equivalence principle> extends the local statement to nongravitational experiments; the <strong equivalence principle> also includes gravitational experiments and self-gravitating bodies.

= Eötvös experiment
{c}
{parent=Weak equivalence principle}
{wiki}

A torsion-balance comparison of the gravitational acceleration of bodies of different composition, testing equality of inertial and passive gravitational mass up to a universal proportionality factor. A universal acceleration law independent of test-body mass and composition agrees with this <weak equivalence principle> test. It does not by itself establish the <strong equivalence principle> for self-gravitating bodies.

= Strong equivalence principle
{parent=Equivalence principle}
{title2=SEP}

= Strong principle of equivalence
{synonym}

The strong <equivalence principle> extends universality of free fall to bodies with appreciable gravitational self-energy and requires local experiments, including gravitational ones, in a freely falling laboratory to be independent of the laboratory's location and velocity. This statement neglects external tidal variation across the laboratory; it does not remove <Riemann curvature tensor>. In <general relativity>, universal metric coupling, fixed local gravitational coupling and the absence of extra preferred-frame gravitational fields implement this principle.

= Nordtvedt effect
{parent=Strong equivalence principle}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$m_G/m_I=1+\eta_N E_G/(mc^2)$}

A violation of the <strong equivalence principle> can make the gravitational-to-inertial mass ratio depend on a body's negative gravitational self-energy $E_G$. Bodies with different self-binding fractions would then fall differently in the same external field, even after composition-dependent weak-equivalence effects are removed. For the Earth and Moon this produces a polarization of the lunar orbit toward the Sun, measurable by lunar laser ranging. <General relativity> predicts $\eta_N=0$.

= Absence of a local gravitational stress-energy tensor
{parent=Equivalence principle}

The <equivalence principle> allows <Riemann normal coordinates> in which the first derivatives of the <metric tensor> vanish at a chosen point. A purported local gravitational <stress-energy tensor> built from those first derivatives would vanish there in that chart and therefore, if tensorial, in all charts. Such an expression cannot encode the gravitational energy assigned by a coordinate-dependent field description. This rules out an ordinary universal local covariant gravitational energy density, not the construction of local curvature tensors or boundary and averaged notions of gravitational energy. The <Riemann curvature tensor> still describes invariant tidal gravity.

= Spacetime dimension
{parent=General relativity}

The spacetime dimension is the dimension of the <smooth manifold> used to model spacetime. Ordinary general relativity uses one temporal and three spatial dimensions.

= Killing vector field
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

= Killing vector
{c}
{synonym}

= Killing field
{c}
{synonym}

A Killing vector field $K$ generates a local one-parameter family of isometries. Equivalently,
$$
\mathcal L_Kg=0
\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
\nabla_{(\mu}K_{\nu)}=0.
$$

= Two-dimensional Killing algebra with a periodic generator is abelian
{parent=Killing vector field}
{title2=$[T,\Phi]=0$}

Suppose the full <Lie algebra> of <Killing fields> has basis $T,\Phi$ and the flow of $\Phi$ has a nonzero period $P$. Write $[T,\Phi]=aT+b\Phi$ with constant coefficients. The <adjoint representation> of the periodic flow satisfies $e^{P\operatorname{ad}\Phi}=1$. Its real eigenvalues are $-a$ and zero, so $a=0$. The remaining adjoint operator is nilpotent and its exponential is $1+P\operatorname{ad}\Phi$, forcing $b=0$. This proves commutation from genuine periodic axial symmetry, without differentiating mere asymptotic component limits.

= Bochner identity for Killing vector fields
{c}
{parent=Killing vector field}
{title2=$\int_M|\nabla X|^2\,dV=\int_M\operatorname{Ric}(X,X)\,dV$}

= Integrated Bochner identity for Killing fields
{synonym}

On a closed <Riemannian manifold>, a <Killing vector field> $X$ satisfies $\nabla^*\nabla X=\operatorname{Ric}^{\sharp}X$ for the nonnegative <rough Laplacian>. This follows by differentiating $\nabla_iX_j+\nabla_jX_i=0$, contracting and commuting <covariant derivatives>; the contraction also gives $\operatorname{div}X=0$. Taking the <inner product> with $X$ and using <integration by parts> gives the displayed identity. If <Ricci curvature> is nonpositive, the nonnegative left side must vanish, so $X$ is a <parallel vector field>. In particular every <Killing vector field> on a closed <flat Riemannian manifold> is a <parallel vector field>.

= Killing fields on incomplete manifolds need not generate global isometries
{parent=Killing vector field}

On a geodesically complete <Riemannian manifold>, every <Killing vector field> is complete and integrates to a global one-parameter subgroup of its <isometry group>. On the incomplete interval $(0,1)$ with its Euclidean metric, $\partial_x$ satisfies the <Killing equation>, but translations leave the interval in finite time. The full isometry group is only the identity and reflection. Local solutions of the Killing equation therefore need not equal the Lie algebra of global isometries without completeness.

= Killing transport
{c}
{parent=Killing vector field}
{title2=$\nabla_V\xi_a=L_{ab}V^b,\quad\nabla_VL_{ab}=-R_{abc}{}^dV^c\xi_d$}

= Killing transport equations
{c}
{synonym}

The <Killing equation> and the <second covariant derivative of a Killing vector> transport a Killing field and its antisymmetric derivative along any curve. Here $L_{ab}=\nabla_b\xi_a$ and the curvature convention is the one in <curvature-sign convention in Killing derivative identities>. In a parallel frame the equations form a homogeneous linear system of ordinary differential equations. Initial value zero has unique zero continuation. Thus on a connected <pseudo-Riemannian manifold>, not just on a Riemannian manifold, the field is determined by its value and first derivative at one point.

= Dimension bound for Killing vector fields
{parent=Killing transport}
{title2=$\dim\mathfrak{kill}(M,g)\leq n(n+1)/2$}

On a connected $n$-dimensional <pseudo-Riemannian manifold>, evaluation of a <Killing vector field> and its first derivative is injective by <Killing transport>. The value has $n$ components and the <Killing equation> makes the derivative an antisymmetric two-tensor with $n(n-1)/2$ components. Their sum gives the bound. Flat metrics attain it through $\xi_a=A_a+B_{ab}x^b$ with $B_{ab}=-B_{ba}$. Connectedness is essential for a uniform global bound in terms of $n$: independent fields can be supported on different connected components.

= Killing field is determined by its value and first covariant derivative
{c}
{parent=Killing vector field}
{title2=$K(p),\quad(\nabla K)_p$}

On a <connected> <Riemannian manifold>, these data determine a <Killing field> uniquely, without a completeness assumption. The <second covariant derivative of a Killing vector> makes its restriction to every <geodesic> a <Jacobi field>. The difference of two fields with identical data has zero Jacobi initial values and vanishes in a <convex normal neighborhood>. The set on which both its value and <covariant derivative> vanish is closed by continuity and open by the same local argument, so connectedness makes it the whole manifold.

= Killing-flow four-acceleration
{c}
{parent=Killing vector field}
{title2=$a_a=-\nabla_a\log\sqrt{K^bK_b}$}

With <metric signature> $(+---)$, normalize a timelike <Killing vector field> by $U^a=K^a/N$, $N^2=K^aK_a$. The <Killing equation> gives $K^b\nabla_bK_a=-\tfrac12\nabla_aN^2$, while $K^b\nabla_bN=0$. Thus the <four-acceleration> of its normalized flow is the displayed gradient. With <metric signature> $(-+++)$ the corresponding lower-index formula has the opposite sign.

= Papapetrou electromagnetic field
{c}
{parent=Killing vector field}
{title2=$F=dK^\flat$}

= Killing-generated Maxwell field
{c}
{synonym}

For a <Killing vector field> $K$, its metric-dual <differential one-form> produces the <electromagnetic field> $F_{ab}=2\nabla_{[a}K_{b]}=2\nabla_aK_b$. The <exterior derivative> gives $dF=0$, while the <second covariant derivative of a Killing vector> gives $\nabla^aF_{ab}=-2R_{bd}K^d$. Thus this construction solves the source-free <Maxwell equations> in a <vacuum spacetime> with zero <cosmological constant>. The field is a test field: adding its <stress-energy tensor> would generally change the original <metric tensor>.

= Kaluza-Klein decomposition along a Killing field
{parent=Killing vector field}
{c}
{title2=$h=V(dx+A)^2+\gamma$}

Where a <Killing vector field> is $\partial_x$ and its squared norm $V=h_{xx}$ is nonzero, an invariant <metric tensor> decomposes as $h=V(dx+A)^2+\gamma$. The quotient one-form has $A_i=h_{xi}/V$ and the quotient metric has $\gamma_{ij}=h_{ij}-h_{xi}h_{xj}/V$. All coefficients are independent of $x$. Changing the fibre coordinate to $x'=x+\lambda$ sends $A$ to $A-d\lambda$. A null Killing direction requires a different decomposition.

= Kaluza-Klein geodesic Lorentz force
{c}
{parent=Kaluza-Klein decomposition along a Killing field}
{title2=$\frac{D\dot x^i}{ds}=kF^i{}_j\dot x^j$}

For $ds_5^2=\gamma_{ij}dx^idx^j+(dz+A_idx^i)^2$ with $z$-independent coefficients, the <geodesic Lagrangian> makes $k=\dot z+A_i\dot x^i$ constant. Its remaining <Euler-Lagrange equations> give the displayed <Lorentz force>, where $F=dA$ and $D$ uses the <Levi-Civita connection> of $\gamma$. For a particle of five-dimensional mass $M$ with proper-time parameter $s$, the conserved momentum is $p_z=Mk$ and the four-dimensional rest mass is $m=M\sqrt{1+k^2}$. Reparametrizing by four-dimensional proper time gives $Du^i/d\tau=(p_z/m)F^i{}_ju^j$.

= Heisenberg metric Kaluza-Klein reduction
{parent=Kaluza-Klein decomposition along a Killing field}
{c}
{title2=$V=\alpha+\chi y^2$}

For $h=\alpha dx^2+\beta dy^2+\chi(dz-y\,dx)^2$, the <Kaluza-Klein decomposition along a Killing field> $\partial_x$ has $V=\alpha+\chi y^2$, $A=-\chi y\,dz/V$ and $\gamma=\beta dy^2+\alpha\chi\,dz^2/V$. For a <Riemannian metric>, positive constants make the fibre norm and quotient metric positive everywhere.

= Euclidean Killing vector field
{c}
{parent=Killing vector field}
{title2=$K(x)=A+Bx,\quad B^T=-B$}

Every <Killing vector field> on <Euclidean space> is the sum of a constant translation and a constant infinitesimal rotation. The <second covariant derivative of a Killing vector> vanishes in flat space, so its components are affine; the <Killing equation> makes their linear coefficient an <antisymmetric matrix>.

= Helical orbit of a Euclidean Killing vector
{parent=Euclidean Killing vector field}

An infinitesimal rotation in one coordinate plane plus a constant translation has <integral curves of a vector field> that rotate about a shifted centre in that plane and move uniformly in the remaining directions. Nonzero transverse drift gives helices; zero drift gives circles, with zero radius allowing straight or stationary curves.

= Killing equation
{c}
{parent=Killing vector field}

Killing's equation is $\nabla_{(a}K_{b)}=0$. It says that the infinitesimal flow of $K$ preserves the metric.

= Killing derivative contraction identity
{c}
{parent=Killing equation}
{title2=$K_aF^{bc}F_{bc}=3F^{bc}K_{[a}F_{bc]}-2F_{sa}a^s$}

For a <Killing vector field>, set $F_{ab}=\nabla_aK_b$ and $a^s=K^t\nabla_tK^s$. The <Killing equation> makes $F$ antisymmetric, and expansion of normalized three-index antisymmetrization gives the displayed identity. On a <Killing horizon>, <hypersurface orthogonality> removes its first right-hand term and relates $F^{ab}F_{ab}$ to <surface gravity>.

= Second covariant derivative of a Killing vector
{parent=Killing equation}

For a <Killing vector field> and the curvature convention $[\nabla_\mu,\nabla_\nu]V^\alpha=R^\alpha{}_{\beta\mu\nu}V^\beta$,
$$
\nabla_\nu\nabla_\mu K^\alpha=R^\alpha{}_{\mu\nu\beta}K^\beta,
\qquad
\nabla_\mu\nabla^\mu K^\alpha=-R^\alpha{}_{\beta}K^\beta.
$$
The identity follows by combining the <Ricci identity>, <Killing equation>, and <first Bianchi identity>.

= Curvature-sign convention in Killing derivative identities
{parent=Second covariant derivative of a Killing vector}
{title2=$k_{a;bc}=-C_{abc}{}^d k_d$}

Let $C^a{}_{bcd}=\partial_d\Gamma^a_{bc}-\partial_c\Gamma^a_{bd}-\Gamma^a_{ec}\Gamma^e_{bd}+\Gamma^a_{ed}\Gamma^e_{bc}$, with all other index positions obtained by the <metric tensor>. This convention is the negative of the convention $[\nabla_c,\nabla_d]V^a=R^a{}_{bcd}V^b$. The <Ricci identity> is $k_{a;bc}-k_{a;cb}=C_a{}^d{}_{bc}k_d$, with $k_{a;bc}=\nabla_c\nabla_b k_a$. For a <Killing vector>, the <Killing equation> makes this second derivative antisymmetric in $a,b$. Applying the commutator three times gives $2k_{a;bc}=(C_a{}^d{}_{bc}+C_c{}^d{}_{ba}-C_b{}^d{}_{ac})k_d=-2C_{abc}{}^d k_d$, where the last step uses the <first Bianchi identity>. Switching the sign of the <Riemann curvature tensor> switches the sign in this identity; moving a raised index by ordinary metric contraction does not switch it.

= Killing vector preserves scalar curvature
{parent=Second covariant derivative of a Killing vector}

Every <Killing vector field> $K$ preserves the <scalar curvature>: $\mathcal L_KR=K^\alpha\nabla_\alpha R=0$. One index proof rewrites this derivative as a doubly contracted curvature commutator acting on the antisymmetric tensor $\nabla_{[\mu}K_{\lambda]}$; both terms vanish by symmetry of the <Ricci tensor>.

= Geodesic conserved quantity from a Killing vector
{parent=Killing vector field}

= Geodesic conserved quantities from Killing vectors
{synonym}

If $U$ is the tangent to an affinely parametrized geodesic and $K$ is a Killing vector, then $g(K,U)$ is constant along the geodesic.

= Killing energy
{parent=Geodesic conserved quantity from a Killing vector}
{c}
{title2=$E=-K_a u^a$}

For a geodesic tangent $u$ and a stationary <Killing vector field> $K$, the <Killing energy> is $E=-g(K,u)$. It is conserved along the <geodesic>. When $K$ is future timelike and $u$ is future causal, $E>0$ for a nonzero tangent; it need not be positive where $K$ becomes spacelike.

= Charged-particle conserved quantity from a Killing symmetry
{parent=Geodesic conserved quantity from a Killing vector}
{title2=$mV_au^a-q\Phi$}

Suppose $V$ is a <Killing vector field>, $F$ is an <electromagnetic field tensor> satisfying $dF=0$ and $\mathcal L_VF=0$, and a particle of nonzero mass $m$ obeys the <Lorentz force> equation $\nabla_uu=(q/m)F^a{}_bu^b$. <Cartan's magic formula> gives $d(\iota_VF)=0$, so the <local potential of a closed differential one-form> proof gives $d\Phi=\iota_VF$ locally. The <Killing equation> eliminates $u^au^b\nabla_aV_b$, and the force term gives $d(V\cdot u)/d\tau=(q/m)d\Phi/d\tau$. Therefore $mV\cdot u-q\Phi$ is locally conserved. The sign assumes contraction in the first argument of $F$. A global potential additionally requires the closed one-form to have zero periods; invariance alone is insufficient without the homogeneous <Maxwell equations>.

= Stress-energy current from a Killing vector
{title2=$J^a=T^a{}_bK^b$}
{parent=Killing vector field}

If $T^{ab}$ is symmetric and covariantly conserved and $K$ is a <Killing vector field>, then $J^a=T^a{}_bK^b$ is conserved. Its flux through a spacelike hypersurface is the energy or momentum charge generated by the isometry.

= Komar charge
{c}
{parent=Killing vector field}

= Komar integral
{c}
{synonym}

A Komar integral constructs a conserved gravitational charge from the exterior derivative of the one-form dual to a Killing vector. In four dimensions, a stationary mass is conventionally normalized as
$$
M_K=-\frac1{8\pi}\int_S\star dk.
$$

= Komar current from trace-reversed stress-energy
{c}
{parent=Komar charge}
{title2=$J^\mu=R^\mu{}_\nu\xi^\nu$}

A <Killing vector field> gives an antisymmetric tensor $\nabla^\mu\xi^\nu$ whose divergence is this current, up to overall surface-orientation convention. The four-dimensional <Einstein field equations> express it as $8\pi G(T^\mu{}_\nu-\tfrac12T\delta^\mu{}_\nu)\xi^\nu$ at zero cosmological constant. The <contracted Bianchi identity> and Killing invariance of <scalar curvature> prove its divergence is zero. Stress conservation alone does not cancel the trace derivative without Killing invariance of the trace.

= Komar angular momentum
{c}
{parent=Komar charge}
{title2=$J=(16\pi)^{-1}\int_S\star dm$}

<Komar angular momentum> is the <Komar charge> associated with an axial <Killing vector field> in four-dimensional <general relativity>. In geometrized units its normalization is $(16\pi)^{-1}\int_S\star dm$, where $m$ is the metric-dual <differential one-form>. With zero <cosmological constant>, vacuum <Einstein field equations> make $d\star dm=0$, so homologous surrounding surfaces in the vacuum region give the same charge.

= Angular momentum conservation in an axisymmetric vacuum region
{parent=Komar angular momentum}
{title2=$J_{S_2}=J_{S_1}$}

For an axial <Killing vector> $\xi$ and zero <cosmological constant>, set $F^{ab}=\nabla^b\xi^a$. The <Killing equation> and <Ricci identity> give $\nabla_bF^{ab}=-R^a{}_b\xi^b$. In a vacuum region this vanishes, so <Stokes theorem> makes the <Komar angular momentum> equal on homologous enclosing surfaces. The region may contain <gravitational waves> with nonzero Weyl curvature: Ricci flatness, rather than complete flatness, is what removes the flux. This proves absence of axial angular-momentum transport by exactly axisymmetric gravitational radiation, without assuming that a radiating spacetime is stationary.

= Komar angular momentum with inner boundaries
{c}
{parent=Komar angular momentum}

With zero <cosmological constant> and the component <Hodge star operator> convention $(\star j)_{abc}=\epsilon_{abcd}j^d$, the <Einstein field equations> and <Killing equation> give $d\star dm=-16\pi\star(T_{ab}m^b-\tfrac12Tm_a)$. If $m$ is tangent to a spacelike <hypersurface>, the trace term pulls back to zero. For $\partial\Sigma=S_\infty-\bigcup_iS_i$, <Stokes theorem> yields
$$
J_\infty-\sum_iJ_i=-\int_\Sigma\star(T_{ab}m^b\,dx^a).
$$
The inner <Komar charges> cannot be dropped for a vacuum <Kerr black hole>, whose <angular momentum> is nonzero although its <stress-energy tensor> vanishes. A matter-only volume formula requires a regular filling with no inner boundaries.

= Komar mass
{c}
{parent=Komar charge}
{wiki}

Komar mass is the <Komar charge> associated with a stationary timelike Killing vector, using the normalization appropriate to gravitational mass.

= Komar mass normalization in higher dimensions
{c}
{parent=Komar mass}
{title2=$M=-\frac{D-2}{16\pi G_D(D-3)}\int\nabla^ak^b\,d\Sigma_{ab}$}

For future static-slice normal $u$ and outward radial normal $n$, take $d\Sigma_{ab}=(n_au_b-n_bu_a)dA$, with both antisymmetric components summed. For the <Schwarzschild-Tangherlini metric> the flux is $-\Omega_{D-2}r^{D-2}f'=-(D-3)\Omega_{D-2}\mu$. The displayed dimension-dependent coefficient then reproduces the <ADM mass>. Reversing orientation reverses the flux; including a factor of one-half in the binormal convention also changes its numerical value. A four-dimensional normalization cannot be carried unchanged into other dimensions.

= Static spherical Komar mass in reciprocal radial gauge
{parent=Komar mass}
{title2=$M_K(r)=R(r)^2f'(r)/(2G)$}

For $ds^2=-f(r)dt^2+dr^2/f(r)+R(r)^2d\Omega^2$ and $K=\partial_t$ normalized at infinity, the <Komar mass> on a round sphere is $R^2f'/(2G)$. The future unit normal to a static slice is $f^{-1/2}K$, and the outward unit radial normal is $f^{1/2}\partial_r$; their contraction with $\nabla_aK_b$ gives $f'/2$, whose integral over the sphere yields the formula. The asymptotic mass is its limit at infinity. In the presence of matter, the finite-radius integral need not be constant.

= Background-subtracted Komar energy
{c}
{parent=Komar charge}

For a spacetime with a non-flat reference background, replacing $k$ by $k-\bar k$ removes the background contribution and can make the Komar surface integral finite.

= Conformal Killing vector field
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

A conformal Killing vector field generates a local flow that preserves the metric up to a position-dependent scale factor.

= Conformal Killing equation
{c}
{parent=Conformal Killing vector field}
{wiki}

In $d$ dimensions, the conformal Killing equation is
$$
\nabla_aX_b+\nabla_bX_a=\frac2d g_{ab}\nabla_cX^c.
$$

= Flat conformal Killing integrability
{parent=Conformal Killing equation}
{title2=$\partial_\beta\partial_\gamma\omega_\alpha=(\delta_{\alpha\beta}\partial_\gamma\rho+\delta_{\alpha\gamma}\partial_\beta\rho-\delta_{\beta\gamma}\partial_\alpha\rho)/d$}

For a flat <conformal Killing vector field>, let $\rho=\partial_\mu\omega^\mu$. Differentiate the <conformal Killing equation> in three index orders, add two equations and subtract the third to obtain the displayed identity. Tracing gives $\Delta\omega_\alpha=(2-d)\partial_\alpha\rho/d$, whose divergence implies $\Delta\rho=0$ for $d>1$. Taking the divergence of the original identity gives $(d-2)\partial_\beta\partial_\gamma\rho=-\delta_{\beta\gamma}\Delta\rho$. Thus $\rho$ is affine and $\omega$ is at most quadratic when $d>2$.

= Gravitational wave
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

= Gravitational radiation
{synonym}

A gravitational wave is a propagating disturbance of spacetime curvature produced by changing mass-energy multipole moments.

= Axisymmetric radiation carries no axial angular momentum
{parent=Gravitational wave}

An exact axial <Killing vector field> makes <Komar angular momentum> independent of the surrounding vacuum surface. Therefore <gravitational waves> in a configuration with exact <axisymmetry> carry no net <angular momentum> about that symmetry axis, although they can carry <energy>. This is specific to axial <angular momentum>; it is not an absence of radiation.

= Gravitational-wave frequency
{title2=$f_{\rm GW}$}
{parent=Gravitational wave}

The gravitational-wave frequency is the temporal oscillation frequency of a radiative metric perturbation. A rigid source rotating with angular frequency $\omega$ and invariant under a half-turn radiates dominantly at angular frequency $2\omega$.

= Mass quadrupole moment
{title2=$Q_{ij}$}
{parent=Gravitational wave}

The trace-free mass quadrupole moment of a mass distribution is
$$
Q_{ij}=\int \rho(\mathbf x)
\left(x_ix_j-\frac13\delta_{ij}|\mathbf x|^2\right)d^3x.
$$
It is the leading time-dependent mass multipole that can radiate in <general relativity>: mass conservation removes monopole radiation, while momentum conservation removes mass-dipole radiation.

= Stress-energy quadrupole identity
{parent=Mass quadrupole moment}
{title2=$\ddot I^{ij}=2\int T^{ij}\,d^3x$}

For a symmetric <stress-energy tensor> with <compact support> and $\partial_bT^{ab}=0$ in <Minkowski spacetime>, let $I^{ij}=\int T^{00}x^ix^j\,d^3x$. Two integrations by parts give $\dot I^{ij}=\int(T^{0i}x^j+T^{0j}x^i)\,d^3x$ and the displayed identity. Compact support removes the boundary fluxes. The support should stay bounded over the time interval used when differentiating the integrals.

= Second mass moment tensor
{parent=Mass quadrupole moment}
{title2=$I_{ij}=\int \rho(\mathbf x)x_ix_j\,d^3x$}

The second mass moment tensor records the quadratic spatial distribution of mass. Its trace-free part is the <mass quadrupole moment> $Q_{ij}=I_{ij}-\delta_{ij}I_{kk}/3$. The mechanical moment-of-inertia tensor instead equals $J_{ij}=\delta_{ij}I_{kk}-I_{ij}$. A rigid rotation $L$ transforms the second mass moment tensor as $I(t)=L(t)I(0)L(t)^T$.

= Scalar second mass moment
{parent=Second mass moment tensor}
{title2=$I=\int r^2\,dm$}

The scalar second mass moment is the <trace> of the <second mass moment tensor>. The <moment of inertia> about a specified axis instead integrates squared distance from that axis. For an isotropic distribution each axial <moment of inertia> is $2I/3$; the sum over three orthogonal axes is $2I$.

= Quadrupole formula
{parent=Mass quadrupole moment}
{wiki}

For a slowly moving isolated source, the leading gravitational-wave luminosity is
$$
P=\frac{G}{5c^5}\left\langle\dddot Q_{ij}\dddot Q_{ij}\right\rangle,
$$
where $Q_{ij}$ is the trace-free mass quadrupole moment.

= Far-zone quadrupole strain
{parent=Quadrupole formula}
{title2=$H^{\mathrm{TT}}_{ij}=\frac{2G}{r}\Lambda_{ij,kl}(\mathbf n)\ddot Q^{kl}(t-r)$}

In units $c=1$, a slowly moving isolated source has trace-free <mass quadrupole moment> $Q^{ij}=\int T^{00}(x^ix^j-\delta^{ij}|\mathbf x|^2/3)\,d^3x$. The retarded <linearized gravity> solution and the <stress-energy quadrupole identity> give the displayed physical spatial strain, with $\mathbf n=\mathbf x/r$ and the <transverse-traceless projector> $\Lambda$. If the spatial metric is written $g_{ij}=-\delta_{ij}-2E_{ij}$ for <metric signature> $(+---)$, its trace-free Lorenz-gauge coefficient before the transverse projection is $E_{ij}=G\ddot Q_{ij}(t-r)/r$. Thus $H^{\mathrm{TT}}=2E^{\mathrm{TT}}$. Far distance alone does not permit dropping source-dependent retardation: the quadrupole approximation also requires the source size to be small compared with the radiation wavelength.

= Quadrupole strain scaling of a weak self-gravitating source
{parent=Far-zone quadrupole strain}
{title2=$E=O(\epsilon^2R/r),\quad\epsilon=GM/R$}

For a weakly self-gravitating bound source of size $R$, mass $M$ and nonspherical dynamical <mass quadrupole moment> $Q=O(MR^2)$, the <virial theorem> gives $v^2=O(GM/R)=O(\epsilon)$. Its time scale is $R/v$, so $\ddot Q=O(Mv^2)$. The <far-zone quadrupole strain> then has a spatial half-strain coefficient $E=O(GMv^2/r)=O(\epsilon^2R/r)$ in units $c=1$. The physical transverse-traceless strain is $2E^{\mathrm{TT}}$. Spherical or stationary sources can have zero radiation, so this is a characteristic scale for a changing nonspherical quadrupole, not a positive lower bound. The radiation zone also requires $r\gg R/v$.

= Circular-binary quadrupole radiation
{parent=Far-zone quadrupole strain}
{title2=$\omega^2=Gm/(4R^3),\quad \omega_{\mathrm{GW}}=2\omega$}

Two equal masses $m$ orbiting their <centre of mass> at radius $R$ have separation $2R$. <Newton's law of universal gravitation> gives the displayed orbital frequency. Their <mass quadrupole moment> varies at twice that frequency, producing <gravitational radiation> with two <gravitational wave polarizations>. For inclination $\iota$, its leading plus and cross amplitudes are proportional to $1+\cos^2\iota$ and $2\cos\iota$, respectively. Face-on radiation is circularly polarized; edge-on radiation is linearly polarized.

= Orbital compactness
{parent=Quadrupole formula}
{title2=$GM/(Rc^2)$}

The dimensionless ratio of gravitational radius to orbital scale measures how relativistic a bound orbit is. In an equal-mass circular binary with individual orbital radius $R$, the leading <quadrupole formula> gives $P=(2/5)(c^5/G)[GM/(Rc^2)]^5$. Compact objects permit smaller noncontact orbits than extended stars and hence larger orbital compactness; near merger the Newtonian approximation must be replaced.

= Equal-mass circular-binary quadrupole luminosity
{parent=Quadrupole formula}
{title2=$P=\frac{2G^4M^5}{5c^5R^5}$}

For two masses $M$ at opposite points of a circular orbit, each at radius $R$ from the centre of mass, $\Omega^2=GM/(4R^3)$. The trace-free <mass quadrupole moment> oscillates at twice the orbital frequency, and its third-derivative squared contraction is $128M^2R^4\Omega^6$. The <quadrupole formula> gives the displayed phase-independent luminosity. Using separation $2R$ rather than individual radius $R$ avoids a factor-of-32 error.

= Head-on quadrupole radiation from equal masses
{parent=Quadrupole formula}
{title2=$P=m^5(1/z-1/z_0)/(15z^4)$}

For two equal <point masses> at $\pm z$ falling from rest at $\pm z_0$ under <Newtonian gravity>, in <geometrized units> one has $\ddot z=-m/(4z^2)$ and $\dot z=-\sqrt{m(1/z-1/z_0)/2}$. The only nonzero component of the <second mass moment tensor> is $I_{zz}=2mz^2$, and its third derivative is $-m^2\dot z/z^2$. The trace-free <mass quadrupole moment> and <quadrupole formula> give the displayed power. From infinity, integrating down to $z=z_c$ gives $E=2\sqrt2\,m^{9/2}/(105z_c^{7/2})$. Relative to the total initial mass $2m$ the fraction is $\sqrt2(m/z_c)^{7/2}/105$. The weak-field slow-motion approximation must be distinguished from its extrapolation to a compact endpoint.

= Quadrupole radiation from a harmonically oscillating point mass
{parent=Quadrupole formula}
{title2=$\langle P\rangle=\frac{16Gm^2L^4\omega^6}{15c^5}$}

For a <point mass> moving as $z=L\cos\omega t$ along one fixed axis, the trace-free <mass quadrupole moment> is $mL^2\cos^2\omega t\,\operatorname{diag}(-1/3,-1/3,2/3)$. Its radiating component has twice the source <angular frequency>. The <quadrupole formula> gives $\langle P\rangle=16Gm^2L^4\omega^6/(15c^5)$ for this contribution in the leading slow-motion <weak-field approximation>. Any physical driver or support has its own quadrupole contribution; this expression is for the prescribed mass alone.

= Gravitational-wave energy and angular-momentum balance
{parent=Quadrupole formula}
{title2=$\dot E=\Omega\dot J$}

For a slowly evolving circular <binary star> with separation $a$, <reduced mass> $\mu$ and orbital angular frequency $\Omega$, the leading <gravitational-wave> losses satisfy $\dot E=\Omega\dot J$. One direct check uses the standard quadrupole angular-momentum loss law
$$
\dot J_i=-\frac{2G}{5c^5}\epsilon_{ijk}\left\langle\ddot Q_{j\ell}\dddot Q_{k\ell}\right\rangle,
$$
where $Q$ is the <mass quadrupole moment> and $\epsilon_{ijk}$ is the <Levi-Civita symbol>. For $\mathbf r=a(\cos\Omega t,\sin\Omega t,0)$, put $A=\mu a^2$; then $Q_{xx}=A(1/6+\cos(2\Omega t)/2)$, $Q_{yy}=A(1/6-\cos(2\Omega t)/2)$, $Q_{xy}=A\sin(2\Omega t)/2$, and $Q_{zz}=-A/3$. Substitution gives $\dot J_z=-32GA^2\Omega^5/(5c^5)$ and the <quadrupole formula> gives $\dot E=-32GA^2\Omega^6/(5c^5)$. Their ratio proves the balance relation. The same relation follows from the rotating wave's angular harmonic number two and frequency $2\Omega$. The additional angular-momentum flux law, not the energy flux alone, is needed to justify preservation of secular circularity.

= Instantaneous quadrupole luminosity of a Kepler binary
{parent=Quadrupole formula}
{title2=$L=32G^3M^2\mu^2(v^2-11\dot r^2/12)/(5c^5r^4)$}

For a Newtonian <Kepler orbit>, let $M=M_1+M_2$ be the total <mass> and $\mu=M_1M_2/M$ the <reduced mass>; put $k=GM$, $\mathbf n=\mathbf r/r$, $u=\dot r$ and $\mathbf v_\perp=\dot{\mathbf r}-u\mathbf n$, with $v^2=\dot{\mathbf r}\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}$. In the convention $q_{ij}=\mu(3r_ir_j-r^2\delta_{ij})/2$, differentiating the acceleration $\ddot{\mathbf r}=-k\mathbf r/r^3$ gives
$$
\dddot q_{ij}=\frac{\mu k}{r^2}\left[u(\delta_{ij}-3n_in_j)-6(n_iv_{\perp j}+v_{\perp i}n_j)\right].
$$
The two terms have zero cross <tensor contraction>, with squared norms $6u^2$ and $72v_\perp^2$. Therefore $\dddot q_{ij}\dddot q_{ij}=72\mu^2k^2(v^2-11u^2/12)/r^4$. Substitution into the <quadrupole formula> in this convention, $L=4G\dddot q_{ij}\dddot q_{ij}/(45c^5)$, proves the luminosity. It is nonnegative since $v^2-11u^2/12=v_\perp^2+u^2/12$. The formula uses a weak gravitational field, slow motion and leading radiation order; orbital averaging gives a secular luminosity.

= Gravitational radiation from a rotating triaxial body
{parent=Quadrupole formula}
{title2=$P=\frac{32G}{5c^5}\Omega^6(I_1-I_2)^2$}

For a rigid body rotating around its third principal axis, the anisotropic part of its <second mass moment tensor> oscillates at angular frequency $2\Omega$. The <quadrupole formula> gives the displayed luminosity. Equal transverse principal moments remove this rotating quadrupole and its leading <gravitational wave> emission.

= Gravitational-wave energy flux
{parent=Gravitational wave}

In the radiation zone, the time-averaged energy flux of a weak wave in <transverse-traceless gauge> is
$$
\frac{dP}{d\Omega}
=\frac{c^3r^2}{32\pi G}
\left\langle\dot h^{\rm TT}_{ij}\dot h^{\rm TT}_{ij}\right\rangle.
$$
Combining this with the far-zone quadrupole field and integrating over directions gives the <quadrupole formula>.

= Newman-Penrose formalism
{c}
{parent=Gravitational wave}
{wiki=Newman–Penrose_formalism}

The Newman--Penrose formalism resolves spacetime curvature into scalars by contracting the Weyl tensor with a complex null tetrad.

= Complex null tetrad
{parent=Newman-Penrose formalism}

A complex null tetrad consists of two real null vectors and a complex-conjugate transverse pair, normalized so that the metric is reconstructed from their pairwise products.

= Newman--Penrose scalar Psi4
{title2=$\Psi_4$}
{c}
{parent=Newman-Penrose formalism}

For a null tetrad $(\ell,k,m,\bar m)$, one common curvature-sign convention defines $\Psi_4=C_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}k^\mu\bar m^\nu k^\rho\bar m^\sigma$. In an outgoing wave zone it encodes the two transverse <gravitational wave polarizations> as one complex scalar.

= Plane-fronted gravitational wave
{parent=Gravitational wave}

For
$$
ds^2=2\,du\,dv+dx^2+dy^2+H(u,x,y)\,du^2,
$$
the only potentially nonzero Ricci component is
$$
R_{uu}=-\frac12(H_{xx}+H_{yy}).
$$
The vacuum equation is therefore the transverse Laplace equation.

= Ricci tensor of a Brinkmann plane wave
{c}
{parent=Plane-fronted gravitational wave}
{title2=$R_{uu}=H_{xx}+H_{yy}$}

For $ds^2=-2du\,dv+dx^2+dy^2-2H(u,x,y)du^2$, the nonzero connections are $\Gamma^v_{uu}=H_u$, $\Gamma^v_{ux}=H_x$, $\Gamma^v_{uy}=H_y$, $\Gamma^x_{uu}=H_x$, and $\Gamma^y_{uu}=H_y$, with symmetric lower indices. Their traces and quadratic Ricci products vanish, leaving only the displayed Ricci component. Hence the vacuum equations require a harmonic transverse profile. The sign is tied to the metric's $-2Hdu^2$ convention.

= Homogeneous wavefront of a plane gravitational wave
{parent=Plane-fronted gravitational wave}

For a Brinkmann plane wave whose transverse profile is quadratic, Killing fields obtained from solutions of transverse oscillator equations act transitively on every constant-retarded-time wavefront. Each wavefront is therefore a homogeneous null hypersurface.

= Rosen coordinates for a plane gravitational wave
{c}
{parent=Plane-fronted gravitational wave}

Rosen coordinates put a plane wave into the transverse-metric form
$$
ds^2=-du\,dv+g_{AB}(u)dx^A dx^B.
$$
For diagonal $g_{AB}=\operatorname{diag}(f^2,g^2)$, the only potentially nonzero <Ricci tensor> component is $R_{uu}=-f''/f-g''/g$.

= Curvature of a diagonal plane wave in Rosen coordinates
{parent=Rosen coordinates for a plane gravitational wave}
{title2=$\operatorname{Ric}=-(A''/A+B''/B)du\otimes du$}

For $g=-dt^2+A(u)^2dx^2+B(u)^2dy^2+dz^2$, $u=t-z$, with nowhere-zero smooth $A,B$, take $e^0=dt$, $e^1=A\,dx$, $e^2=B\,dy$, $e^3=dz$, $\ell=du$. In signature $(-,+,+,+)$, <Cartan's first structure equation> and <metric compatibility> give the lower-index <connection 1-forms> $\omega_{01}=\omega_{13}=-(A'/A)e^1$ and $\omega_{02}=\omega_{23}=-(B'/B)e^2$. <Cartan's second structure equation> gives $\Theta_{01}=\Theta_{13}=-(A''/A)\ell\wedge e^1$ and $\Theta_{02}=\Theta_{23}=-(B''/B)\ell\wedge e^2$. All other independent <curvature 2-forms> vanish: in particular the $12$ component cancels between the time and longitudinal terms. Contracting $R^a{}_{bad}$ yields $\operatorname{Ric}=-(A''/A+B''/B)du\otimes du$. Thus the <vacuum Einstein equations> are $A''/A+B''/B=0$, while nonzero $A''/A=-B''/B$ gives opposite transverse tidal curvatures and <plus polarization> relative to these axes.

= Gravitational-wave pulse
{parent=Plane-fronted gravitational wave}

A gravitational-wave pulse has curvature supported in a bounded interval of <retarded time>. Spacetime can be flat both before and after the pulse even though freely falling bodies retain a displacement or relative velocity produced while the pulse passed.

= Gravitational-wave memory effect
{parent=Gravitational-wave pulse}
{wiki}

The gravitational-wave memory effect is a lasting change in the relative configuration of freely falling test bodies after a transient <gravitational wave> has passed.

= Displacement memory
{parent=Gravitational-wave memory effect}

Displacement memory is a persistent change in the separation of freely falling bodies after a <gravitational-wave pulse>.

= Velocity memory
{parent=Gravitational-wave memory effect}

Velocity memory is a persistent change in the relative velocity of freely falling bodies after a <gravitational-wave pulse>.

= Covariant derivative
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

A connection defines covariant differentiation by adding one connection term for each contravariant index and subtracting one for each covariant index.

= Covariant divergence formula
{parent=Covariant derivative}
{title2=$\nabla_\mu A^\mu=|g|^{-1/2}\partial_\mu(\sqrt{|g|}A^\mu)$}

The <Christoffel trace identity> converts the <covariant derivative> of a <vector field> into a coordinate divergence weighted by the metric volume density. This is valid for the <Levi-Civita connection>. Applied to the raised gradient of a <scalar field>, it gives $\Box\phi=|g|^{-1/2}\partial_\mu(\sqrt{|g|}g^{\mu\nu}\partial_\nu\phi)$ in Lorentzian signature, the curved <d'Alembert operator>.

= Covariant divergence
{parent=Covariant derivative}

The covariant divergence contracts a covariant-derivative index with a contravariant tensor index. For a mixed rank-two tensor it is $\nabla_\mu T^\mu{}_{\nu}$.

= Tensor type
{title2=$(r,s)$}
{parent=Covariant derivative}

A tensor of type $(r,s)$ has $r$ contravariant and $s$ covariant indices. Taking a covariant derivative adds one covariant index, while an outer tensor product adds the corresponding contravariant and covariant index counts.

= Tensor component transformation law
{parent=Tensor type}

Under a coordinate change $x\mapsto x'$, the components of a type-$(r,s)$ tensor acquire one forward Jacobian for each contravariant index and one inverse Jacobian for each covariant index. For a type-$(1,1)$ tensor,
$$
T^{\mu'}{}_{\nu'}
=\frac{\partial x^{\mu'}}{\partial x^\alpha}
\frac{\partial x^\beta}{\partial x^{\nu'}}
T^\alpha{}_{\beta}.
$$

= Tensor density
{parent=Tensor component transformation law}

A tensor density of weight $w$ transforms like a <tensor> with an additional factor $|\det(\partial x/\partial x')|^w$ under $x\mapsto x'$. A scalar density of weight one can be integrated against the coordinate volume element to give an invariant integral. On a spatial hypersurface, $\sqrt h$ is such a scalar density; hence $\pi^{ij}=\sqrt h p^{ij}$ is a tensor density when $p^{ij}$ is an ordinary tensor. The <Levi-Civita connection> extends to densities and satisfies $D_i\sqrt h=0$.

= Identity tensor
{title2=$\operatorname{id}_{TM}=\delta^\mu{}_{\nu}$}
{parent=Tensor type}

The identity tensor is the canonical type-$(1,1)$ tensor that maps each tangent vector to itself. It pairs a covector $\eta$ and vector $V$ as $\eta(V)$, and its components are the <Kronecker delta> $\delta^\mu{}_{\nu}$ in every coordinate system.

= Covariant derivative of a covariant tensor
{parent=Covariant derivative}

For $W_{\mu\nu}$,
$$
\nabla_\alpha W_{\mu\nu}
=\partial_\alpha W_{\mu\nu}
-\Gamma^\rho_{\alpha\mu}W_{\rho\nu}
-\Gamma^\rho_{\alpha\nu}W_{\mu\rho}.
$$

= Levi-Civita connection
{parent=Covariant derivative}
{c}
{wiki}

The Levi-Civita connection is the unique torsion-free connection compatible with the metric; its coefficients are the Christoffel symbols formed from first metric derivatives.

= Parallel metrics with a common Levi-Civita connection
{parent=Levi-Civita connection}
{title2=$\widetilde g(u,v)=g(Au,v),\quad\nabla A=0$}

Two <Riemannian metrics> have the same <Levi-Civita connection> precisely when their relative positive <self-adjoint> <endomorphism> $A$ is parallel for that connection. <Parallel transport> preserves both metrics. Consequently agreement at one point implies agreement everywhere on a <connected> manifold. Without that agreement, constant scalar multiples always give examples; constant nonproportional Euclidean forms show that a common connection does not force scalar proportionality.

= Fundamental theorem of Riemannian geometry
{parent=Levi-Civita connection}

A <Riemannian metric> determines a unique <torsion-free connection> that is a <metric connection>. Metric compatibility and the torsion identity imply the <Koszul formula>, which proves uniqueness. Conversely the right-hand side of the <Koszul formula> is a smooth one-form in its last vector-field argument. The <musical isomorphism> defines $D_XY$ from it; expanding the <Lie bracket of vector fields> verifies the connection rules, metric compatibility and zero torsion. Thus it constructs the <Levi-Civita connection> intrinsically.

= Metric compatibility
{parent=Levi-Civita connection}
{wiki}

Metric compatibility is $\nabla_\alpha g_{\mu\nu}=0$, allowing the metric to pass through covariant derivatives and index operations.

= Lie derivative of a metric with nonmetricity
{c}
{parent=Metric compatibility}
{title2=$\mathcal L_\xi g_{ab}=\xi^c\nabla_cg_{ab}+g_{cb}\nabla_a\xi^c+g_{ac}\nabla_b\xi^c$}

For any <torsion-free connection>, expand the coordinate <Lie derivative of a tensor field> and cancel the symmetric connection terms to obtain the displayed identity. If the connection is <metric-compatible>, lowering the vector index commutes with the <covariant derivative> and it becomes $\mathcal L_\xi g_{ab}=2\nabla_{(a}\xi_{b)}$. Symmetry of the connection alone does not suffice: with $g=e^{2x}dx^2$, zero connection coefficients and $\xi=\partial_x$, the two expressions $\mathcal L_\xi g$ and $2\nabla_x\xi_x$ are $2e^{2x}$ and $4e^{2x}$, respectively.

= Lie derivative of an affine connection
{parent=Levi-Civita connection}

The Lie derivative of a connection is the tensor
$$
(\mathcal L_X\nabla)_YZ
=\mathcal L_X(\nabla_YZ)-\nabla_{\mathcal L_XY}Z-\nabla_Y(\mathcal L_XZ).
$$
An isometry preserves the Levi-Civita connection, so $\mathcal L_K\nabla=0$ for a <Killing vector field>.

= Riemann curvature tensor
{parent=General relativity}
{c}
{wiki}

= Riemannian curvature tensor
{c}
{synonym}

= Riemann tensor
{c}
{synonym}

The Riemann tensor measures the failure of covariant derivatives to commute.

= Pair symmetry from the normal-coordinate curvature formula
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}
{title2=$R_{ij,kl}=R_{kl,ij}$}

With convention $R(X,Y)=\nabla_{[X,Y]}-[\nabla_X,\nabla_Y]$, and $R_{ij,kl}=g(R(\partial_i,\partial_j)\partial_k,\partial_l)$, normal coordinates give
$$
R_{ij,kl}=\tfrac12(g_{jk,il}+g_{il,jk}-g_{jl,ik}-g_{ik,jl})
$$
at their center. Symmetry of the metric and commutation of second derivatives prove pair exchange. The opposite curvature convention changes the formula's overall sign but preserves all these algebraic symmetries.

= Bianchi identities from covariant derivative commutators
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}

The <first Bianchi identity> follows by applying cyclic curvature commutators to the gradient of an arbitrary <scalar field>: its symmetric Hessian makes the third-derivative terms cancel, leaving $R_{abc}{}^d+R_{bca}{}^d+R_{cab}{}^d=0$. For the <second Bianchi identity>, write the vector-bundle derivative as $D_a=\partial_a+\Gamma_a$, so $[D_b,D_c]=\mathcal R_{bc}$. The operator <Jacobi identity> gives the cyclic sum of $\partial_a\mathcal R_{bc}+[\Gamma_a,\mathcal R_{bc}]$ equal to zero. Restoring the connection terms on the two curvature-form indices gives precisely $\nabla_aR_{bcd}{}^e+\nabla_bR_{cad}{}^e+\nabla_cR_{abd}{}^e=0$: the additional terms cancel because the connection is torsion-free and curvature is antisymmetric in its first pair.

= Curvature commutator is a tensor derivation
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}
{title2=$\Delta_{ab}(Q\otimes S)=(\Delta_{ab}Q)\otimes S+Q\otimes\Delta_{ab}S$}

For a torsion-free <linear connection on a manifold>, the commutator of two <covariant derivatives> obeys the tensor-product <Leibniz rule>: mixed first-derivative terms cancel after antisymmetrizing. It vanishes on <scalar fields>, so $\Delta_{ab}(fQ)=f\Delta_{ab}Q$; its action is therefore pointwise tensorial. Its vector action defines the <Riemann curvature tensor>. Applying the commutator to a scalar contraction proves that every covariant tensor index contributes minus the corresponding curvature action, while every contravariant index contributes plus. Torsion-free does not by itself imply <metric compatibility>.

= Three-dimensional curvature from the Ricci tensor
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}
{title2=$\operatorname{Ric}=0\ \Longrightarrow\ \operatorname{Riem}=0\quad(n=3)$}

In dimension three the <Riemann curvature tensor> is $R_{ijpq}=g_{ip}S_{jq}-g_{iq}S_{jp}-g_{jp}S_{iq}+g_{jq}S_{ip}$, where $S$ is the <Schouten tensor>. Consequently every three-dimensional <Ricci-flat Riemannian manifold> is a <flat Riemannian manifold>. The implication fails in higher dimensions.

= Kretschmann scalar
{c}
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}
{title2=$K=R_{abcd}R^{abcd}$}

The Kretschmann scalar is the scalar <tensor contraction> of two copies of the <Riemann curvature tensor>. In <Schwarzschild spacetime>, with metric coefficient $1-2M/r$ and $G=c=1$, it is $48M^2/r^6$. Divergence of this scalar along a curve approaching a proposed regular point proves that there is no twice continuously differentiable, nondegenerate metric extension through that point: such an extension would have continuous finite curvature components and hence a finite scalar contraction. This gives a coordinate-independent test of a <curvature singularity>. Boundedness of this single scalar does not prove regularity.

= Curvature commutator on a covariant tensor
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}

= Ricci identity
{c}
{synonym}

For a rank-two covariant tensor, the derivative commutator contributes one curvature action on each index:
$$
[\nabla_\alpha,\nabla_\beta]W_{\mu\nu}
=-R^\rho{}_{\mu\alpha\beta}W_{\rho\nu}
-R^\rho{}_{\nu\alpha\beta}W_{\mu\rho}.
$$

= Curvature commutator on a contravariant tensor
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}

For a type-$(2,0)$ tensor, the <Ricci identity> acts once on each contravariant index:
$$
[\nabla_\alpha,\nabla_\beta]T^{\mu\nu}
=R^\mu{}_{\rho\alpha\beta}T^{\rho\nu}
+R^\nu{}_{\rho\alpha\beta}T^{\mu\rho}.
$$

= Geodesic deviation
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}
{wiki}

For a family of affinely parametrized geodesics with tangent $U$ and connecting field $J$, the Jacobi equation in the convention used here is
$$
\nabla_U\nabla_UJ=R(U,J)U.
$$
It describes the relative acceleration produced by spacetime curvature.

= Local tidal acceleration scaling
{parent=Geodesic deviation}
{title2=$a_{\mathrm{tidal}}=O(\mathcal R L)$}

The <geodesic deviation> equation makes the relative gravitational acceleration proportional to the separation $L$ and a typical <Riemann curvature tensor> component $\mathcal R$. Thus tidal acceleration vanishes as $L\to0$ at fixed bounded curvature. A uniform <electric field> can give different accelerations $qE/m$ at the same point, independently of separation. Relative tidal changes accumulated over a fixed long time need not vanish as a fraction of the initial separation; a local spacetime experiment must control its duration as well as its spatial size.

= Conserved deviation energy in constant curvature
{parent=Geodesic deviation}
{title2=$K=|DS|^2-\kappa|S|^2$}

In <constant sectional curvature> $\kappa$, a <deviation vector> orthogonal to a unit <timelike geodesic> obeys $D^2S=\kappa S$, where $D=\nabla_T$. Therefore $K=g(DS,DS)-\kappa g(S,S)$ is constant, since its derivative is $2g(DS,D^2S)-2\kappa g(S,DS)=0$. Its squared length $f=g(S,S)$ satisfies $\ddot f=2K+4\kappa f$. In four spacetime dimensions, $\kappa=R/12$.

= Exponential and oscillatory geodesic deviation
{parent=Conserved deviation energy in constant curvature}
{title2=$D^2S=\kappa S$}

In a <parallel-propagated orthonormal frame>, orthogonal <geodesic deviation> in <constant sectional curvature> reduces to three scalar equations $\ddot S^i=\kappa S^i$. For $\kappa>0$, $S=Ae^{\sqrt\kappa\tau}+Be^{-\sqrt\kappa\tau}$: generic initial data grow exponentially, but the pure decaying mode is a counterexample to universal growth. Initially comoving neighbors have $S=S(0)\cosh(\sqrt\kappa\tau)$. For $\kappa<0$, $S=A\cos(\sqrt{-\kappa}\tau)+B\sin(\sqrt{-\kappa}\tau)$, so the deviation is bounded and oscillatory. Simultaneous focusing requires compatible vector initial data and is not automatic for every oscillatory solution.

= Orthogonality of proper-time deviation vectors
{parent=Geodesic deviation}
{title2=$\frac{d}{d\tau}g(S,T)=0$}

For a <geodesic variation> of unit <timelike geodesics>, <proper time> normalization holds across the entire variation: $g(T,T)=-1$. <Metric compatibility> and $\nabla_TS=\nabla_ST$ then give $d g(S,T)/d\tau=\tfrac12S(g(T,T))=0$. Consequently an initially orthogonal <deviation vector> stays orthogonal. The common normalization is essential: an arbitrary family of affinely parametrized curves with different tangent norms does not imply this conclusion.

= Jacobi field
{parent=Geodesic deviation}
{wiki}

A Jacobi field is the infinitesimal connecting vector between neighboring members of a one-parameter family of geodesics. It satisfies the geodesic-deviation equation along the reference geodesic.

= Jacobi fields vanishing at their initial point
{c}
{parent=Jacobi field}
{title2=$J(t)=(d\exp_p)_{tV}(tD_tJ(0))$}

For $\gamma(t)=\exp_p(tV)$, every <Jacobi field> with $J(0)=0$ is uniquely of the form
$$
J(t)=(d\exp_p)_{tV}(tB),\qquad B=D_tJ(0)\in T_pM.
$$
Differentiate the <geodesic variation> $\exp_p(t(V+sB))$ in $s$ to obtain the formula. Uniqueness of the Jacobi equation with prescribed initial value and derivative shows that all such fields are obtained. A nontrivial field vanishing at $t=L>0$ therefore corresponds to a nonzero vector in the kernel of $(d\exp_p)_{LV}$, giving the <conjugate point> criterion.

= Jacobi curvature operator
{c}
{parent=Jacobi field}
{title2=$\mathcal R_X(v)=R(v,X)X$}

For the <Riemann curvature tensor> convention $R(X,Y)Z=\nabla_X\nabla_YZ-\nabla_Y\nabla_XZ-\nabla_{[X,Y]}Z$, define $\mathcal R_X(v)=R(v,X)X$. Curvature pair interchange makes this operator self-adjoint. If $v\perp X$, then $\langle\mathcal R_Xv,v\rangle=K(v,X)|v|^2|X|^2$. The <Jacobi field> equation is $D_t^2J+\mathcal R_{\dot\gamma}J=0$. The operator $v\mapsto R(X,v)X$ is $-\mathcal R_X$ and is likewise self-adjoint; this sign distinction matters when testing curvature inequalities.

= Jacobi fields from conjugation at a central endpoint
{c}
{parent=Jacobi field}
{title2=$J_X(t)=Xe^{tA}-e^{tA}X$}

For a <Lie group> with a <bi-invariant Riemannian metric>, vary $\gamma(t)=e^{tA}$ by conjugation: $F(s,t)=e^{sX}e^{tA}e^{-sX}$. Its <Jacobi field> is $J_X(t)=Xe^{tA}-e^{tA}X$. If $e^A$ is central, all these fields vanish at both endpoints. Their space has dimension $\dim\mathfrak g-\dim\mathfrak z(A)$, since the kernel is the <centralizer of an element of a Lie algebra>.

= Endpoint-vanishing normal Jacobi fields have dimension at most n-1
{parent=Jacobi field}
{title2=$\dim\{J\perp\dot\gamma:J(0)=J(1)=0\}\le n-1$}

In dimension $n\ge2$, along a nonconstant <geodesic>, such a <Jacobi field> is determined injectively by $D_tJ(0)$, which lies in the $(n-1)$-dimensional normal space. On the round unit sphere the geodesic from a point to its antipode, parametrized with speed $\pi$ on $[0,1]$, has fields $J(t)=\sin(\pi t)E(t)$ for all parallel normal fields $E$, attaining the bound. For a constant geodesic the endpoint-vanishing Jacobi field space is zero.

= Symmetric Hessian of a scalar field
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}

For a torsion-free connection, $U_\mu=\partial_\mu\phi$ satisfies $\nabla_\alpha U_\beta=\nabla_\beta U_\alpha$.

= First Bianchi identity
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}

For a torsion-free connection, $R^\mu{}_{\alpha\beta\gamma}+R^\mu{}_{\beta\gamma\alpha}+R^\mu{}_{\gamma\alpha\beta}=0$.

= Second Bianchi identity
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}

For the <Levi-Civita connection>, the cyclic covariant derivative of curvature vanishes: $\nabla_{[e}R^a{}_{|b|cd]}=0$. Contracting this identity gives the <contracted Bianchi identity>.

= Weyl tensor
{title2=$C_{\alpha\beta\gamma\delta}$}
{c}
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}
{wiki}

The Weyl tensor is the completely trace-free part of the <Riemann curvature tensor>. In four dimensions,
$$
\begin{aligned}
C_{\alpha\beta\gamma\delta}
={}&R_{\alpha\beta\gamma\delta}
-\frac12(
g_{\alpha\gamma}R_{\beta\delta}
+g_{\beta\delta}R_{\alpha\gamma}\\
&\qquad
-g_{\alpha\delta}R_{\beta\gamma}
-g_{\beta\gamma}R_{\alpha\delta})
+\frac R6(
g_{\alpha\gamma}g_{\beta\delta}
-g_{\alpha\delta}g_{\beta\gamma}).
\end{aligned}
$$

= Magnetic part of the Weyl tensor
{parent=Weyl tensor}
{title2=$B_{ab}=\tfrac12\epsilon_{acde}C^{de}{}_{bf}n^cn^f$}

= Magnetic Weyl tensor
{synonym}

In four spacetime dimensions, relative to a timelike <unit normal> $n$, define $B_{ab}=\tfrac12\epsilon_{acde}C^{de}{}_{bf}n^cn^f$, with the chosen spacetime <metric volume tensor>. This is the dual tidal part of the <Weyl tensor>. Normal contractions vanish by curvature and volume-tensor antisymmetry. Its sign depends on the specified orientation and dualization convention.

= Electric part of the Weyl tensor
{parent=Weyl tensor}
{title2=$E_{ab}=C_{acbd}n^cn^d$}

= Electric Weyl tensor
{synonym}

Relative to a timelike <unit normal> $n$, the electric part is $E_{ab}=C_{acbd}n^cn^d$. It describes the tidal component of the <Weyl tensor> measured by that observer. Antisymmetry in each curvature pair makes it spatial in both slots; pair symmetry and vanishing traces make it symmetric and trace-free.

= Trace-free property of the Weyl tensor
{parent=Weyl tensor}
{c}

Every contraction of the Weyl tensor vanishes. Contracting its first and third indices directly cancels the Ricci and scalar-curvature terms; antisymmetry in each pair and symmetry under exchanging the two pairs reduce all other contractions to this one or make them vanish immediately.

= Weyl tensor of a conformally flat metric
{parent=Weyl tensor}
{c}

Every <conformally flat metric> in dimension at least four has identically zero Weyl tensor. The Weyl tensor is the obstruction to local conformal flatness in dimensions at least four.

= First-pair antisymmetry of the Riemann tensor
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}

Applying the curvature commutator to the parallel metric gives $R_{\mu\alpha\beta\gamma}=-R_{\alpha\mu\beta\gamma}$.

= Ricci tensor
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}
{c}

The Ricci tensor is the contraction $R_{\alpha\beta}=R^\mu{}_{\alpha\mu\beta}$ and is symmetric for the Levi-Civita connection.

= Schouten tensor
{c}
{parent=Ricci tensor}
{title2=$S_{ij}=\frac1{n-2}(R_{ij}-\frac{R}{2(n-1)}g_{ij})$}

For dimension $n\geq3$, the Schouten tensor packages the <Ricci tensor> and <scalar curvature> in the trace part of the <Riemann curvature tensor>. In dimension three it is $S_{ij}=R_{ij}-Rg_{ij}/4$, and the full <Riemann curvature tensor> is reconstructed from it.

= Ricci tensor in two dimensions
{parent=Ricci tensor}

The algebraic symmetries of the <Riemann curvature tensor> leave only one independent curvature component in two dimensions and force
$$
R_{\alpha\beta}=\frac12g_{\alpha\beta}R.
$$
Consequently the <Einstein tensor> vanishes identically.

= Two-dimensional general relativity
{parent=Ricci tensor in two dimensions}

With no cosmological term, the two-dimensional <Einstein field equations> reduce identically on the geometric side to $G_{\alpha\beta}=0$. They impose $T_{\alpha\beta}=0$ and supply no local metric dynamics; the Einstein-Hilbert action is topological.

= Contracted Bianchi identity
{parent=Ricci tensor}

The Ricci tensor and scalar satisfy $\nabla^\alpha R_{\alpha\beta}=\tfrac12\nabla_\beta R$.

= Double divergence of the Riemann tensor
{parent=Contracted Bianchi identity}
{title2=$R^{ik}{}_{mn;ik}=0$}

For the <Levi-Civita connection>, the <second Bianchi identity> gives $\nabla_iR^{ik}{}_{mn}=\nabla_mR^k{}_n-\nabla_nR^k{}_m$. Applying another <covariant derivative> and the <contracted Bianchi identity> reduces its divergence to a commuting scalar Hessian plus curvature commutators on the <Ricci tensor>. The latter are the difference of symmetric tensors: a square of the <Ricci tensor> and $R^{ab}R_{anbm}$, which is symmetric in $m,n$ by curvature pair exchange and Ricci symmetry. Both differences vanish. This identity does not require vacuum field equations and is independent of the overall curvature sign convention.

= Einstein tensor
{c}
{parent=Contracted Bianchi identity}
{wiki}

The Einstein tensor is
$$
G_{\mu\nu}=R_{\mu\nu}-\frac12Rg_{\mu\nu}.
$$
The contracted Bianchi identity and metric compatibility give
$\nabla^\nu G_{\mu\nu}=0$, ensuring compatibility of the Einstein equations with covariant stress-energy conservation.

= Flat FLRW Einstein-tensor divergence
{parent=Einstein tensor}

For $ds^2=-dt^2+a(t)^2d\mathbf x^2$, the nonzero connection coefficients are
$$
\Gamma^t_{ij}=a\dot a\,\delta_{ij},
\qquad
\Gamma^i_{tj}=\Gamma^i_{jt}=H\delta^i_j.
$$
With $G_t^t=-3H^2$ and
$G_i^j=-(2\ddot a/a+H^2)\delta_i^j$, direct substitution into the mixed-tensor covariant derivative gives zero.

= de Sitter scale factor in flat slicing
{c}
{parent=Flat FLRW Einstein-tensor divergence}

The vacuum equation with positive cosmological constant gives
$H^2=\Lambda/3$. Its expanding flat-slicing solution is
$$
a(t)=a_0e^{\sqrt{\Lambda/3}\,t}.
$$

= Constant sectional curvature
{title2=$R_{abcd}=K(g_{ac}g_{bd}-g_{ad}g_{bc})$}
{parent=Ricci tensor}
{wiki}

A pseudo-Riemannian manifold has constant sectional curvature $K$ when $R_{abcd}=K(g_{ac}g_{bd}-g_{ad}g_{bc})$. In dimension $n$, its Ricci tensor and scalar are $R_{ab}=(n-1)Kg_{ab}$ and $R=n(n-1)K$.

= Schur theorem in pseudo-Riemannian geometry
{parent=Constant sectional curvature}
{c}

In dimension greater than two, if sectional curvature is pointwise independent of the two-plane, the contracted Bianchi identity forces that curvature to be constant on each connected component.

The positive-definite specialization is <Schur's lemma (Riemannian geometry)>.

= Penrose wave equation
{c}
{parent=Riemann curvature tensor}

In a Ricci-flat spacetime, the Penrose wave equation is a nonlinear covariant wave equation for the Riemann tensor. Its principal part is $\nabla^e\nabla_eR_{abcd}$ and its lower-order terms are quadratic contractions of curvature.

= Normal coordinates
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

= Riemann normal coordinates
{c}
{synonym}

= Geodesic coordinates
{synonym}

At any spacetime point, normal coordinates make the metric equal to the Minkowski metric and all Christoffel symbols vanish at that point.

= Fermi coordinates
{c}
{parent=Normal coordinates}
{wiki}

= Fermi normal coordinates
{c}
{synonym}

<Fermi coordinates> transport a local frame along a timelike <worldline>. Along a <timelike geodesic>, a parallel-transported orthonormal frame defines <Fermi normal coordinates> in which the <metric tensor> is Minkowskian and the <Christoffel symbols> vanish on the worldline. Curvature first appears in terms quadratic in the spatial distance from it, so finite laboratories retain <tidal forces>.

= Lorentz frame
{parent=Normal coordinates}
{c}

A <Lorentz frame> at a spacetime event is an orthonormal tangent basis with metric $\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Future-oriented frames are related by proper orthochronous <Lorentz transformations>. Any such basis can be realized at the event by <normal coordinates>.

= Orthonormal frame in spacetime
{parent=General relativity}

= Orthonormal tetrad
{synonym}

= Orthonormal frame
{synonym}

An orthonormal frame $\{e_a\}$ satisfies $g(e_a,e_b)=\eta_{ab}$. Any two such frames differ by a local Lorentz transformation.

= Parallel-propagated orthonormal frame
{parent=Orthonormal frame in spacetime}

Along a curve with tangent $T$, a frame is parallel propagated when $\nabla_Te_a=0$. Metric compatibility makes every inner product $g(e_a,e_b)$ constant along the curve, so an initially orthonormal frame remains orthonormal.

= Orthonormal coframe in spacetime
{parent=Orthonormal frame in spacetime}

= Orthonormal coframe
{synonym}

= Vierbein
{synonym}

The dual coframe obeys $\theta^a(e_b)=\delta^a_b$ and reconstructs the metric as $g=\eta_{ab}\theta^a\otimes\theta^b$.

= Curved spacetime gamma matrices
{parent=Orthonormal coframe in spacetime}
{title2=$\gamma_a=e_a{}^I\Gamma_I,\quad\{\gamma_a,\gamma_b\}=2g_{ab}\mathbf1$}

= Curved gamma matrices
{synonym}

An orthonormal coframe converts fixed flat <gamma matrices> to coordinate-index Clifford multiplication. Its metric identity gives the curved anticommutation relation. The <spin connection> is chosen so that these matrices are covariantly constant under the combined spin and tensor connection, which is the tetrad postulate in Clifford form.

= Zweibein
{parent=Orthonormal coframe in spacetime}
{title2=$e^A{}_{\mu}$}

A zweibein is a two-dimensional <orthonormal coframe in spacetime>: its components obey $h_{\mu\nu}=\eta_{AB}e^A{}_{\mu}e^B{}_{\nu}$. It converts worldsheet coordinate indices into local Lorentz-frame indices and defines curved gamma matrices $\gamma^\mu=e_A{}^\mu\gamma^A$. The associated <spin connection> supplies the <covariant derivative> of worldsheet spinors. In flat <conformal gauge> the coframe can be constant.

\Include[connection-1-form]{parent=Orthonormal coframe in spacetime}

= Stress-energy tensor
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

= Energy-momentum tensor
{synonym}

The stress-energy tensor describes local energy density, momentum density, and stress. Compatibility with the <Einstein field equations> requires covariant conservation $\nabla^\mu T_{\mu\nu}=0$.

= Hilbert stress-energy tensor
{c}
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$T_{ab}=\frac{2}{\sqrt{-g}}\frac{\delta S_m}{\delta g^{ab}}\quad(+---)$}

The metric variational stress tensor is defined by the matter action's response to a metric change. With signature $(+---)$ and the corresponding usual matter-action signs, the displayed convention means $\delta S_m=\tfrac12\int\sqrt{-g}\,T_{ab}\delta g^{ab}=-\tfrac12\int\sqrt{-g}\,T^{ab}\delta g_{ab}$. Fix the independent matter variables while varying the metric. Sign conventions must be matched to the action; copying a mostly-plus stress formula into this convention can reverse its energy density.

= Stress-energy tensor of a canonical scalar field
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$T_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu\phi\partial_\nu\phi-g_{\mu\nu}[(\nabla\phi)^2/2-V]$}

For signature $(+---)$ and $S_\phi=\int\sqrt{-g}[(\nabla\phi)^2/2-V(\phi)]$, the convention $T_{\mu\nu}=2(\delta S_\phi/\delta g^{\mu\nu})/\sqrt{-g}$ gives the displayed tensor. Varying the inverse metric yields the derivative product, while varying the volume element yields the term proportional to $g_{\mu\nu}$. A homogeneous field has energy density $\dot\phi^2/2+V$ and pressure $\dot\phi^2/2-V$.

= Stress-energy tensor improvement
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$T\prime{}^{\mu\nu}=T^{\mu\nu}+\partial_\rho B^{\rho\mu\nu}$}

Adding $\partial_\rho B^{\rho\mu\nu}$ with $B^{\rho\mu\nu}=-B^{\mu\rho\nu}$ to a <stress-energy tensor> preserves its divergence identically, since commuting partial derivatives annihilates the antisymmetric pair. The total <four-momentum> changes only by a spatial boundary term. Improvements can make local densities symmetric or <gauge invariant>; equality of integrated charges requires the chosen <boundary conditions>. Equation-of-motion terms can also relate representatives <on shell>.

= Improved stress-energy tensor of a free massless scalar
{parent=Stress-energy tensor improvement}
{title2=$\Theta_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu\phi\partial_\nu\phi-\frac12\eta_{\mu\nu}(\partial\phi)^2+\frac{d-2}{4(d-1)}(\eta_{\mu\nu}\Box-\partial_\mu\partial_\nu)\phi^2$}

For $d>1$, the displayed <stress-energy tensor> is symmetric and conserved on the free massless <scalar field> equation $\Box\phi=0$. Its improvement is identically divergence-free. Since $\Box\phi^2=2(\partial\phi)^2$ on shell, its trace vanishes. Contracting it with a <conformal Killing vector field> therefore produces a conserved conformal current. In $d=2$ the improvement coefficient is zero: the canonical tensor already has zero trace.

= Stress-energy superpotential
{parent=Stress-energy tensor improvement}
{title2=$B^{\rho\mu\nu}=-B^{\mu\rho\nu}$}

A <stress-energy superpotential> is an antisymmetric tensor $B^{\rho\mu\nu}=-B^{\mu\rho\nu}$ whose divergence supplies a <stress-energy tensor improvement>. In the <Maxwell field>, the potential-dependent expression $B^{\rho\mu}{}_{\nu}=F^{\mu\rho}A_\nu$ produces the difference between canonical and gauge-invariant <stress-energy tensors> <on shell>. Antisymmetry ensures identically conserved improvement terms.

= Dust stress-energy tensor
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$T^{\mu\nu}=mn_0u^\mu u^\nu$}

A pressureless fluid with proper number density $n_0$, rest mass $m$ and <four-velocity> $u$ has tensor $mn_0u^\mu u^\nu$. In its local rest frame there is energy density but no spatial stress. Particle-number conservation and the Lorentz force make its divergence the electromagnetic force density.

= Null dust
{parent=Dust stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$T_{ab}=\varepsilon k_ak_b$}

Null dust is matter with a <stress-energy tensor> $T_{ab}=\varepsilon k_ak_b$ for a future-directed <null vector> $k$. For every future-directed <timelike vector> $t$, the energy current is $-T^a{}_bt^b=\varepsilon(-k\cdot t)k^a$. Therefore its <dominant energy condition> holds exactly when $\varepsilon\geq0$, allowing the zero current. Rescaling $k$ rescales $\varepsilon$ inversely by the square and leaves the <stress-energy tensor> unchanged. Ingoing <Vaidya metric> matter has $k=-\partial_r$, $k_a=-(dv)_a$, and $\varepsilon=\dot m/(4\pi r^2)$.

= Momentum density
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$q^{\hat i}=T^{\hat0\hat i}$}

<Momentum density> is <momentum> per unit physical volume. In a local <orthonormal tetrad> of the measuring observer it is $q^{\hat i}=T^{\hat0\hat i}$. A <perfect fluid in general relativity> moving at a small velocity relative to that observer has $q^{\hat i}=(\rho+P)v^{\hat i}$ to first order. For isotropic background <photons>, $P=\rho/3$, so the factor is $4\rho/3$ rather than $\rho$. Integrating the <kinetic stress-energy tensor> gives $q^{\hat i}=\int f p^{\hat i}\,d^3p$.

= Anisotropic stress
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$\pi^{ij}=T^{ij}-\tfrac13\delta^{ij}T^k{}_k$}

In a local <orthonormal tetrad>, <anisotropic stress> is the trace-free spatial part of the <stress-energy tensor>, conventionally denoted $\pi^{ij}$. Some <linear cosmological perturbation theory> conventions instead use $\Pi^{ij}=-\pi^{ij}$ and write $T^{ij}=P\delta^{ij}-\Pi^{ij}$. The sign must be specified before using a <photon Euler equation>. The <photon quadrupole> determines the linear <photon> <anisotropic stress>.

= Stress-energy tensor of a nonminimally coupled scalar field
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$T_{ab}=\nabla_a\Phi\nabla_b\Phi-\frac12g_{ab}(\nabla\Phi)^2+2\xi[\Phi^2G_{ab}+g_{ab}\Box(\Phi^2)-\nabla_a\nabla_b(\Phi^2)]$}

For action $S=\int\sqrt{-g}[-\frac12(\nabla\Phi)^2-\xi R\Phi^2]d^4x$, <metric variation> defines $\delta S=\frac12\int\sqrt{-g}T^{ab}\delta g_{ab}d^4x$ and gives the displayed tensor. The <contracted Bianchi identity> and the scalar derivative commutator yield $\nabla^aT_{ab}=(\Box\Phi-2\xi R\Phi)\nabla_b\Phi$, so the <stress-energy tensor> is conserved on the field equation.

= Stress-energy conservation
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}

= Covariant conservation of stress-energy
{synonym}

Local energy-momentum conservation is the covariant equation $\nabla_\mu T^{\mu\nu}=0$. Its normal and spatial projections give energy and momentum evolution equations in a spacetime foliation.

= Noether identity
{c}
{parent=Stress-energy conservation}

A Noether identity is an off-shell differential relation among Euler-Lagrange expressions implied by a local gauge symmetry. Diffeomorphism invariance relates the divergence of the stress-energy tensor to the matter field equations.

= Diffeomorphism identity for a metric action
{parent=Noether identity}
{title2=$\nabla_aE^{ab}\equiv0$}

= Off-shell metric divergence identity
{synonym}

For a diffeomorphism-invariant pure metric action, write $\delta S=-\int\sqrt{-g}E^{ab}\delta g_{ab}$. Substitute $\delta g_{ab}=\nabla_a\xi_b+\nabla_b\xi_a$ for an arbitrary compactly supported <vector field> and integrate by parts. Symmetry of $E^{ab}$ gives $\nabla_aE^{ab}=0$ without using field equations. This <Noether identity> concerns the gravitational variational tensor alone.

= Diffeomorphism Noether identity for a scalar field
{parent=Noether identity}
{title2=$\nabla^aT_{ab}=\frac1{\sqrt{-g}}\frac{\delta S}{\delta\Phi}\nabla_b\Phi$}

Simultaneously varying a <metric tensor> and a <scalar field> by the <Lie derivative> along a compactly supported <vector field> leaves a covariant matter action invariant. <Integration by parts> gives the displayed <Noether identity>. It implies <stress-energy conservation> when the scalar equation holds without needing to impose the metric field equation.

= Perfect fluid in general relativity
{title2=$T_{ab}=(\rho+p)u_au_b+pg_{ab}$}
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}

A relativistic perfect fluid has energy density $\rho$, isotropic pressure $p$ and unit timelike four-velocity $u$. Projecting $\nabla_aT^{ab}=0$ along and orthogonally to $u$ gives its energy and Euler equations.

For a <perfect fluid>, the relativistic stress tensor is $T_{ab}=(\rho+p)u_au_b+pg_{ab}$ in signature $(-,+,+,+)$ with $u^au_a=-1$.

= Perfect-fluid alignment in a static spacetime
{parent=Perfect fluid in general relativity}
{title2=$u^a=k^a/\sqrt{k^bk_b}$}

For <metric signature> $(+---)$, a <perfect fluid> has $T_{ab}=(\rho+p)u_au_b-pg_{ab}$. The <Einstein field equations> and <static Killing field is a Ricci eigenvector> imply $(\rho+p)(k\cdot u)u_{[a}k_{b]}=0$. Two nonzero timelike vectors cannot be orthogonal, so $k\cdot u\ne0$. If $\rho+p\ne0$, the <four-velocity> is parallel to the static <Killing vector>. Choosing both future-directed gives the displayed normalization. For $\rho+p=0$, the <stress-energy tensor> is proportional to the <metric tensor> and does not determine a preferred fluid velocity; the strict enthalpy hypothesis is therefore material.

= Stiff fluid
{parent=Perfect fluid in general relativity}
{title2=$P=\rho$}

A <perfect fluid> with <equation of state> $P=\rho$ is called a stiff fluid. For positive <energy density>, its constant equation-of-state parameter is $w=1$. The <cosmological continuity equation> gives $\rho\propto a^{-6}$. In a flat universe dominated by this fluid, the <Friedmann equation> then gives $a(t)\propto(t-t_B)^{1/3}$. This statement assumes a nonzero fluid density and negligible other components.

= Homogeneous free scalar as a stiff fluid
{parent=Stiff fluid}
{title2=$a^3\dot\phi=\mathrm{constant}$}

For a spatially homogeneous <minimally coupled scalar field> with zero <scalar potential>, the <stress-energy tensor> has $\rho=P=\dot\phi^2/2$. Its field equation is $\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi=0$, so $a^3\dot\phi$ is constant and its <energy density> decreases as $a^{-6}$. Thus its homogeneous stress is precisely that of a <stiff fluid>. Spatial gradients would introduce directional stresses, so this equivalence requires homogeneity.

= Cosmological contraction with a free homogeneous scalar
{parent=Homogeneous free scalar as a stiff fluid}
{title2=$a(\tau)=(-\tau)^{1/2},\quad\phi_0=\sqrt{3/2}\log(-\tau)$}

In units $8\pi G=1$, a free homogeneous scalar has $\rho=P=\dot\phi_0^2/2$ and therefore a stiff equation of state. Its equation gives $a^2\phi_0'=\text{constant}$, while the <Friedmann equation> gives $3\mathcal H^2=\phi_0'^2/2$. The displayed contracting branch satisfies both equations for negative <conformal time>. Constant normalizations and an additive scalar shift can be restored.

= Newtonian-potential Hankel modes in stiff contraction
{parent=Cosmological contraction with a free homogeneous scalar}
{title2=$\Phi_k=N_k(-\tau)^{-1}H_1^{(1)}(-k\tau)$}

In <conformal Newtonian gauge>, a free scalar has no linear anisotropic stress. Eliminating its perturbation with the momentum constraint gives $\Phi_k''+3\Phi_k'/\tau+k^2\Phi_k=0$. Substitution of $\Phi_k=(-\tau)^{-1}y(-k\tau)$ gives the order-one <Bessel equation>. The displayed <Hankel function> has positive incoming frequency $e^{-ik\tau}$ at early times.

= Vacuum-normalized Newtonian spectrum in stiff contraction
{parent=Newtonian-potential Hankel modes in stiff contraction}
{title2=$|\Phi_k|^2\sim3\hbar/(8\pi k^4\tau^4)$}

The momentum constraint relates the incoming scalar mode $a^{-1}\sqrt{\hbar/(2k)}e^{-ik\tau}$ to the <Newtonian-potential Hankel modes in stiff contraction>. Large-argument Hankel asymptotics give $|N_k|^2=3\pi\hbar/(32k^2)$. The small-argument behavior $H_1^{(1)}(z)\sim-2i/(\pi z)$ then gives the displayed spectrum. Its dimensional power is $k^{-4}$ and its power per logarithmic interval is proportional to $k^{-1}$. These are Newtonian-potential spectra; a curvature-perturbation spectrum need not have the same exponent.

= Relativistic perfect-fluid energy equation
{parent=Perfect fluid in general relativity}

The timelike projection of stress-energy conservation is $u^a\nabla_a\rho+(\rho+p)\nabla_au^a=0$. It is the local adiabatic first law for a perfect fluid.

= Relativistic Euler equation
{parent=Perfect fluid in general relativity}

The spatial projection of stress-energy conservation is $(\rho+p)u^b\nabla_bu^a=-(g^{ab}+u^au^b)\nabla_bp$.

= Hydrostatic equilibrium along a Killing vector
{parent=Relativistic Euler equation}
{title2=$dp=-(\rho+p)d\log N,\quad N=\sqrt{K^aK_a}$}

For <metric signature> $(+---)$, a stationary <perfect fluid> follows a timelike <Killing vector field> $K$ with <four-velocity> $U=K/N$. Its <Killing-flow four-acceleration> is $U^b\nabla_bU_a=-\nabla_a\log N$. The <relativistic Euler equation> and stationarity of the <pressure> give the displayed <hydrostatic equilibrium> equation. This uses stationarity along $K$ and does not require hypersurface orthogonality.

= Stationary radiation density
{parent=Hydrostatic equilibrium along a Killing vector}
{title2=$\rho N^4=C$}

The <equation of state> of isotropic <blackbody radiation> is $p=\rho/3$. The <barotropic hydrostatic first integral> gives $\rho N^4=C$. The <Stefan–Boltzmann law> $\rho\propto T^4$ then yields the <Tolman–Ehrenfest relation> $TN=\mathrm{constant}$. Here $N=\sqrt{|K^aK_a|}$ for the stationary timelike <Killing vector field>; its normalization only changes the constants.

= Radiation fluid has no regular free surface
{parent=Stationary radiation density}
{title2=$\rho=C/N^4>0$}

A connected nonvacuum <perfect fluid> with <equation of state> $p=\rho/3$ obeys the <stationary radiation density> law $\rho=C/N^4$ with $C>0$. On a compact region of a regular <spacetime>, a smooth <Killing vector field> has bounded norm. Consequently $\rho$ cannot tend to zero at a finite regular <free surface>. A vanishing timelike norm instead makes the density diverge. A radiation-filled cavity can have a material wall with nonzero boundary <pressure>; this is a different boundary condition.

= Barotropic hydrostatic first integral
{parent=Hydrostatic equilibrium along a Killing vector}
{title2=$\int^p\frac{d\widetilde p}{\rho(\widetilde p)+\widetilde p}+\log N=C$}

For a <barotropic fluid> in <hydrostatic equilibrium along a Killing vector>, assume $\rho+p>0$. Integrating $dp/(\rho+p)=-d\log N$ gives the displayed <first integral>, constant on each connected fluid region. For the <equation of state> $p=w\rho$ with constant $w>0$, it gives $\rho N^{(1+w)/w}=C_1$.

= Tolman–Oppenheimer–Volkoff equation
{title2=TOV equation}
{c}
{parent=Perfect fluid in general relativity}
{wiki}

The Tolman–Oppenheimer–Volkoff equation governs a static, spherically symmetric <perfect fluid in general relativity>. In geometrized units, if $m(r)$ is the mass inside areal radius $r$, then
$$
\frac{dm}{dr}=4\pi r^2\rho,
\qquad
\frac{dp}{dr}=-\frac{(\rho+p)(m+4\pi r^3p)}{r(r-2m)}.
$$

= Stellar compactness
{parent=Tolman–Oppenheimer–Volkoff equation}
{title2=$C(r)=\frac{m(r)}r$}

The ratio of enclosed gravitational mass to areal radius in units $G=c=1$. In a spherical star the local value is $m(r)/r$, and the surface value is $M/R$. Bounds on this ratio constrain relativistic stellar models; it is unrelated to topological compactness.

= Regular central expansion of the TOV equations
{parent=Tolman–Oppenheimer–Volkoff equation}
{title2=$m=\frac{4\pi}3\rho_cr^3+O(r^5)$}

For a smooth centre with density $\rho_c$ and pressure $p_c$, the <Tolman–Oppenheimer–Volkoff equation> gives $m(r)=4\pi\rho_cr^3/3+O(r^5)$ and $p(r)=p_c-2\pi(\rho_c+p_c)(\rho_c/3+p_c)r^2+O(r^4)$. A fixed <barotropic stellar equation of state> leaves one physical central parameter; the additive lapse constant fixes time normalization.

= Interior Schwarzschild metric
{c}
{parent=Tolman–Oppenheimer–Volkoff equation}
{wiki}

= Interior Schwarzschild solution
{c}
{synonym}

The interior Schwarzschild metric is the exact static solution for a spherical fluid of constant density. If the star has mass $M$ and radius $R$, its pressure is
$$
p(r)=\rho
\frac{\sqrt{1-2Mr^2/R^3}-\sqrt{1-2M/R}}
{3\sqrt{1-2M/R}-\sqrt{1-2Mr^2/R^3}}.
$$

= Central pressure of a constant-density relativistic star
{parent=Interior Schwarzschild metric}

The <interior Schwarzschild metric> has central pressure
$$
p(0)=\rho
\frac{1-\sqrt{1-2M/R}}
{3\sqrt{1-2M/R}-1}.
$$
It diverges when $M/R=4/9$, the limiting compactness in <Buchdahl's theorem>.

= Dominant-energy compactness bound for an incompressible star
{parent=Central pressure of a constant-density relativistic star}
{title2=$\frac MR\leq\frac38$}

The <dominant energy condition> for the <interior Schwarzschild metric> reduces to its largest pressure satisfying $p_c\leq\rho$. Substitution of the exact central pressure gives $\sqrt{1-2M/R}\geq1/2$, hence $M/R\leq3/8$. Equality has finite central pressure and is allowed by the non-strict condition.

= Buchdahl's theorem
{c}
{parent=Tolman–Oppenheimer–Volkoff equation}
{wiki}

Buchdahl's theorem gives $2M/R\leq8/9$ for a static spherical perfect-fluid star whose density is nonincreasing outwards, subject to its regularity assumptions. Equality would require divergent central pressure, so every regular such star satisfies $M/R<4/9$.

= High-density core bound from TOV compactness
{parent=Buchdahl's theorem}
{title2=$a<\frac1{\sqrt{3\pi\rho_0}}$}

Let $\rho_0>0$ be a density threshold and let $a,\mu$ be the radius and enclosed mass where a monotonically decreasing stellar density reaches that threshold. Then $\mu\geq4\pi\rho_0a^3/3$. A local compactness bound $\mu/a<4/9$ implies $a<(3\pi\rho_0)^{-1/2}$ and $\mu<4/(9\sqrt{3\pi\rho_0})$, independently of the <equation of state> above the threshold. A total-mass bound also requires suitable control of the known low-density envelope.

= Null energy condition
{title2=NEC}
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{wiki}

The null energy condition requires $T_{\mu\nu}k^\mu k^\nu\geq0$ for every null vector $k$. Through the Einstein field equations this is equivalent to $R_{\mu\nu}k^\mu k^\nu\geq0$ when the cosmological term is absent.

= Null energy condition for a canonical scalar field
{parent=Null energy condition}
{title2=$T_{ab}\ell^a\ell^b=(\ell^a\partial_a\phi)^2\geq0$}

The classical <stress-energy tensor of a canonical scalar field> has a gradient-product term and a metric-proportional term. Contracting with a real <null vector> removes the latter, leaving the displayed square for a real field. This proves the <null energy condition> for a minimally coupled canonical scalar, including the massless case. It is not a statement about arbitrary curvature couplings, wrong-sign kinetic terms or a renormalized quantum <stress-energy tensor>.

= Null energy condition for a planar warped spacetime
{parent=Null energy condition}
{title2=$(\log A)''\le0$}

In a <planar warped spacetime>, contraction of the <Ricci tensor> with a <null vector> in an <orthonormal frame> gives $R_{ab}k^ak^b=-2(\log A)''(k^3)^2$. The <Einstein field equations> identify this with $8\pi T_{ab}k^ak^b$; metric terms, including a cosmological constant, vanish in a null contraction. Thus the <null energy condition> is equivalent to concavity of $\log A$ in the proper transverse coordinate $z$. Null directions confined to a planar slice saturate the condition.

= Null convergence condition
{parent=Null energy condition}
{title2=$R_{ab}k^ak^b\geq0$}

The <null convergence condition> requires $R_{ab}k^ak^b\geq0$ for every <null vector>. Through the <Einstein field equations>, it follows from the <null energy condition>, since all terms proportional to the <metric tensor> vanish on a <null vector>.

= Cosmological constant preserves null convergence
{parent=Null convergence condition}
{title2=$R_{ab}\ell^a\ell^b=8\pi G T_{ab}\ell^a\ell^b$}

Contract the <Einstein field equations> with a <null vector>. Both the trace term and the <cosmological constant> term multiply $g_{ab}\ell^a\ell^b=0$ and disappear. The <null energy condition> therefore still implies the <null convergence condition>, independently of the sign of the <cosmological constant>. A <black-hole area theorem> still needs the appropriate global horizon assumptions as well as this local focusing condition.

= Averaged null energy condition
{title2=ANEC}
{parent=Null energy condition}

The averaged null energy condition requires
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}T_{ab}k^ak^b\,d\lambda\geq0
$$
along every complete affinely parametrized null geodesic. It is weaker than the pointwise null energy condition.

= Averaged null energy condition for a nonminimally coupled scalar
{parent=Averaged null energy condition}

For the curvature-coupled scalar equation considered here, contraction with an affine null tangent and integration by parts gives, for $\xi<0$,
$$
\int T_{ab}k^ak^b\,d\lambda
=\int\frac{1+\xi(4\xi-1)\Phi^2}{(1-\xi\Phi^2)^2}(\Phi')^2\,d\lambda\geq0
$$
when $\Phi\Phi'/(1-\xi\Phi^2)$ vanishes at the endpoints.

= Conserved Klein-Gordon scalar stress-energy ansatz
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{c}

For $\nabla_\alpha\nabla^\alpha\phi=m^2\phi$, consider
$$
T_{\mu\nu}=\frac12\left[
\nabla_\mu\phi\nabla_\nu\phi
+g_{\mu\nu}\left(A\nabla_\rho\phi\nabla^\rho\phi+B\phi^2\right)
\right].
$$
Covariant conservation for all solutions holds exactly when
$$
A=-\frac12,
\qquad
B=-\frac{m^2}{2}.
$$

= Klein-Gordon scalar stress-energy tensor
{c}
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}

For a real scalar satisfying $\nabla_a\nabla^a\psi-\mu^2\psi=0$,
$$
T_{ab}=\nabla_a\psi\nabla_b\psi-\frac12g_{ab}\left(\nabla_c\psi\nabla^c\psi+\mu^2\psi^2\right)
$$
is symmetric and covariantly conserved.

= Weak energy condition
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{wiki}

The weak energy condition requires
$$
T_{\mu\nu}X^\mu X^\nu\geq0
$$
for every timelike vector $X$, so every observer measures nonnegative energy density.

= Weak energy condition for a quadratic scalar stress-energy ansatz
{parent=Weak energy condition}

For the general scalar ansatz in <conserved Klein-Gordon scalar stress-energy ansatz>, work in a unit timelike frame. Then
$$
2T_{00}=(1+A)(\partial_0\phi)^2
-A|\nabla_{\rm s}\phi|^2-B\phi^2.
$$
The weak energy condition for arbitrary local field data is therefore equivalent to
$$
-1\leq A\leq0,
\qquad B\leq0.
$$

= Strong energy condition
{title2=SEC}
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{wiki}

The strong energy condition requires $(T_{ab}-Tg_{ab}/2)u^au^b\geq0$ for every timelike vector $u$ in four spacetime dimensions. Through the <Einstein field equations>, it implies the timelike convergence condition $R_{ab}u^au^b\geq0$ when the cosmological constant vanishes.

= Dominant energy condition
{title2=DEC}
{parent=Stress-energy tensor}
{wiki}

The dominant energy condition requires the energy flux measured by every future-directed timelike observer to be future-directed and causal. Equivalently, $T_{ab}u^av^b\geq0$ for every pair of future-directed causal vectors $u$ and $v$ under the $(-,+,+,+)$ convention.

= Dominant energy condition for a kinetic scalar field
{parent=Dominant energy condition}

For a <scalar field> with $X=-q^2/2$, Lagrangian $F(X)$ and <stress-energy tensor> $T_{ab}=F\prime q_aq_b+Fg_{ab}$, the inequalities $A=F\prime\ge0$ and $Y=XF\prime-F\ge0$ imply the <dominant energy condition> for any gradient type. In a local <Lorentz frame>, $\rho=A(q_0^2+\sum_iq_i^2)/2+Y\ge0$ and $\rho^2-\sum_iT_{0i}^2=A^2X^2+A(q_0^2+\sum_iq_i^2)Y+Y^2\ge0$.

= Einstein field equations
{parent=General relativity}
{c}
{wiki}

= Einstein equation
{c}
{synonym}

With signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and vanishing cosmological constant, the Einstein field equations are
$$
R_{\mu\nu}-\frac12 g_{\mu\nu}R=\kappa T_{\mu\nu}.
$$
In four spacetime dimensions, contraction with $g^{\mu\nu}$ gives the useful trace relation $R=-\kappa T$.

= Newtonian normalization of the Einstein field equations
{c}
{parent=Einstein field equations}
{title2=$G_{ab}+\Lambda g_{ab}=\kappa T_{ab}$}

Assume a metric theory in four dimensions, a <Levi-Civita connection>, covariant <stress-energy conservation> and field equations linear in second metric derivatives. The <contracted Bianchi identity> selects $G_{ab}=R_{ab}-g_{ab}R/2$. Its trace-reversed equation is $R_{ab}=\kappa(T_{ab}-g_{ab}T/2)+\Lambda g_{ab}$. In the convention $R_{00}\simeq-\nabla^2\varphi$ and signature $(+---)$, nonrelativistic matter gives $R_{00}\simeq\kappa\rho/2$. The <Poisson equation> $\nabla^2\varphi=4\pi G\rho$ fixes $\kappa=-8\pi G$. Reversing the curvature convention reverses this displayed coupling sign without changing the physical theory. The <strong equivalence principle> alone does not establish uniqueness without the stated additional assumptions.

= Einstein-equation coupling under reversed curvature
{c}
{parent=Einstein field equations}
{title2=$R^a{}_{bcd}\mapsto-R^a{}_{bcd}\implies\kappa\mapsto-\kappa$}

With the metric and <stress-energy tensor> fixed and zero <cosmological constant>, changing the sign convention for the <Riemann curvature tensor> changes the signs of the <Ricci tensor>, <scalar curvature> and <Einstein tensor>. The same physical field equation is therefore written with the opposite coupling sign. For signature $(+---)$, the convention $R^a{}_{bcd}=\partial_d\Gamma^a_{bc}-\partial_c\Gamma^a_{bd}-\Gamma^a_{ec}\Gamma^e_{bd}+\Gamma^a_{ed}\Gamma^e_{bc}$ and $R_{ab}=R^c{}_{acb}$ gives $R_{00}\simeq-\Delta\Phi$ in a static <Newtonian limit>. The trace-reversed equation and $\Delta\Phi=4\pi G\rho$ then require $G_{ab}=-8\pi GT_{ab}$.

= Planar Einstein metric with cubic radial function
{parent=Einstein field equations}
{title2=$ds^2=z^{-2}[-Fdt^2+dx^2+dy^2+F^{-1}dz^2],\quad F=1-\alpha z^3$}

This metric has $R_{ab}=-3g_{ab}$ and <cosmological constant> $\Lambda=-3$ in unit curvature-radius normalization. Its regular static coframe is real when $F>0$ and $z\ne0$. Setting $q=\alpha z^3$, its six independent <curvature 2-form> coefficients are $-(1-q)$ in the $03$ and $12$ planes and $-(1+q/2)$ in the other four planes. Their Ricci contractions cancel $q$. The <Weyl tensor> squared invariant is $12\alpha^2z^6$, so nonzero $\alpha$ does not give constant sectional curvature despite the Einstein equation.

= Lovelock's theorem
{c}
{parent=Einstein field equations}

In four dimensions, a local natural symmetric divergence-free metric tensor involving the metric and its derivatives of at most second order has the Lovelock form $aG_{ab}+bg_{ab}$ with constant coefficients. Generic nonlinear metric <f(R) gravity> instead has fourth-order terms and does not meet that derivative-order hypothesis. For affine $f$, the metric field equation is precisely of Lovelock form.

= Vacuum Einstein equations
{parent=Einstein field equations}
{c}

In a region with vanishing <stress-energy tensor>, the <Einstein field equations> reduce to $R_{\mu\nu}=0$ when the <cosmological constant> vanishes.

= Vacuum spacetime
{parent=Vacuum Einstein equations}
{title2=$T_{ab}=0$}

A <vacuum spacetime> has vanishing matter <stress-energy tensor>. In $D>2$ dimensions, tracing the <vacuum Einstein equations> gives $R=2D\Lambda/(D-2)$ and hence $R_{ab}=2\Lambda g_{ab}/(D-2)$; in four dimensions this reduces to $R_{ab}=\Lambda g_{ab}$. With zero <cosmological constant> it is <Ricci flat>, although its full <Riemann curvature tensor> can be nonzero: a vacuum <Kerr black hole> and vacuum <gravitational waves> illustrate that distinction.

= Einstein-Hilbert action
{c}
{parent=Einstein field equations}
{wiki}

The Einstein-Hilbert action is the integral of the <Ricci scalar> against the metric volume form. Varying it with respect to the metric gives the vacuum <Einstein field equations>, up to a boundary term.

= Einstein-Cartan theory
{parent=Einstein-Hilbert action}
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$(e^a,\omega^{ab})$}

An independent <vierbein> and metric-compatible <spin connection> allow <spin> density to source a <torsion tensor>. The connection equation from the <Einstein-Hilbert action> is algebraic in torsion. In the absence of connection-dependent matter it fixes the <Levi-Civita connection>. For a minimally coupled <Dirac field>, eliminating the connection generates a local four-fermion interaction. Thus its equivalent torsion-free second-order <action> includes this contact term; simply dropping it defines a different matter theory.

= 1.5-order formalism
{parent=Einstein-Cartan theory}
{title2=$\delta S/\delta\omega=0$}

Solve the algebraic connection equation for $\widehat\omega(e,\chi)$, but perform the remaining variations as though the connection were independent. The chain-rule contribution $(\delta S/\delta\omega)\delta\widehat\omega$ vanishes on that solution. The resulting equations are those of the connection-eliminated second-order <action>. This is especially useful for local <supersymmetry> calculations, where the gravitino-induced contorsion is quadratic in <fermions> and the omitted induced connection variation would otherwise be lengthy.

= Algebraic torsion of a Dirac field
{parent=Einstein-Cartan theory}
{title2=$K_{abc}=-\kappa^2\bar\chi\gamma_{abc}\chi/4$}

Use $K_{abc}=e_a{}^\mu(\omega-\omega(e))_{\mu bc}$ and $T_{abc}=K_{bac}-K_{cab}$. For the symmetrized Dirac <action> $-e\bar\chi\gamma^\mu\overleftrightarrow\nabla_\mu\chi/2-em\bar\chi\chi$, varying the <spin connection> gives $-e\bar\chi\gamma^{\mu ab}\chi\,\delta\omega_{\mu ab}/4$. Only totally antisymmetric contorsion is sourced. Its <action> is $-eK^2/(2\kappa^2)-eK_{abc}\bar\chi\gamma^{abc}\chi/4$, hence the stated solution. Substitution generates $e\kappa^2(\bar\chi\gamma^{abc}\chi)(\bar\chi\gamma_{abc}\chi)/32$. The torsion is an <auxiliary field>, not an independent propagating degree of freedom.

= First-order three-dimensional gravity
{parent=Einstein-Hilbert action}
{title2=$T^a=0,\quad R^{ab}=-3\lambda E^a\wedge E^b$}

For a nondegenerate <orthonormal coframe in spacetime> $E^a$ and independent antisymmetric Lorentz <connection 1-form>, the action $\int\epsilon_{abc}(R^{ab}\wedge E^c+\lambda E^a\wedge E^b\wedge E^c)$ has independent coframe and connection variations. Since $\delta R^{ab}=D\delta\omega^{ab}$, integration by parts yields a connection equation $\epsilon_{abc}T^c=0$. The three variations of the cubic coframe term give the coframe equation $\epsilon_{abc}(R^{ab}+3\lambda E^a\wedge E^b)=0$. In three dimensions these are exactly the equations displayed above. The solution has <constant sectional curvature> $K=-3\lambda$. Omitting the factor of three changes the cosmological normalization.

= Einstein-Proca theory
{c}
{parent=Einstein-Hilbert action}

Einstein-Proca theory couples <general relativity> to a massive vector field governed by the <Proca action>. Its matter stress-energy tensor is
$$
T_{ab}=F_{ac}F_b{}^c-\frac14g_{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd}
+m^2\left(A_aA_b-\frac12g_{ab}A_cA^c\right).
$$
The <contracted Bianchi identity> makes the Einstein equation compatible with the covariant <Proca equation>.

= Proca stress-energy tensor
{parent=Einstein-Proca theory}

The Proca stress-energy tensor is the metric variation of the matter part of the <Proca action>. It consists of the <electromagnetic stress-energy tensor> plus the quadratic mass contribution $m^2(A_aA_b-g_{ab}A^2/2)$.

= Einstein-Maxwell-dilaton theory
{c}
{parent=Einstein-Hilbert action}

Einstein-Maxwell-dilaton theory couples the metric to a scalar field and a Maxwell field with scalar-dependent gauge coupling, typically through $e^{\alpha\Phi}F_{ab}F^{ab}$. Its Maxwell equation is $\nabla_a(e^{\alpha\Phi}F^{ab})=0$.

= Magnetically charged dilaton black hole
{parent=Einstein-Maxwell-dilaton theory}
{title2=$V=1-r_+/r,\quad R^2=r(r-r_-)$}

The unit-coupling magnetic solution of <Einstein-Maxwell-dilaton theory> has $ds^2=-Vdt^2+V^{-1}dr^2+R^2d\Omega_2^2$, magnetic potential $A=P\cos\theta\,d\phi$, and dilaton $\phi=-\tfrac12\log(1-r_-/r)$, where $r_-=2P^2/r_+$ and $r_+>0$. Its physical asymptotic component has $r>r_-$. For $0\le r_-<r_+$ it has a regular <Killing horizon> at $r_+$, area $4\pi r_+(r_+-r_-)$ and <surface gravity> $1/(2r_+)$. Its asymptotic mass is $r_+/2$. For $r_->r_+$ the zero-area singularity is naked. The limiting case $r_-=r_+$ is singular and must not be treated as a regular nondegenerate horizon.

= Causal structure of the magnetic dilaton black hole
{parent=Magnetically charged dilaton black hole}

For $r_-<r_+$, an ingoing chart with $v=t+r_*$ has metric $-Vdv^2+2dv\,dr+R^2d\Omega_2^2$. In its future interior, $\nabla r=\partial_v+V\partial_r$ is future timelike, so every future causal curve decreases $r$ and cannot reach <future null infinity>. The singularity $r=r_-$ is spacelike. A <Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates> extension has the same qualitative <Penrose diagram> as <Schwarzschild spacetime>, with two exterior regions, a future black-hole interior and a past <white hole> interior. The past interior has the same radial interval but is not a <black hole> region; specifying the radial interval alone does not identify the future component. For $r_->r_+$, the physical exterior $r>r_-$ has $V>0$ everywhere and outgoing null rays escape from arbitrarily near a timelike <naked singularity>.

= Geodesic potential of the magnetic dilaton black hole
{parent=Magnetically charged dilaton black hole}
{title2=$\widetilde V=\tfrac12[V(\epsilon+L^2/R^2)-E^2]$}

Rotate any <geodesic> into an equatorial plane of the <magnetically charged dilaton black hole>. Conserved energy and angular momentum are $E=V\dot t$ and $L=R^2\dot\phi$. Normalizing its tangent to $g(\dot x,\dot x)=-\epsilon$, with $\epsilon=1,0,-1$ for timelike, null and spacelike geodesics, gives $\dot r^2=E^2-V(\epsilon+L^2/R^2)$. Thus its radial equation is $\dot r^2/2+\widetilde V=0$. Radial null rays have $\dot r=\pm E$ and use the same <Schwarzschild tortoise coordinate> with $2M$ replaced by $r_+$.

= Komar mass of the magnetic dilaton black hole
{c}
{parent=Magnetically charged dilaton black hole}
{title2=$M_K(r)=r_+/2-P^2/r$}

For the <magnetically charged dilaton black hole>, the metric-dual one-form of $k=\partial_t$ is $k^\flat=-Vdt$. With orientation $dt\wedge dr\wedge d\theta\wedge d\phi$, its <Hodge star operator> gives $\star dk^\flat=-V'R^2\sin\theta\,d\theta\wedge d\phi$. Therefore the <Komar mass> is $R^2V'/2=r_+/2-P^2/r$. Its radial dependence is allowed because the <Ricci tensor> is nonzero; the divergence of $dk^\flat$ is sourced by $R_{ab}k^b$. At the horizon $M_K(r_+)=(r_+-r_-)/2=\kappa A_H/(4\pi)$.

= Trace-reversed Einstein field equations in D dimensions
{parent=Einstein field equations}
{c}

For $D>2$, tracing and substituting back gives
$$
R_{\mu\nu}
=\kappa\left(T_{\mu\nu}-\frac1{D-2}g_{\mu\nu}T\right).
$$

= Ricci curvature sourced by a Klein-Gordon scalar field
{parent=Trace-reversed Einstein field equations in D dimensions}
{c}

For the conserved scalar stress tensor above,
$$
R_{\mu\nu}
=\frac\kappa2\left[
\nabla_\mu\phi\nabla_\nu\phi
+\frac{m^2}{D-2}g_{\mu\nu}\phi^2
\right].
$$

= Metric variation
{parent=Einstein field equations}

For $h_{ab}=\delta g_{ab}$, variation of the inverse metric and metric volume form gives
$$
\delta g^{ab}=-h^{ab},
\qquad
\delta(d\operatorname{vol}_g)=\frac12g^{ab}h_{ab}\,d\operatorname{vol}_g.
$$
The <Levi-Civita connection> varies by
$$
\delta\Gamma^a{}_{bc}=\frac12g^{ad}
(\nabla_bh_{cd}+\nabla_ch_{bd}-\nabla_dh_{bc}).
$$

= Palatini identity
{c}
{parent=Metric variation}
{title2=$\delta R_{ab}=\nabla_c\delta\Gamma^c{}_{ab}-\nabla_b\delta\Gamma^c{}_{ac}$}

For the <Levi-Civita connection>, the difference of two connections is a tensor. Varying the coordinate expression for the <Ricci tensor> then gives the displayed covariant identity. It is the starting point for the <metric variation of scalar curvature>.

= Metric variation of scalar curvature
{parent=Metric variation}

For $h_{ab}=\delta g_{ab}$, the <Ricci scalar> varies as
$$
\delta R=-R^{ab}h_{ab}+\nabla_a\nabla_bh^{ab}-\nabla^a\nabla_ah,
\qquad h=g^{ab}h_{ab}.
$$
The last two terms form a <covariant divergence> and hence contribute only a boundary term to a compactly supported variation of the <Einstein-Hilbert action>.

= Modified gravity
{parent=Einstein field equations}
{wiki}

Modified gravity changes the Einstein-Hilbert action or its field equations in order to produce gravitational dynamics different from general relativity.

= f(R) gravity
{c}
{parent=Modified gravity}

An f(R) theory replaces the Ricci scalar in the gravitational action by a smooth function $f(R)$. Its metric field equation is
$$
f'(R)R_{ab}-\frac12f(R)g_{ab}
+(g_{ab}\Box-\nabla_a\nabla_b)f'(R)=8\pi T_{ab}.
$$

= Einstein-frame representation of metric f(R) gravity
{c}
{parent=f(R) gravity}
{title2=$\widetilde g_{\mu\nu}=-f'g_{\mu\nu},\quad\phi=\sqrt{3/2}\log(-f')$}

In the mostly-minus convention with vacuum gravitational action proportional to $\int\sqrt{-g}f(R)$ and $f'<0$, the displayed <conformal transformation> turns its vacuum equation into $\widetilde G_{\mu\nu}=T_{\mu\nu}(\phi)$, with potential $V=(Rf'-f)/(2f'^2)$ and the <stress-energy tensor of a canonical scalar field>. The conformal Ricci formula cancels the second derivatives of $f'$ against the original field equation. Its remaining derivative tensor is $\partial_\mu\phi\partial_\nu\phi-\widetilde g_{\mu\nu}(\widetilde\nabla\phi)^2/2$. Expressing $V$ as a function of $\phi$ requires a locally invertible $R\mapsto f'(R)$. When $f''=0$, this scalar-tensor parametrization degenerates and introduces no new propagating scalar degree of freedom.

= Einstein metric condition in f(R) gravity
{c}
{parent=f(R) gravity}
{title2=$f(4\Lambda)=2\Lambda f'(4\Lambda)$}

Every four-dimensional Einstein metric with $R_{ab}=\Lambda g_{ab}$ has constant <Ricci scalar> $R=4\Lambda$. The vacuum metric <f(R) gravity> tensor reduces to $(\Lambda f'(4\Lambda)-f(4\Lambda)/2)g_{ab}$, giving the displayed necessary and sufficient condition for a fixed <cosmological constant>. Both function and derivative may vanish, so division by $f'$ is unnecessary. Requiring this for all real $\Lambda$ and a smooth $f$ instead yields $f(R)=CR^2$.

= Normalization of the metric f(R) field equation
{parent=f(R) gravity}
{title2=$A E_{ab}=\frac12T_{ab}$}

For a metric action $S_g=A\int\sqrt{-g}f(R)\,d^4x$ and the usual <stress-energy tensor> convention, covariant metric variation gives $A E_{ab}=T_{ab}/2$, where $E_{ab}=f'R_{ab}-fg_{ab}/2+(g_{ab}\Box-\nabla_a\nabla_b)f'$. With $A=1/(16\pi G)$ this becomes $E_{ab}=8\pi G T_{ab}$; with $A=1$ it becomes $E_{ab}=T_{ab}/2$. The chosen normalization does not alter the vacuum equation.

= Linearized f(R) gravity about Minkowski spacetime
{c}
{parent=f(R) gravity}

If $f(0)=0$, $f'(0)=1$, and $f''(0)=0$, then $f(R)=R+O(R^3)$ near zero. The field equations linearized about <Minkowski spacetime> are therefore the <linearized Einstein equations>; deviations first appear beyond linear order.

= Linearized gravity
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

= Metric perturbation
{synonym}

Linearized gravity writes $g_{\mu\nu}=\eta_{\mu\nu}+h_{\mu\nu}$ with $|h_{\mu\nu}|\ll1$ and discards terms quadratic in $h$ and its derivatives.

= Gauge-invariant scalar-vector-tensor perturbations of Minkowski spacetime
{parent=Linearized gravity}
{title2=$\Phi=A-\dot B+\ddot E,\quad\Psi=C-\Delta E/3,\quad V_i=B_i-\dot E_i$}

= Gauge-invariant Minkowski perturbation
{synonym}

For signature $(+---)$, decompose a <metric perturbation> into $h_{00}=2A$, $h_{0i}=\partial_iB+B_i$ and $h_{ij}=2C\delta_{ij}+2(\partial_i\partial_j-\delta_{ij}\Delta/3)E+\partial_iE_j+\partial_jE_i+h_{ij}^{\rm TT}$, where the spatial vectors are divergence-free and $h^{\rm TT}$ is a <transverse-traceless tensor>. Under a passive coordinate shift $\xi^0=\tau$, $\xi^i=\partial_iL+L_i$, with $\partial_iL_i=0$, the changes are $\delta A=-\dot\tau$, $\delta B=\dot L-\tau$, $\delta C=\Delta L/3$, $\delta E=L$, $\delta B_i=\dot L_i$, and $\delta E_i=L_i$. The displayed combinations and $h^{\rm TT}$ are therefore gauge invariant. The decomposition is unique after appropriate spatial decay or mode restrictions; homogeneous and boundary modes require separate conventions.

= Vacuum constraints for gauge-invariant Minkowski perturbations
{parent=Gauge-invariant scalar-vector-tensor perturbations of Minkowski spacetime}
{title2=$\Delta\Psi=0,\quad\Delta V_i=0,\quad\Delta\Phi=0,\quad\Box h_{ij}^{\rm TT}=0$}

With the reversed convention in <curvature-sign convention in Killing derivative identities>, choose the representative $h_{00}=2\Phi$, $h_{0i}=V_i$, $h_{ij}=2\Psi\delta_{ij}+h_{ij}^{\rm TT}$. The linearized Einstein components are $G_{00}^{(1)}=-2\Delta\Psi$, $G_{0i}^{(1)}=-2\partial_i\dot\Psi-\Delta V_i/2$ and $G_{ij}^{(1)}=(\partial_i\partial_j-\delta_{ij}\Delta)(\Phi-\Psi)-2\delta_{ij}\ddot\Psi-(\partial_i\dot V_j+\partial_j\dot V_i)/2+\Box h_{ij}^{\rm TT}/2$. On all space with regular decaying fields, or for nonzero spatial Fourier modes, the vacuum constraints remove the scalar and vector parts, leaving two radiative <gravitational wave polarizations>. Singular Coulomb fields, spatial boundaries and homogeneous modes are not covered by that removal argument.

= Static scalar perturbation of Minkowski spacetime
{parent=Linearized gravity}
{title2=$ds^2=(1+2A)dt^2-(1+2C)\delta_{ij}dx^idx^j$}

For static scalar perturbations with no mixed time-space or spatial shear terms, the displayed <metric tensor> uses signature $(+---)$. With the reversed convention in <curvature-sign convention in Killing derivative identities>, its first-order <Einstein tensor> is $\delta G_{00}=2\Delta C$, $\delta G_{0i}=0$, and $\delta G_{ij}=\partial_i\partial_j(A+C)-\delta_{ij}\Delta(A+C)$. These follow by substituting the metric into the first-order <Christoffel symbols> and <Ricci tensor>, discarding products of perturbations. A static source with the <dust stress-energy tensor> obeys $\Delta C=-4\pi G\rho$ and the decaying spatial equation makes $A=-C$. For a point mass the <Newtonian potential of a point mass> gives $A=-GM/r$, $C=GM/r$. The spatial potential is necessary to determine light propagation; a time potential alone is insufficient.

= Linearized Schwarzschild metric
{parent=Linearized gravity}
{title2=$ds^2=(1-r_S/r)dt^2-(1+r_S/r)dr^2-r^2d\Omega^2$}

For a static spherical point source, the weak-field solution in isotropic radius $\rho$ has $g_{tt}=1-2GM/\rho$ and spatial metric $-(1+2GM/\rho)(d\rho^2+\rho^2d\Omega^2)$. The <isotropic-to-areal weak-field radius change> gives the displayed metric with $r_S=2GM$. Its validity requires $r_S/r\ll1$. Treating it as an exact metric near $r\sim r_S$ can turn omitted terms into large corrections; it does not establish horizons or strong-field photon dynamics.

= Weak-field photon-orbit validity
{parent=Linearized Schwarzschild metric}
{title2=$u=r_S/r\ll1$}

The first-order null-orbit equation $(u')^2=r_S^2E^2/h^2-u^2(1-u)$ formally has a circular solution at $u=2/3$, with $|h/E|=3\sqrt3r_S/2$. Its second-order equation gives a perturbation $\delta u''=\delta u$, hence instability. However, $u=2/3$ is outside the weak-field expansion. The exact <Schwarzschild spacetime> has a corresponding unstable <photon sphere>, but that physical result needs the exact geometry; extrapolating the linearized metric is not a controlled proof.

= Isotropic-to-areal weak-field radius change
{parent=Linearized Schwarzschild metric}
{title2=$r=\rho+GM+O(G^2M^2/\rho)$}

The area of a symmetry sphere in the weak-field isotropic metric is $4\pi\rho^2(1+2GM/\rho)$. Equating it to $4\pi r^2$ defines the <areal radius> and gives the displayed change. To first order $dr=d\rho$, and replacing $1/\rho$ by $1/r$ changes the perturbation only at second order. This explains why the isotropic angular correction disappears in the <linearized Schwarzschild metric> without discarding spatial curvature.

= Quadratic gravitational effective stress-energy tensor
{parent=Linearized gravity}
{title2=$t_{\mu\nu}=-G^{(2)}_{\mu\nu}[h,h]/(8\pi)$}

= Quadratic gravitational stress-energy
{synonym}

Expand the <Einstein tensor> about the <Minkowski metric>. The quadratic term in the <metric perturbation> defines $t_{\mu\nu}=-G^{(2)}_{\mu\nu}[h,h]/(8\pi)$, a symmetric tensor relative to the background. For a first-order vacuum solution, the expanded <contracted Bianchi identity> gives $\partial^\mu t_{\mu\nu}=0$. Equivalently, a second-order correction $j$ obeys $G^{(1)}[j]=8\pi t$, whose flat divergence vanishes identically. This conservation is an on-shell statement. The local $t$ depends on perturbative gauge and the background split; conservation alone does not make it a gauge-independent local gravitational <stress-energy tensor>.

= Massless Fierz-Pauli action
{parent=Linearized gravity}
{title2=$\delta S=-\int d^4x\,G^{(1)}_{\alpha\beta}\delta h^{\alpha\beta}$}

A <massless Fierz-Pauli action> is the quadratic flat-background action for a symmetric metric perturbation that yields the vacuum <linearized Einstein equations>. One normalization, up to a total derivative, is
$$
\mathcal L=-\frac14\partial_\mu h^{\rho\sigma}\partial^\mu h_{\rho\sigma}
+\frac12A_\mu A^\mu-\frac12A^\mu\partial_\mu h
+\frac14\partial_\mu h\partial^\mu h,\qquad A_\mu=\partial_\nu h^\nu{}_\mu.
$$
Varying the symmetric field with compact support gives the displayed linearized <Einstein tensor> coefficient. Its linearized coordinate-gauge symmetry is consistent with the contracted <Bianchi identity>. It is the massless gravitational action, distinct from imposing the massive <Fierz-Pauli equations> with five polarizations.

= Linearized coordinate gauge transformation
{parent=Linearized gravity}
{title2=$h_{\mu\nu}\mapsto h_{\mu\nu}-\partial_\mu\xi_\nu-\partial_\nu\xi_\mu$}

An infinitesimal coordinate change generated by $\xi$ changes a metric perturbation about <Minkowski spacetime> by $h_{\mu\nu}\mapsto h_{\mu\nu}-\partial_\mu\xi_\nu-\partial_\nu\xi_\mu$, in the passive sign convention. For a passive coordinate change $x^\mu\mapsto x^\mu+\xi^\mu$, the first-order change is minus the <Lie derivative of a tensor field> of the background metric, equivalently the pullback by the inverse flow. The <gauge invariance of the linearized Riemann tensor> follows because the added terms have commuting third derivatives, which cancel in the curvature antisymmetrizations. Gauge choices alter the metric components while preserving linearized tidal effects.

= Affine transverse trace in a diagonal linearized plane wave is pure gauge
{parent=Linearized coordinate gauge transformation}

For a perturbation $h_{xx}=2a(t-z)$, $h_{yy}=2b(t-z)$ with all other components zero, the linearized <vacuum Einstein equations> give $(a+b)''=0$. Put $s=a+b$ and choose $\xi_x=sx/2$, $\xi_y=sy/2$, $\xi_t=-s'(x^2+y^2)/4$, $\xi_z=s'(x^2+y^2)/4$. Under the <linearized coordinate gauge transformation>, the mixed time/transverse and longitudinal/transverse components cancel, while the other time/longitudinal changes are proportional to $s''=0$. The remaining components are $h_{xx}=a-b$, $h_{yy}=-(a-b)$, $h_{xy}=0$, a <transverse-traceless gauge> perturbation of <plus polarization>. This explicitly removes a nonzero affine trace without assuming it vanishes by boundary conditions. If $(a-b)''=0$ too, all linearized curvature vanishes.

= Weak-field approximation
{parent=Linearized gravity}

The weak-field approximation requires every dimensionless component of the <metric perturbation> to have magnitude much smaller than one, so terms quadratic in the perturbation may be neglected.

= Linearized Einstein equations
{c}
{parent=Linearized gravity}

In terms of the <trace-reversed metric perturbation> and in the <Lorenz gauge in linearized gravity>, the linearized Einstein equations on Minkowski spacetime are
$$
\Box\bar h_{\mu\nu}=-16\pi T_{\mu\nu}.
$$

= Parallel photon beams in linearized gravity
{parent=Linearized Einstein equations}
{title2=$h_{ik}=H(u,y,z)\ell_i\ell_k,\quad\Delta_\perp H=16\pi G\rho(u)$}

For signature $(+---)$ and the curvature convention giving $\Box\bar h_{ik}=-16\pi G\epsilon^{-1}T_{ik}$, take $u=t-x$, $\ell_i=(1,-1,0,0)$ and $T_{ik}=\epsilon\rho(u)\ell_i\ell_k$. A trace-free Lorenz-gauge particular solution has the displayed form. The vector $\ell^i=(1,1,0,0)$ is null, and $\Gamma^i{}_{jk}\ell^j\ell^k=0$, so co-propagating test photons are undeflected by this beam field. For a transversely uniform source, $H=4\pi G\rho(u)(y^2+z^2)$ is one particular solution. A function of $u$ alone has vanishing wave operator and cannot source nonzero $\rho$; homogeneous gravitational perturbations and boundary conditions remain separate data.

= Radiation boundary condition for linearized gravity
{parent=Linearized Einstein equations}

The retarded source solution of the <linearized Einstein equations> is selected by excluding added incoming or freely specified homogeneous <gravitational waves>. A homogeneous wave can satisfy <Lorenz gauge in linearized gravity> while having no source <mass quadrupole moment>. Thus a formula for the retarded source field is not an identity for every solution of the sourced wave equation.

= Retarded quadrupole field
{parent=Linearized Einstein equations}
{title2=$\bar h_{ij}(t,\mathbf x)\simeq 2\ddot I_{ij}(t-r)/r$}

For a compact slowly moving source in the <weak-field approximation>, with no incoming homogeneous wave and $G=c=1$, the retarded <linearized Einstein equations> give this leading far-zone <trace-reversed metric perturbation>. The key consequence of <stress-energy conservation> is $\ddot I_{ij}=2\int T_{ij}\,d^3x$. The physical <gravitational wave> is obtained by the <transverse-traceless projector>.

= Long-wavelength source approximation
{parent=Retarded quadrupole field}
{title2=$d/\tau\ll1$}

Dropping the position-dependent source delay in the leading far-zone retarded field requires source size $d$ to be small compared with its variation time $\tau$, in units $c=1$. The <retarded quadrupole field> also requires $d/r\ll1$. For a typical nonrelativistic bound source, $\tau\sim d/v$ implies this long-wavelength condition; an arbitrarily rapidly oscillating small displacement is not controlled merely by its instantaneous speed.

= Linearized inverse metric
{parent=Linearized gravity}

If $g_{\mu\nu}=\eta_{\mu\nu}+h_{\mu\nu}$, then $g^{\mu\nu}=\eta^{\mu\nu}-h^{\mu\nu}+O(h^2)$, with indices on $h$ raised using the background metric.

= Linearized Levi-Civita connection
{parent=Linearized gravity}

About a Cartesian flat background,
$$
\Gamma^\alpha_{\beta\gamma}
=\frac12\eta^{\alpha\rho}
(\partial_\beta h_{\gamma\rho}+\partial_\gamma h_{\beta\rho}-\partial_\rho h_{\beta\gamma})+O(h^2).
$$

= Linearized Riemann curvature operator
{parent=Linearized gravity}

The linearized Riemann tensor defines an operator on two-forms by
$$
(\mathcal R\omega)_{ab}=\frac12R^{(1)}_{ab}{}^{cd}\omega_{cd}.
$$
Unlike the metric perturbation itself, this operator is invariant under linearized gauge transformations.

= Gauge invariance of the linearized Riemann tensor
{parent=Linearized Riemann curvature operator}

Under $h_{\mu\nu}\mapsto h_{\mu\nu}+\partial_\mu\xi_\nu+\partial_\nu\xi_\mu$, every contribution to the linearized Riemann tensor contains three commuting derivatives of $\xi$ and cancels pairwise. Linearized curvature is therefore gauge invariant about flat spacetime.

= Trace-reversed metric perturbation
{title2=$\bar h_{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Linearized gravity}

In four dimensions, the trace-reversed perturbation is
$$
\bar h_{\mu\nu}=h_{\mu\nu}-\frac12\eta_{\mu\nu}h,
\qquad h=\eta^{\rho\sigma}h_{\rho\sigma}.
$$
Trace reversal simplifies the linearized Einstein equations.

= Lorenz gauge in linearized gravity
{c}
{parent=Trace-reversed metric perturbation}

The Lorenz condition $\partial^\mu\bar h_{\mu\nu}=0$ reduces the vacuum field equation to $\Box\bar h_{\mu\nu}=0$. A plane-wave amplitude therefore satisfies $k^\mu H_{\mu\nu}=0$ and $k^\mu k_\mu=0$.

= Residual gauge symmetry of linearized gravity
{parent=Lorenz gauge in linearized gravity}

An infinitesimal coordinate change generated by $\xi_\mu$ changes
$$
\bar h_{\mu\nu}\mapsto
\bar h_{\mu\nu}-\partial_\mu\xi_\nu-\partial_\nu\xi_\mu
+\eta_{\mu\nu}\partial_\rho\xi^\rho.
$$
It preserves Lorenz gauge exactly when $\Box\xi_\mu=0$.

= Plane gravitational wave in linearized gravity
{parent=Lorenz gauge in linearized gravity}

= Plane gravitational waves in linearized gravity
{synonym}

A vacuum mode $\bar h_{\mu\nu}=H_{\mu\nu}e^{ik\cdot x}$ has a null wavevector and a transverse symmetric amplitude. Residual gauge freedom can further reduce it to two physical transverse-traceless polarizations.

= Transverse-traceless gauge
{title2=TT gauge}
{parent=Plane gravitational wave in linearized gravity}
{wiki}

Transverse-traceless gauge sets the time and longitudinal components and the spatial trace of a vacuum gravitational-wave perturbation to zero.

= Explicit plane-wave reduction to transverse-traceless gauge
{parent=Transverse-traceless gauge}
{title2=$H_+=(H_{11}-H_{22})/2,\quad H_\times=H_{12}$}

For a <plane gravitational wave in linearized gravity> with $k_\mu=(-\omega,0,0,\omega)$, <Lorenz gauge in linearized gravity> gives $H_{3\nu}=-H_{0\nu}$. Under $\delta H_{\mu\nu}=i(k_\mu X_\nu+k_\nu X_\mu-\eta_{\mu\nu}k^\rho X_\rho)$, choose $X_i=H_{0i}/(i\omega)$ for $i=1,2$, $X_3-X_0=iH_{00}/\omega$, and $X_0+X_3=(H_{11}+H_{22})/(2i\omega)$. This <residual gauge symmetry of linearized gravity> removes all time and longitudinal entries and the transverse trace. The remaining amplitudes $H_+,H_\times$ are the two <gravitational wave polarizations>. The formula uses the plus-sign convention for $\delta h=2\partial_{(\mu}\xi_{\nu)}$; reversing the definition of $\xi$ reverses the gauge change.

= TT coordinate separation and measured separation
{c}
{parent=Transverse-traceless gauge}
{title2=$\xi^{\hat A}=\xi_{\rm coord}^A+\tfrac12h_{AB}L^B$}

In <transverse-traceless gauge>, particles initially at rest have fixed spatial coordinates because $\Gamma^\mu{}_{00}=0$. Their separation measured in a <parallel-propagated orthonormal frame> changes: to first order $S^{\hat A}=S^A+h_{AB}S^B/2$. Hence the physical <geodesic deviation> is $\ddot\xi^{\hat A}=\ddot h_{AB}L^B/2$, whereas the coordinate displacement obeys $\ddot\xi^A_{\rm coord}=0$. The extra $\partial_0\Gamma^A{}_{0B}L^B$ term in the coordinate expansion of the second <covariant derivative> reconciles the two results. This applies to infinitesimal separations and linear <metric perturbations>.

= Sliding-bead gravitational wave detector
{parent=TT coordinate separation and measured separation}
{title2=$\ddot L=\tfrac12\epsilon\ddot h_{nn}L_0+O(\epsilon^2)$}

A short rigid nonrotating guide constrains two freely sliding beads to one axis $n$. In its <parallel-propagated orthonormal frame>, the axial <geodesic deviation> is $\ddot L=-R_{n0n0}L$. For a transverse <gravitational wave>, $R_{n0n0}=-\epsilon\ddot h_{nn}/2$, so a wave incident after the beads are at rest changes the measured separation by $\epsilon h_{nn}L_0/2$ to first order. Transverse reaction forces do not supply an axial restoring force. Alignment with the propagation direction gives no first-order length change.

= Gravitational wave polarization
{parent=Transverse-traceless gauge}
{wiki}

A gravitational wave in four-dimensional general relativity has two independent linear polarizations, conventionally called plus and cross. A rotation by angle $\vartheta$ in the transverse plane rotates their pair by $2\vartheta$.

= Transverse-traceless tensor
{parent=Gravitational wave polarization}
{title2=$k_ih_{ij}=0,\quad h_{ii}=0$}

A transverse-traceless spatial tensor is orthogonal to its <Fourier mode> wavevector in each index and has zero trace. The <transverse-traceless projector> extracts this part. Trace terms and symmetrized coordinate-displacement gradients have no such part, explaining the linear gauge invariance of <tensor cosmological perturbations>.

= Plus polarization
{title2=$+$ polarization}
{parent=Gravitational wave polarization}

A <gravitational wave> of plus polarization has opposite transverse strain or <geodesic deviation> <eigenvalues> along two fixed perpendicular axes. For a sinusoidal <periodic function> profile, the stretching and compressing roles alternate every half-period.

= Cross polarization
{title2=$\times$ polarization}
{parent=Gravitational wave polarization}

The cross polarization is the plus pattern rotated through $45$ degrees in the transverse plane.

= Transverse-traceless projector
{title2=$\Pi_{ij,mn}^{\rm TT}$}
{parent=Gravitational wave polarization}

The transverse-traceless projector maps a symmetric tensor to the part orthogonal to a chosen wavevector and with zero trace. It is the identity on physical gravitational-wave polarizations.

= Polarization completeness relation
{parent=Transverse-traceless projector}

A polarization completeness relation expresses a projector as a sum of outer products of an orthonormal polarization basis. For transverse-traceless tensors, $\sum_s\epsilon_{ij}^s\epsilon_{mn}^{s*}$ is the transverse-traceless projector up to the chosen normalization.

= Linearized Ricci tensor and scalar
{parent=Linearized gravity}

To first order around flat spacetime,
$$
R_{\mu\nu}
=\frac12\left(
\partial_\rho\partial_\mu h^\rho{}_{\nu}
+\partial_\rho\partial_\nu h^\rho{}_{\mu}
-\mathop{\Box}h_{\mu\nu}
-\partial_\mu\partial_\nu h
\right),
$$
and
$$
R=\partial_\mu\partial_\nu h^{\mu\nu}-\mathop{\Box}h,
\qquad h=\eta^{\mu\nu}h_{\mu\nu}.
$$

= Newtonian limit
{parent=Linearized gravity}
{c}
{wiki}

= Newtonian limit of general relativity
{c}
{synonym}

For a weak, static field produced by nonrelativistic matter, $T_{00}=\rho$ is the dominant stress-energy component. The field equations and the slow-particle <geodesic equation> imply
$$
h_{00}=-2\Phi,
\qquad
\kappa=8\pi G,
$$
where the <Poisson equation> is $\nabla^2\Phi=4\pi G\rho$.

= Newtonian connection from an exponential lapse
{c}
{parent=Newtonian limit}
{title2=$\Gamma^i{}_{tt}=\partial_i\phi$}

For smooth spatial $\phi$ and $g(c)=-c^2e^{2\phi/c^2}dt^2+\delta_{ij}dx^idx^j$, the only nonzero <Christoffel symbols> are $\Gamma^t{}_{ti}=\Gamma^t{}_{it}=c^{-2}\partial_i\phi$ and $\Gamma^i{}_{tt}=e^{2\phi/c^2}\partial_i\phi$. Consequently the <Levi-Civita connection> converges smoothly on compact sets as $c\to\infty$ although the metric itself diverges in these coordinates. The limiting <torsion-free connection> has only $\Gamma^i{}_{tt}=\partial_i\phi$ and gives $d^2x^i/dt^2=-\partial_i\phi$ when $t$ is an <affine parameter>. With $R^a{}_{bcd}=\partial_c\Gamma^a{}_{db}-\partial_d\Gamma^a{}_{cb}+\cdots$, its <Ricci tensor> is $(\Delta\phi)dt\otimes dt$: quadratic connection terms vanish and $R^i{}_{tjt}=\partial_j\partial_i\phi$. Thus zero Ricci tensor is the <Laplace equation>, while full flatness would require the entire Hessian to vanish.

= Static radius
{title2=$r_{\rm s}=(3GM/\Lambda)^{1/3}$}
{parent=Newtonian limit}

For a point mass in a positive <cosmological constant>, the inward Newtonian acceleration $GM/r^2$ balances the outward cosmological acceleration $\Lambda r/3$ at the static radius $r_{\rm s}=(3GM/\Lambda)^{1/3}$.

= Linearized point-mass metric in radial Cartesian form
{parent=Newtonian limit}

For a point mass and $r>0$, write
$$
h_{00}=\frac{2GM}{r},
\qquad
h_{ij}=f(r)x_i x_j.
$$
The vacuum scalar-curvature equation gives $rf'+3f=0$, so $f=C/r^3$. The remaining vacuum equations fix $C=2GM$ in the corresponding Schwarzschild gauge.

= Slowly rotating weak-field metric
{parent=Linearized gravity}

Far from a slowly rotating isolated body of mass $m$ and angular momentum $\mathbf J$, the scalar part of the metric is the linearized point-mass field while $g_{0i}$ is the dipole field of $\mathbf J$. For rotation about the z-axis, the cross term is $4Jr^{-3}(y\,dx-x\,dy)dt$ in a common convention.

= Curvature singularity
{parent=General relativity}

A curvature singularity is an incomplete spacetime boundary approached by curves along which a scalar curvature invariant or a component of the <Riemann curvature tensor> in a parallel-propagated frame becomes unbounded. Such divergence cannot be removed by changing coordinates.

= Schwarzschild spacetime
{parent=General relativity}
{c}

= Schwarzschild metric
{synonym}
{c}

= Schwarzschild black hole
{synonym}
{c}

In units $G=c=1$, the Schwarzschild exterior metric is
$$
ds^2=-\left(1-\frac{2M}{r}\right)dt^2
+\left(1-\frac{2M}{r}\right)^{-1}dr^2+r^2d\Omega^2.
$$

= Schwarzschild radial fall from rest at a finite radius
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{title2=$r=\tfrac{r_0}2(1+\cos\eta),\quad s=\sqrt{r_0^3/(8M)}(\eta+\sin\eta)$}

For radial timelike <geodesic> fall released from rest at $r_0>2M$, the <Killing energy> is $E=\sqrt{1-2M/r_0}$ and the <proper time> radial equation is $(dr/ds)^2=2M/r-2M/r_0$. The displayed parameterization integrates its inward branch, with $s=0$ at release. The <Schwarzschild event horizon> is reached at $\eta_H=\arccos(4M/r_0-1)$ in finite proper time, even though <Schwarzschild time> diverges.

// Target: general-relativity.bigb

= Finite pulse count for a falling Schwarzschild beacon
{parent=Schwarzschild radial fall from rest at a finite radius}
{title2=$N=\left\lceil(s_H-\delta)/P\right\rceil$}

A freely falling source emits <optical pulses> at proper times $\delta+nP$, for integers $n\ge0$. If its horizon-crossing <proper time> is finite, only pulses beginning before $s_H$ can escape. Their number is the displayed ceiling, for $0\le\delta<P<s_H$. Infinite <Schwarzschild time> at crossing does not give infinitely many emitted pulses. The pulse phase $\delta$ fixes both the count and the redshift of the last pulse. If a finite pulse straddles the horizon, its exterior portion has an arbitrarily late, increasingly redshifted received tail.

= Proper-time bound inside a Schwarzschild black hole
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{title2=$\Delta s\leq2M\arcsin\sqrt{r_0/(2M)}-\sqrt{r_0(2M-r_0)}<\pi M$}

Inside the future black-hole region, the radius decreases along every future-directed timelike path. Its unit tangent obeys $|dr/ds|\geq\sqrt{2M/r-1}$ from the metric normalization, independently of radial acceleration or angular motion. Integrating from $r_0<2M$ to zero gives the displayed finite upper bound. The statement concerns future continuation within the black-hole region; reversing time in the white-hole region reverses the radial direction.

= Horizon redshift of a Schwarzschild infaller
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}

For smooth radial infall through a future <Schwarzschild event horizon>, outgoing retarded time obeys $u\sim-4M\log(\tau_H-\tau)$. A distant observer sees the <redshift> grow as $e^{t_{\rm rec}/(4M)}$. Its exponent in emission <Schwarzschild time> is instead $1/(2M)$, because $t_{\rm rec}\sim2t_{\rm em}$. At finite fixed observer radius the wavelength ratio includes $\sqrt{1-2M/r_B}$.

= Schwarzschild time
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{c}
{title2=$t$}

<Schwarzschild time> $t$ is the exterior time coordinate normalized to the <proper time> of static observers at infinity. At fixed exterior radius, $d\tau=\sqrt{1-2M/r}\,dt$. A received signal and its emission occur at different values of this coordinate.

= Kruskal spacetime
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{c}

The <Kruskal spacetime> is the maximal analytic extension of the positive-mass vacuum <Schwarzschild spacetime>. It contains two exterior regions, a <black hole>, and a <white hole>. <Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates> remove both horizon coordinate singularities, while $r=0$ remains a <curvature singularity>.

= Non-traversability of the Schwarzschild bridge
{parent=Kruskal spacetime}

In <Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates>, every future-directed radial causal curve has nondecreasing $U$ and $V$. The right exterior has $U<0,V>0$, while the left has $U>0,V<0$. No future-directed causal curve can travel between the exteriors because it would have to decrease one null coordinate. The spatial bridge on a time-symmetric slice is therefore not a traversable passage.

= Kruskal diagram
{parent=Kruskal spacetime}
{c}
{title2=$T_K=(V_K+U_K)/2,\quad X_K=(V_K-U_K)/2$}

This radial spacetime diagram uses <Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates> to display both exteriors, the black hole and the white hole of maximally extended <Schwarzschild spacetime>. Each point represents a symmetry two-sphere. Radial <null geodesics> have slopes $\pm1$ in the displayed coordinates; the horizons are $T_K=\pm X_K$ and the singularities satisfy $T_K^2-X_K^2=1$ in the usual normalization. A further compactification gives the corresponding <Penrose diagram>.

= Radial timelike incompleteness in Kruskal spacetime
{parent=Kruskal spacetime}

Every maximal radial <timelike geodesic> in positive-mass <Kruskal spacetime> is incomplete in at least one direction. Its equations are $\dot r^2=E^2-1+2M/r$ and $\ddot r=-M/r^2$. A finite turning point is a maximum, and each trajectory reaches $r=0$ at one or both ends. The remaining <proper time> is finite because $d\tau\sim\sqrt{r/(2M)}\,dr$. Complete circular timelike geodesics exist, but they have nonzero <angular momentum>.

= Negative heat capacity of a Schwarzschild black hole
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{title2=$C=-8\pi M^2$}

In units $G=\hbar=c=k_B=1$, a <Schwarzschild black hole> has <Hawking temperature> $T=1/(8\pi M)$, so its <heat capacity> is $dM/dT=-8\pi M^2$. It heats up as it loses <mass>, preventing stable canonical equilibrium with an unlimited thermal reservoir.

= Ultrarelativistic limit of the boosted Schwarzschild metric away from the null plane
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}

Boost a Schwarzschild mass with speed $v$ and hold $p=m\gamma$ fixed as $v\to1$. Away from the null plane $x=t$, the isotropic-coordinate expression tends to
$$
ds^2=ds_{\rm Minkowski}^2+\frac{4p}{|x-t|}(dt-dx)^2.
$$
The complete distributional limit on the null plane requires the additional coordinate treatment that produces the Aichelburg-Sexl shock wave.

= Schwarzschild-de Sitter spacetime
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{wiki}

= Kottler spacetime
{c}
{synonym}

The Schwarzschild-de Sitter spacetime is the spherically symmetric vacuum solution with positive <cosmological constant>. In static coordinates its metric function is $f(r)=1-2m/r-H^2r^2$, with a black-hole horizon and a cosmological horizon when $mH<1/\sqrt{27}$.

= Geodetic effect
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{wiki}

= Geodetic precession
{synonym}

Geodetic precession is the rotation of a parallel-transported gyroscope relative to the orbital radial direction in curved spacetime. For a circular Schwarzschild orbit its exact precession per revolution is $2\pi[1-\sqrt{1-3M/R}]$.

= Painlevé–Gullstrand coordinates
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{wiki}

Painlevé–Gullstrand coordinates write the Schwarzschild metric as
$$
ds^2=-dt^2+\left(dr+\sqrt{\frac{2M}{r}}\,dt\right)^2+r^2d\Omega^2.
$$
The constant-$t$ spatial slices are flat, and the coordinates remain regular at the <Schwarzschild radius>.

= Domain of the ingoing Painleve-Gullstrand chart
{parent=Painlevé–Gullstrand coordinates}
{title2=$V>0,\quad UV<1$}

Choose <Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates> with the right exterior at $U<0,V>0$ and $UV=(1-r/(2M))e^{r/(2M)}$. The ingoing <Painlevé–Gullstrand coordinates> cover that exterior and the future black-hole region $U>0,V>0$, together with their common future horizon. They exclude the other exterior, the white hole, the bifurcation sphere and the singularity. Indeed $\tau=v+F(r)-r_*(r)$, with $v=4M\log V$ and $F'=\sqrt{2M/r}/f$; the difference $F-r_*$ is finite at $r=2M$, whereas $V=0$ requires infinite coordinate time.

= Free-fall proper time in Schwarzschild spacetime
{parent=Painlevé–Gullstrand coordinates}
{title2=$d\tau=dt+\frac{\sqrt{2M/r}}{1-2M/r}dr$}

The inward radial <geodesic congruence> with unit <Killing energy> has $U=f^{-1}\partial_t-\sqrt{2M/r}\,\partial_r$, where $f=1-2M/r$. Its metric dual is $U^\flat=-d\tau$, so $\tau$ is a synchronized <proper time> for the congruence. Locally $\tau=t+2\sqrt{2Mr}+2M\log| (\sqrt r-\sqrt{2M})/(\sqrt r+\sqrt{2M}) |$. This yields <Painlevé–Gullstrand coordinates> with flat spatial slices and a regular future horizon.

= Schwarzschild horizon-to-singularity proper time for fall from infinity
{parent=Free-fall proper time in Schwarzschild spacetime}
{c}
{title2=$\Delta\tau=4M/3$}

A radial observer released from rest at infinity has $dr/d\tau=-\sqrt{2M/r}$. Integration from $r=2M$ to $r=0$ gives $\Delta\tau=\int_0^{2M}\sqrt{r/(2M)}dr=4M/3$ in geometric units. In ordinary units it is $4GM/(3c^3)$. The endpoint is the curvature singularity, not a regular spacetime centre; this value belongs to that specified energy, not every possible infalling worldline.

= Orthonormal coframe in Painlevé–Gullstrand coordinates
{parent=Painlevé–Gullstrand coordinates}

An orthonormal coframe is
$$
e^0=dt,
\qquad e^1=dr+\sqrt{\frac{2M}{r}}\,dt,
\qquad e^2=r\,d\theta,
\qquad e^3=r\sin\theta\,d\phi.
$$

= Radial Weyl curvature of Schwarzschild spacetime
{title2=$C_{trtr}=-2M/r^3$}
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}

With the curvature convention
$$
R^\alpha{}_{\beta\gamma\delta}
=\partial_\gamma\Gamma^\alpha_{\beta\delta}
-\partial_\delta\Gamma^\alpha_{\beta\gamma}+\cdots,
$$
the vacuum Schwarzschild metric satisfies
$$
C_{trtr}=R_{trtr}=-\frac{2M}{r^3}.
$$
The opposite Riemann-sign convention reverses this sign.

= Schwarzschild tidal force
{parent=Radial Weyl curvature of Schwarzschild spacetime}

The orthonormal-frame components of Schwarzschild curvature scale as $M/r^3$. The <geodesic deviation> equation therefore gives radial stretching and transverse compression whose magnitude diverges as $r\to0$.

= Tidal gravity and the equivalence principle
{parent=Radial Weyl curvature of Schwarzschild spacetime}

Weyl curvature produces relative acceleration between neighbouring freely falling trajectories and therefore tidal deformation. A freely falling frame removes gravity at one event, as required by the equivalence principle, but cannot remove curvature over an extended region.

= Equatorial null orbit in Schwarzschild spacetime
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}

For inverse radius $y=1/r$ and azimuth $\phi$, a Schwarzschild null geodesic obeys
$$
(y')^2+y^2-2My^3=\frac1{b^2},
\qquad
y''+y=3My^2,
$$
where $b=L/E$ is the <impact parameter>.

= Critical photon orbits of Schwarzschild spacetime
{parent=Equatorial null orbit in Schwarzschild spacetime}
{title2=$u-\tfrac13=-4Ae^\phi/(1+2Ae^\phi)^2$}

At the critical <impact parameter> $b=3\sqrt3M$, the null orbit equation factors as $(u')^2=(u-1/3)^2(2u+1/3)$ for $u=M/r$. One oriented family has the displayed solution. As $\phi\to-\infty$, positive $A$ approaches the <photon sphere> from larger radii and negative $A$ from smaller radii; $A=0$ is the circular orbit. The physical domain must retain $r>0$ and the positive square root in its implicit parametrization.

= Circular null geodesic
{parent=Equatorial null orbit in Schwarzschild spacetime}

A circular null geodesic remains at constant areal radius. It lies at a stationary point of the null radial effective potential and may be stable or unstable according to the sign of the second derivative there.

= Photon sphere
{parent=Circular null geodesic}
{wiki}

A photon sphere is a spherical set of circular <null geodesics>. In <Schwarzschild spacetime> it occurs at $r=3M$ and is unstable.

= Photon capture cross-section of a Reissner-Nordstrom black hole
{parent=Photon sphere}
{title2=$\sigma=2\pi r_{\mathrm{ph}}^3/(r_{\mathrm{ph}}-M)$}

For a <Reissner-Nordstrom black hole> with $|Q|\le M$, the outer unstable <circular null geodesic> has radius $r_{\mathrm{ph}}=(3M+\sqrt{9M^2-8Q^2})/2$. Maximizing $f/r^2$ for $f=1-2M/r+Q^2/r^2$ gives the critical <impact parameter> $b_c^2=r_{\mathrm{ph}}^2/f(r_{\mathrm{ph}})=2r_{\mathrm{ph}}^3/(r_{\mathrm{ph}}-M)$. The <geometrical optics> capture cross-section is $\pi b_c^2$, reducing to $27\pi M^2$ in <Schwarzschild spacetime> and $16\pi M^2$ in the extremal charged case. Finite-wavelength absorption need not equal this geometric limit.

// Target: general-relativity.bigb

= Schwarzschild radius
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{c}
{wiki}

The Schwarzschild radius is $r_s=2GM/c^2$, or $2M$ in geometrized units.

= Schwarzschild tortoise coordinate
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{c}

The tortoise coordinate satisfies
$$
\frac{dr_*}{dr}=\left(1-\frac{2M}{r}\right)^{-1},
\qquad
r_*=r+2M\log\left|\frac{r}{2M}-1\right|.
$$

= Outgoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates
{parent=Schwarzschild tortoise coordinate}
{c}
{title2=$u=t-r_*$}

The retarded coordinate $u=t-r_*$ puts the radial <Schwarzschild metric> into $ds^2=-f\,du^2-2\,du\,dr+r^2d\Omega^2$, with $f=1-2M/r$. Outgoing radial <null geodesics> have constant $u$. These coordinates are regular at the past horizon; an infaller crossing the future horizon has $u\to+\infty$.

= Ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates
{parent=Schwarzschild tortoise coordinate}
{c}

The advanced coordinate $v=t+r_*$ puts the radial Schwarzschild metric into the form
$$
ds^2=-\left(1-\frac{2M}{r}\right)dv^2+2\,dv\,dr+r^2d\Omega^2,
$$
which is regular at the future horizon.

These are the advanced, ingoing member of the <Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates>.

= Finkelstein diagram
{parent=Ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates}
{c}

A <Finkelstein diagram> plots <areal radius> $r$ against $T=v-r$ in <ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates>. Its radial <null directions> show explicitly that both future light-ray families decrease $r$ inside a <Schwarzschild black hole>. It is not a compactified <Penrose diagram>.

= Radial null trajectories in ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates
{parent=Ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates}

Radial null curves comprise the ingoing family $v=\text{constant}$ and the outgoing family
$$
\frac{dr}{dv}=\frac12\left(1-\frac{2M}{r}\right).
$$
Outside the horizon the outgoing family moves to larger $r$; inside it both future-directed families move to smaller $r$.

= Schwarzschild event horizon
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{c}

The surface $r=2M$ is the future event horizon of Schwarzschild spacetime. Its singularity in Schwarzschild coordinates is removable, whereas $r=0$ is a curvature singularity.

= Schwarzschild horizon collision energy
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild event horizon}
{title2=$E_{\rm com}^2=m^2[4+(h_1-h_2)^2/(4M^2)]$}

For two equal-mass inward <timelike geodesics> of unit specific <Killing energy>, their invariant <center-of-mass energy> has this finite horizon limit. If both are captured from infinity, <Schwarzschild marginally bound capture> gives $E_{\rm com}<m\sqrt{20}$, with $m\sqrt{20}$ a supremum. No such universal bound follows for particles prepared inside the angular-momentum barrier.

= Schwarzschild singularity
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}

The Schwarzschild singularity is the spacelike curvature singularity at $r=0$. Curvature invariants and orthonormal tidal components diverge there.

= Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{wiki}

Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates extend the Schwarzschild solution smoothly through its future and past horizons. Constant Schwarzschild radius curves are hyperbolae, while the horizons are null lines through the bifurcation sphere.

= Kruskal extension across a simple static horizon
{c}
{parent=Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates}
{title2=$r_*=(2\kappa)^{-1}\log|r-r_H|+h(r)$}

For $ds^2=-fdt^2+dr^2/f+R^2d\Omega^2$, suppose $f$ and $R$ are analytic at $r_H$, $f(r_H)=0$, $f'(r_H)=2\kappa>0$ and $R(r_H)>0$. The <tortoise coordinate> obeys the displayed expansion with analytic $h$. For $U=-e^{-\kappa(t-r_*)}$ and $V=e^{\kappa(t+r_*)}$ in the right exterior, $-UV=(r-r_H)e^{2\kappa h(r)}$ after fixing an additive constant. The inverse function theorem makes $r$ analytic in $UV$ through zero, while $ds^2=f\,dU\,dV/(\kappa^2UV)+R^2d\Omega^2$ has a finite nonzero radial coefficient there. This supplies the local analytic extension and its <bifurcate Killing horizon>.

= Schwarzschild geodesic from the bifurcation sphere to the singularity
{parent=Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates}

A radial geodesic orthogonal to the time-symmetric Kruskal slice starts at $r=2M$ with zero Schwarzschild energy and reaches $r=0$ after proper time $\pi M$.

= Gravitational redshift between static Schwarzschild observers
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}

For static observers at radii $r_A$ and $r_B$, equal Schwarzschild-coordinate time intervals correspond to proper times $d\tau=\sqrt{1-2M/r}\,dt$. Hence
$$
\frac{\Delta\tau_B}{\Delta\tau_A}
=\sqrt{\frac{1-2M/r_B}{1-2M/r_A}}.
$$

This static-observer relation is a special case of <gravitational redshift>, not a definition of every gravitational frequency shift.

= Proper acceleration of a static Schwarzschild observer
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}

A static observer at Schwarzschild radius $r>2M$ must maintain the outward <proper acceleration>
$$
a=\frac{M}{r^2\sqrt{1-2M/r}}.
$$
It approaches the Newtonian value $M/r^2$ at large radius and diverges at the horizon.

= Birkhoff's theorem
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}
{wiki=Birkhoff's_theorem_(relativity)}

= Birkhoff theorem
{c}
{synonym}

Every spherically symmetric vacuum solution of the Einstein field equation is locally a portion of Schwarzschild spacetime. In particular, a spherically symmetric body's exterior field depends only on its total mass.

= Redshifted gravitational mass of a static thin shell
{parent=Birkhoff's theorem}

A thin spherical shell of proper surface mass density $\rho$ held at Schwarzschild radius $r_{\rm st}$ around mass $M$ contributes, to first order in its mass,
$$
\Delta M_\infty
=4\pi r_{\rm st}^2\rho\sqrt{1-\frac{2M}{r_{\rm st}}}
$$
to the exterior Schwarzschild mass parameter.

= Schwarzschild light deflection
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild spacetime}

A null Schwarzschild geodesic with $b\gg r_s$ is deflected through $2r_s/b=4GM/(bc^2)$ to first order.

This is the leading spherical-mass deflection underlying a <gravitational lens>.

= Perturbative derivation of Schwarzschild light deflection
{parent=Schwarzschild light deflection}

Writing $y=b^{-1}\sin\phi+\Delta y$ in $y''+y=3My^2$ and retaining first order in $M$ gives
$$
\Delta y''+\Delta y=\frac{3M}{2b^2}(1-\cos2\phi).
$$
The displacement of the outgoing zero is $4M/b$.

= Nordström's theory of gravitation
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

= Nordstrom theory of gravitation
{c}
{synonym}

Nordstrom's scalar theory restricts the spacetime metric to the conformally flat form $g_{\mu\nu}=\varphi^2\eta_{\mu\nu}$. Its null geodesics have the same unparametrized paths as those of the <Minkowski metric>, so the theory predicts no gravitational bending of light.

= Newtonian limit of Nordstrom gravity
{parent=Nordström's theory of gravitation}
{c}
{title2=$\phi=1+\Phi,\quad\Delta\Phi=4\pi\rho$}

For a weak, nearly static <conformal factor> in <Nordstrom theory of gravitation>, the trace of the <dust stress-energy tensor> is negative in signature $(-+++)$, so its scalar field equation reduces to the <Poisson equation> for the <Newtonian gravitational potential>. The <geodesic equation for a conformally flat metric> gives $\ddot{\boldsymbol x}=-\boldsymbol\nabla\Phi$ at small speeds. The constant one in $\phi$ has no effect on the force.

= Conformally flat metric
{parent=Nordström's theory of gravitation}

= Conformally flat
{synonym}

A metric is conformally flat when it is locally a positive scalar multiple of a flat metric. Multiplication by the conformal factor preserves null cones and unparametrized null geodesics.

A conformally flat metric is locally a positive scalar multiple of a flat metric. It makes its domain a <conformally flat manifold>.

= Tolman wormhole
{c}
{parent=Conformally flat metric}
{title2=$ds^2=(1+\lambda/r^2)^2\delta_{ij}dx^idx^j$}

For $\lambda>0$, this four-dimensional Euclidean metric has two asymptotically flat ends. Inversion $\rho=\lambda/r$ puts the metric near the $r=0$ end into the same form with $\rho\to\infty$. On $r>0$, its <Ricci scalar> vanishes because $\Delta(r^{-2})=0$, but its <Ricci tensor> is $R_{ij}=4\lambda(\delta_{ij}-4x_ix_j/r^2)/(r^2+\lambda)^2$. It therefore does not satisfy the <vacuum Einstein equations> unless $\lambda=0$. For negative $\lambda$ the conformal factor vanishes at a finite radius, so that metric is not a smooth wormhole across that sphere.

= Curvature of a conformally Euclidean metric
{parent=Conformally flat metric}
{title2=$g_{ij}=e^{2u}\delta_{ij}$}

For a positive conformal factor in dimension $n\geq3$, set $S_{ij}=-u_{ij}+u_i u_j-\tfrac12|\nabla u|^2\delta_{ij}$. These are the coordinate components of the <Schouten tensor>. In the convention $R_{ijkl}=g(e_i,R(e_k,e_l)e_j)$, the <Riemann curvature tensor> is $R_{ijkl}=e^{2u}(\delta_{ik}S_{jl}+\delta_{jl}S_{ik}-\delta_{il}S_{jk}-\delta_{jk}S_{il})$. The <orthonormal coframe> $e^u dx^i$ has <connection 1-forms> $\omega^i{}_j=u_jdx^i-u_idx^j$; substituting into <Cartan's second structure equation> proves this expression directly. Its contractions give $R_{ij}=(n-2)S_{ij}+\delta_{ij}\sum_kS_{kk}$ and $R=e^{-2u}[-2(n-1)\Delta u-(n-1)(n-2)|\nabla u|^2]$.

= Conformal factor
{parent=Conformally flat metric}
{wiki}

A conformal factor is the positive scalar field multiplying a metric under a conformal rescaling. In numerical relativity one often writes a spatial metric as $\gamma_{ij}=\psi^4\widetilde\gamma_{ij}$.

= Conformal preservation of null geodesic paths
{parent=Conformal factor}
{title2=$\widetilde g=\Omega^2g$}

The difference of the two <Levi-Civita connections> is $\widetilde\Gamma^a{}_{bc}-\Gamma^a{}_{bc}=\delta^a_b\partial_c\log\Omega+\delta^a_c\partial_b\log\Omega-g_{bc}g^{ad}\partial_d\log\Omega$. Along a <null geodesic> with tangent $k$, the last term vanishes and the remaining acceleration is $2k(\log\Omega)k$. It is parallel to the tangent, so changing the <affine parameter> removes it. Thus a positive <conformal factor> preserves unparametrized <null geodesics>, although it generally does not preserve timelike or spacelike geodesics.

= Geodesic equation for a conformally flat metric
{parent=Nordström's theory of gravitation}

For $g_{\mu\nu}=\varphi^2\eta_{\mu\nu}$,
$$
\Gamma^\mu_{\alpha\beta}
=\delta^\mu_\alpha\partial_\beta\log\varphi
+\delta^\mu_\beta\partial_\alpha\log\varphi
-\eta_{\alpha\beta}\partial^\mu\log\varphi.
$$
For a null curve, the last term drops from the <geodesic equation>; the remaining acceleration is parallel to the tangent and can be removed by reparametrization.

= de Sitter spacetime
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

= de Sitter
{c}
{synonym}

De Sitter spacetime is the maximally symmetric Lorentzian spacetime of constant positive curvature. Its expanding flat slicing has scale factor $a(\tau)=-1/(H\tau)$ in conformal time $\tau<0$.

= Two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime
{parent=de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$ds^2=dt^2-\cosh^2t\,d\chi^2$}

Unit-radius <two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime> is the <hyperboloid> $(X^0)^2-(X^1)^2-(X^2)^2=-1$ in three-dimensional <Minkowski spacetime>. Its closed coordinates are $X^0=\sinh t$, $X^1=\cosh t\cos\chi$, $X^2=\cosh t\sin\chi$, with $t\in\mathbb R$ and $\chi$ periodic modulo $2\pi$. Unwrapping $\chi$ gives its universal covering spacetime instead.

= Universal cover of two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$ds^2=\sec^2\eta(-d\eta^2+d\xi^2),\quad\xi\in\mathbb R$}

The unrestricted spatial coordinate unwraps the spatial circle of <two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime>. The <conformal time> $\eta=\arcsin(\tanh t)$ lies in $(-\pi/2,\pi/2)$, and <null geodesics> have slopes $d\xi/d\eta=\pm1$. Constant-$\eta$ lines are <Cauchy hypersurfaces>: along any causal curve $|d\xi/d\eta|\leq1$, so a curve cannot escape to spatial infinity in a bounded conformal-time interval. The covering spacetime is therefore <globally hyperbolic>, despite its observer-dependent cosmological horizons. Identifying $\xi$ modulo $2\pi$ gives the de Sitter cylinder, also globally hyperbolic.

= Conformal cylinder of two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$ds^2=\sec^2T(dT^2-d\chi^2),\quad -\pi/2<T<\pi/2$}

The <conformal time> $T=2\arctan(e^t)-\pi/2$ satisfies $\sin T=\tanh t$ and $\cos T=\operatorname{sech}t$. Multiplying the physical metric by $\cos^2T$ gives a finite <conformal compactification> with periodic spatial coordinate. Its past and future conformal boundaries are spacelike circles. <Null geodesics> have slopes $d\chi/dT=\pm1$.

= Observer horizons in two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Conformal cylinder of two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$d(\chi,0)<\pi/2-T\quad\text{or}\quad d(\chi,0)<T+\pi/2$}

For the complete observer $\chi=0$ in <two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime>, let $d(\chi,0)\in[0,\pi]$ be shortest angular distance. An event can signal to the observer exactly when $d(\chi,0)<\pi/2-T$, and can receive its signals exactly when $d(\chi,0)<T+\pi/2$. Equality describes its future and past <observer event horizons>. Their intersection is the <static patch of de Sitter spacetime> containing the observer. These are observer-dependent cosmological horizons; constant-$T$ circles are <Cauchy hypersurfaces>, so there are no <Cauchy horizons> in the full spacetime.

= Geodesics of two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$X''=\varepsilon X,\quad \varepsilon=X'^2\in\{1,0,-1\}$}

A <geodesic> on the embedded <two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime> is the intersection with a two-plane through the ambient origin. For initial point $P$ and tangent $V$ satisfying $P^2=-1$, $P\cdot V=0$, $V^2=\varepsilon$, its affine parametrization is $P\cosh s+V\sinh s$ when timelike, $P+sV$ when null and $P\cos s+V\sin s$ when spacelike. These solve $X''=\varepsilon X$. The <spacelike geodesics> are closed; <timelike geodesics> and <null geodesics> extend to both conformal infinities with infinite affine length.

= Closed slicing of de Sitter spacetime
{parent=de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$ds^2=\ell^2[-d\tau^2+\cosh^2\tau\,d\Omega_3^2],\quad\ell^2=3/\Lambda$}

The vacuum <Einstein field equations> with positive <cosmological constant> admit a closed spherical slicing with scale factor $a(t)=\ell\cosh[(t-t_0)/\ell]$. The time-symmetric slice is at $t=t_0$. The substitution $T=2\arctan(e^\tau)-\pi/2$ gives $dT/d\tau=\operatorname{sech}\tau$ and $\cos T=\operatorname{sech}\tau$, so the metric is $\ell^2\sec^2T[-dT^2+d\Omega_3^2]$. The conformal metric is a finite-time slab of the <Einstein static universe>, with spacelike conformal infinities $T=\pm\pi/2$.

= Cosmological horizons in global de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Closed slicing of de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$\chi_p=T+\pi/2,\quad\chi_e=\pi/2-T$}

For a complete comoving observer at the north pole of the <closed slicing of de Sitter spacetime>, radial null rays have $d\chi=\pm dT$. The finite past conformal interval gives the displayed <particle horizon>; the finite remaining future interval gives the displayed <observer event horizon>. The angular distances range strictly between zero and $\pi$ at every finite proper time. Complete constant-$T$ three-spheres are <Cauchy hypersurfaces>, so their domains of dependence cover the global spacetime and they have no <Cauchy horizons>. Cosmological horizons are observer-dependent and are not failures of global hyperbolicity.

= Static patch of de Sitter spacetime
{parent=de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$ds^2=-(1-r^2/R^2)dt^2+dr^2/(1-r^2/R^2)+r^2d\Omega_2^2$}

This region $0\leq r<R$ of <de Sitter spacetime> is static for the central observer, with a cosmological <Killing horizon> at $r=R$. The central <proper time> fixes the normalization of $\partial_t$. Static observers have acceleration $r/(R^2\sqrt{1-r^2/R^2})$. Horizon-crossing null coordinates remove the coordinate singularity, while the global extension has compact spherical spatial slices and spacelike past/future conformal infinity.

= Euclidean de Sitter action and entropy
{c}
{parent=Static patch of de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$I_E=-3\pi/\Lambda,\quad S=3\pi/\Lambda$}

In units $G=\hbar=c=k_B=1$, a smooth <Wick rotation> of the four-dimensional <static patch of de Sitter spacetime> has Euclidean time period $2\pi\sqrt{3/\Lambda}$. Its compact geometry is a round four-sphere of radius $\sqrt{3/\Lambda}$. The <Einstein field equations> give $R=4\Lambda$, and direct integration gives volume $24\pi^2/\Lambda^2$, hence gravitational <Euclidean action> $I_E=-3\pi/\Lambda$. With zero assigned internal energy, $I_E=\beta E-S$ gives <Bekenstein-Hawking entropy> $S=3\pi/\Lambda=A/4$.

= de Sitter horizon temperature
{c}
{parent=Static patch of de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$T(r)=1/(2\pi R\sqrt{1-r^2/R^2})$}

In units $\hbar=c=k_B=1$, the central inertial observer has temperature $1/(2\pi R)$. The <Tolman temperature law> redshifts it to the displayed static-observer value. Its acceleration satisfies $4\pi^2T^2=a^2+R^{-2}$. Near the horizon the temperature approaches $a/(2\pi)$, as predicted by the <Unruh effect>; at the center temperature remains nonzero despite vanishing acceleration.

= de Sitter isometry
{c}
{parent=de Sitter spacetime}

Four-dimensional <de Sitter spacetime> has ten independent isometries. In the expanding flat slicing they act on the spatial boundary as three translations, three rotations, one dilation, and three special conformal transformations.

= Spatial translation
{parent=de Sitter isometry}

A spatial translation in de Sitter flat slicing sends $\mathbf x\mapsto\mathbf x+\mathbf a$ at fixed conformal time. Translation invariance of a momentum-space correlator produces its momentum-conserving <Dirac delta distribution>.

= Spatial rotation
{parent=de Sitter isometry}

A spatial rotation acts by $\mathbf x\mapsto R\mathbf x$ with $R$ orthogonal. A rotationally invariant scalar two-point function depends only on the magnitude of its momentum.

= de Sitter dilation
{c}
{parent=de Sitter isometry}

In flat conformal coordinates, a de Sitter dilation acts as $(\tau,\mathbf x)\mapsto(s\tau,s\mathbf x)$. It is an isometry because the common coordinate rescaling cancels the inverse-square scaling of the conformal factor.

= Special conformal transformation
{parent=de Sitter isometry}
{wiki}

The three special conformal transformations complete translations, rotations, and dilations to the ten-dimensional de Sitter isometry group in flat slicing. A propagation speed different from the metric light speed generally breaks these transformations.

= Expanding flat slicing of de Sitter spacetime
{c}
{parent=de Sitter spacetime}

The expanding flat slicing has metric
$$
ds^2=-dt^2+e^{2Ht}d\mathbf x^2,
\qquad H>0.
$$
It covers one half of global <de Sitter spacetime> and has a past coordinate boundary at $t\to-\infty$.

= Timelike geodesic in the expanding flat slicing of de Sitter spacetime
{c}
{parent=Expanding flat slicing of de Sitter spacetime}

For a timelike geodesic moving in the $x$ direction with $t(0)=x(0)=0$ and $\dot x(0)=v_0>0$, spatial translation symmetry gives $e^{2Ht}\dot x=v_0$. Proper-time normalization then gives
$$
\dot t=\sqrt{1+v_0^2e^{-2Ht}}.
$$

= Past incompleteness of the expanding flat slicing of de Sitter spacetime
{c}
{parent=Timelike geodesic in the expanding flat slicing of de Sitter spacetime}

The geodesic with nonzero conserved spatial momentum reaches $t=-\infty$ after the finite past proper time
$$
\tau_-=-\frac1{2H}\log\frac{\sqrt{1+v_0^2}+1}{\sqrt{1+v_0^2}-1}.
$$
This is incompleteness of the expanding coordinate patch, whose geodesics extend smoothly in global <de Sitter spacetime>; it is not a curvature singularity.

= Einstein static universe
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

The Einstein static universe is a product of time with a round three-sphere supported by matter and a positive cosmological constant. For pressureless matter its cosmological repulsion balances the spatial curvature exactly.

= Anti-de Sitter spacetime
{parent=General relativity}
{c}
{wiki}

= AdS
{c}
{synonym}

= Anti-de Sitter
{c}
{synonym}

Anti-de Sitter spacetime is the maximally symmetric Lorentzian spacetime of constant negative curvature.

= Matrix model of anti-de Sitter three-space
{parent=Anti-de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$\operatorname{AdS}_3\cong SL(2,\mathbb R),\quad g=B/8$}

For ambient <quadratic form> $Q=-x_0^2-x_4^2+x_1^2+x_2^2$, associate $M=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x_4+x_1&x_2+x_0\\x_2-x_0&x_4-x_1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. Its <determinant> is $-Q$, so the unit <anti-de Sitter spacetime> hyperboloid is the <special linear group> of real two-by-two matrices. On its tangent space at the identity, the induced <bilinear form> is $\tfrac12\operatorname{tr}(UV)$. The <Killing form> is $4\operatorname{tr}(UV)$, and left and right matrix multiplication preserve the ambient form. Consequently the induced <bi-invariant pseudo-Riemannian metric> is $B/8$. This model has periodic time; its causally unwrapped version is the <universal covering Lie group>.

= Left-right double cover of SO0(2,2)
{parent=Matrix model of anti-de Sitter three-space}
{title2=$SL(2,\mathbb R)^2/\{(I,I),(-I,-I)\}\cong SO_0(2,2)$}

The action $(A,B):M\mapsto AMB^{-1}$ preserves the <quadratic form> $-\det M$ on real two-by-two matrices. Its <kernel> consists of the simultaneous scalar pairs $(I,I)$ and $(-I,-I)$. The differential $(a,b):M\mapsto aM-Mb$ is injective on the two traceless <Lie algebras>, and both source and target have dimension six. Its image is therefore open and, since the source is connected, equals the <identity component of a Lie group> $SO_0(2,2)$. This gives a two-sheeted <covering map> onto the identity component, not onto the disconnected full <special orthogonal group> $SO(2,2)$.

= Poincare coordinates on anti-de Sitter spacetime
{c}
{parent=Anti-de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$ds^2=z^{-2}(dz^2+\eta_{ab}du^a du^b)$}

For Lorentzian boundary metric $\eta$ and $z>0$, this <anti-de Sitter spacetime> patch has unit curvature radius. Its <orthonormal coframe> has connection $\omega^a{}_3=-\theta^a$ and curvature $\Omega^a{}_b=-\theta^a\wedge\theta_b$, giving <sectional curvature> $-1$. The full local <isometry> algebra is $\mathfrak{so}(d-1,2)$ in spacetime dimension $d$. A patch need not support every local Killing flow for all parameter values.

= Two-dimensional anti-de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Anti-de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$ds^2=\cosh^2r\,dt^2-dr^2$}

The unit hyperboloid $(X^0)^2+(X^1)^2-(X^2)^2=1$ in ambient signature $(++-)$ has $X^0=\cosh r\cos t$, $X^1=\cosh r\sin t$, $X^2=\sinh r$. It gives the displayed metric with $r\in\mathbb R$ and periodic $t$ modulo $2\pi$. Its time circles are timelike, so the <universal cover>, with $t\in\mathbb R$, removes these <closed timelike curves>. The metric alone does not prescribe the global identification. Both versions have constant negative curvature and two spatial ends.

= Geodesics of two-dimensional anti-de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Two-dimensional anti-de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$X''=-\kappa X,\quad\kappa=X'^2\in\{1,0,-1\}$}

A geodesic of the unit embedded hyperboloid lies in the plane spanned by its initial point and tangent. The constraint $X^2=1$ forces the displayed ambient acceleration. Timelike geodesics are $X=P\cos s+V\sin s$, null ones are $X=P+sV$, and spacelike ones are $X=P\cosh s+V\sinh s$. Timelike geodesics are closed on the hyperboloid but lift to complete nonclosed curves on its <universal cover>. In static coordinates, conserved $E=\cosh^2r\,dt/ds$ gives $(dr/ds)^2=E^2/\cosh^2r-1$ and $\sinh r=\sqrt{E^2-1}\sin(s-s_0)$. Null affine parameters obey $\lambda=\pm\sinh r/E+\text{constant}$, proving completeness at both spatial ends.

= Conformal strip of two-dimensional anti-de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Two-dimensional anti-de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$ds^2=\sec^2\psi(dt^2-d\psi^2),\quad\psi=\arctan(\sinh r)$}

On the universal cover, $-\pi/2<\psi<\pi/2$ and $t\in\mathbb R$. The two <conformal boundaries> are timelike. <Null geodesics> are $t\pm\psi=\text{constant}$ and can travel from either boundary to the center in finite coordinate time, despite taking infinite physical affine parameter to reach the boundary. There is no event horizon for the complete central static observer. The spacetime is not <globally hyperbolic>, unlike the global conformal cylinder of <two-dimensional de Sitter spacetime>; well-posed field evolution requires boundary conditions as well as initial data.

= Group model of three-dimensional anti-de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Anti-de Sitter spacetime}
{title2=$\mathrm{AdS}_3\cong SL(2,\mathbb R)$}

Associate $(u,v,x,y)$ with $X=\begin{pmatrix}u+x&y+v\\y-v&u-x\end{pmatrix}$. The ambient quadratic form is $-\det X$; hence the radius-$\ell$ anti-de Sitter quadric is $X=\ell g$ with $g\in SL(2,\mathbb R)$. The action $X\mapsto AXB^{-1}$ gives $SO_0(2,2)\cong(SL(2,\mathbb R)\times SL(2,\mathbb R))/\{(I,I),(-I,-I)\}$. Its induced <bi-invariant pseudo-Riemannian metric> is $g=\ell^2K/8$, giving $\operatorname{Ric}=-2g/\ell^2$ and cosmological constant $-1/\ell^2$. The antipodal quotient is $PSL(2,\mathbb R)$; unwrapping global time gives the universal covering group instead. The connected component is essential in the displayed orthogonal-group isomorphism.

= Static coordinates on anti-de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Anti-de Sitter spacetime}

In four dimensions, global static coordinates have
$$ds^2=-(1+r^2/a^2)dt^2+(1+r^2/a^2)^{-1}dr^2+r^2d\Omega^2.$$

= Radial timelike geodesic in anti-de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Static coordinates on anti-de Sitter spacetime}

A radial timelike geodesic through the origin obeys a harmonic radial equation and returns to the origin after proper time $\pi a$, independently of its energy.

= Radial null geodesic in anti-de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Static coordinates on anti-de Sitter spacetime}

In global static coordinates, an outward radial null geodesic takes infinite affine parameter to approach the anti-de Sitter conformal boundary but only finite static coordinate time $\pi a/2$.

= Stable circular timelike geodesic in anti-de Sitter spacetime
{parent=Static coordinates on anti-de Sitter spacetime}

At every radius $r_0>0$, global anti-de Sitter spacetime has stable circular timelike geodesics with $|\dot\phi|=1/a$ and $\dot t=1$.

= Planarity of geodesics in spherical symmetry
{parent=General relativity}

Rotational symmetry conserves the angular-momentum direction, so every nonradial geodesic lies in a plane through the symmetry centre; coordinates may place that plane at $\theta=\pi/2$.

= Timelike geodesic effective potential
{parent=General relativity}

After using Killing constants and four-velocity normalization, radial timelike geodesic motion can often be written as kinetic energy plus an effective potential equal to a constant.

= Effective-potential stability of a circular orbit
{parent=Timelike geodesic effective potential}

A circular orbit at a strict local minimum of the radial effective potential is stable under small radial perturbations.

= Effective potential for timelike Schwarzschild geodesics
{parent=Timelike geodesic effective potential}

For equatorial timelike motion with specific angular momentum $h$,
$$
\frac12\dot r^2+V_{\rm eff}(r)=\frac{E^2-1}{2},
\qquad
V_{\rm eff}=-\frac mr+\frac{h^2}{2r^2}-\frac{mh^2}{r^3}.
$$
A circular orbit has $h^2=mr^2/(r-3m)$ and therefore requires $r>3m$.

= Schwarzschild marginally bound capture
{c}
{parent=Effective potential for timelike Schwarzschild geodesics}
{title2=$|h|<4M$}

= Marginally bound Schwarzschild capture
{synonym}

A <timelike geodesic> with unit specific <Killing energy> can plunge from infinity into a <Schwarzschild black hole> only if its specific <angular momentum> satisfies $|h|<4M$. The limiting case $|h|=4M$ asymptotes to the unstable orbit at $r=4M$. A geodesic already inside the angular-momentum barrier can cross the <event horizon> with larger $|h|$, so the origin-at-infinity condition matters.

= Angular velocity of a circular Schwarzschild geodesic
{parent=Effective potential for timelike Schwarzschild geodesics}
{c}

A circular timelike geodesic of Schwarzschild radius $r>3M$ has coordinate angular velocity
$$
\frac{d\phi}{dt}=\sqrt{\frac{M}{r^3}}.
$$
Its coordinate period is $2\pi\sqrt{r^3/M}$.

= Proper period of a circular Schwarzschild geodesic
{parent=Angular velocity of a circular Schwarzschild geodesic}
{c}

Over one circular orbit at radius $r>3M$, the orbiting clock measures
$$
\Delta\tau=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^2(r-3M)}M}.
$$
The factor $r-3M$ combines gravitational and orbital kinematic time dilation.

= Schwarzschild perihelion precession
{parent=Effective potential for timelike Schwarzschild geodesics}
{c}

The nearly circular orbit equation
$$
u''+u=\frac m{h^2}+3mu^2
$$
has radial angular frequency $1-3m^2/h^2$ to leading order. The perihelion advances by $6\pi m^2/h^2$ per orbit.

= Metric tensor
{title2=$g_{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

A metric tensor is a <nondegenerate bilinear form>[nondegenerate] symmetric <covariant tensor> that defines squared line elements by $ds^2=g_{\mu\nu}dx^\mu dx^\nu$.

= Inverse metric volume variation
{parent=Metric tensor}
{title2=$\delta\sqrt{|g|}=-\tfrac12\sqrt{|g|}g_{ab}\delta g^{ab}$}

For a nondegenerate <metric tensor>, differentiating $g_{ac}g^{cb}=\delta_a{}^b$ gives $\delta g_{ab}=-g_{ac}g_{bd}\delta g^{cd}$. Combining this with the <Jacobi determinant derivative formula> proves the displayed volume-density variation. It is the determinant contribution when defining a <stress-energy tensor> by varying the inverse <metric tensor>.

= Prolate spheroidal coordinates
{parent=Metric tensor}
{title2=$(\lambda,\mu,\phi)$}

For $0<\alpha<\beta$, define $-\beta\leq\mu\leq-\alpha\leq\lambda$ by $R^2/(\tau+\alpha)+z^2/(\tau+\beta)=1$. Then $R^2=(\lambda+\alpha)(\mu+\alpha)/(\alpha-\beta)$ and $z^2=(\lambda+\beta)(\mu+\beta)/(\beta-\alpha)$. Constant $\lambda$ surfaces are confocal <prolate spheroids> and constant interior $\mu$ surfaces are two-sheeted hyperboloids, with foci $z=\pm\sqrt{\beta-\alpha}$. The coordinates are orthogonal away from their degeneracies. The squared scale factors are $h_\lambda^2=(\lambda-\mu)/[4(\lambda+\alpha)(\lambda+\beta)]$, $h_\mu^2=(\mu-\lambda)/[4(\mu+\alpha)(\mu+\beta)]$ and $h_\phi^2=R^2$. The two signs of $z$ are coordinate branches, joined at the equatorial fold $\mu=-\beta$.

= Lorentzian metric
{c}
{parent=Metric tensor}
{title2=$\operatorname{signature}(g)=(-,+,\ldots,+)$}

A Lorentzian metric is a smooth nondegenerate symmetric <metric tensor> with one negative and all other positive directions, or its opposite-sign convention. At each point, a nonzero tangent is timelike, null or spacelike according as $g(v,v)$ is negative, zero or positive in the first convention. Unlike a <Riemannian metric>, it gives a light cone rather than a positive norm. <Minkowski spacetime> is its constant flat model, and <proper time> along a timelike curve is $\int\sqrt{-g(\dot\gamma,\dot\gamma)}\,du$.

= Spacetime metric
{parent=Lorentzian metric}
{title2=$g_{\mu\nu}$}

A spacetime metric is the <Lorentzian metric> on a <spacetime>. It determines <proper time> along timelike paths and the null directions that describe local light propagation.

= Null coordinate
{parent=Lorentzian metric}

A <null coordinate> is a coordinate function $u$ whose gradient is null, $g^{ab}\partial_au\partial_bu=0$. Its level sets are <null hypersurfaces>. Pairs of such coordinates straighten radial <null directions> in two-dimensional orbit metrics; a monotone reparametrization of either function preserves its null level sets. Examples include the <retarded and advanced null coordinates> and the radial <Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates>.

= Schwarzschild retarded null coordinate
{c}
{parent=Null coordinate}
{title2=$u=t-r_*,\quad r_*=r+2M\log(r/(2M)-1)$}

In the exterior of <Schwarzschild spacetime>, $dr_*/dr=(1-2M/r)^{-1}$. Outgoing <radial null geodesics> therefore have constant $u=t-r_*$. At fixed receiver radius $R$, their arrival Schwarzschild time is $u+r_*(R)$, so differences in $u$ give arrival-time differences. A static receiver's <proper time> includes the factor $\sqrt{1-2M/R}$.

// Target: optics.bigb

= Spacelike vector
{parent=Lorentzian metric}
{title2=$g(v,v)>0$}

With <metric signature> $(-,+,+,+)$, a nonzero <vector> $v$ is spacelike when its <Lorentzian inner product> with itself satisfies $g(v,v)>0$. In <Minkowski spacetime>, this means its spatial components have larger squared Euclidean length than its time component. The classification is preserved by <Lorentz transformations>. A <timelike vector> has negative squared <Lorentzian inner product>, and a <null vector> has zero squared <Lorentzian inner product>.

= Lorentzian inner product
{parent=Lorentzian metric}

A <Lorentzian inner product> is a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form with one negative and the remaining positive eigenvalues in the mostly-plus convention. Two nonzero future causal vectors have nonpositive inner product; if either is timelike, it is strictly negative.

= Null covector
{parent=Lorentzian inner product}
{title2=$g^{\alpha\beta}\ell_\alpha\ell_\beta=0$}

A nonzero <covector> $\ell$ is null when its squared dual <Lorentzian inner product> is zero: $g^{\alpha\beta}\ell_\alpha\ell_\beta=0$. Raising its index gives a <null vector>. Null covectors describe normals to characteristic <light cones> and are the test directions in the <classical null condition for wave equations>.

= Causal vector
{parent=Lorentzian metric}

A nonzero <causal vector> is timelike or null: $g(v,v)\le0$ with <metric signature> $(-,+,+,+)$. The zero vector is commonly included when stating the <dominant energy condition>.

= Timelike vector
{parent=Lorentzian metric}
{title2=$g(v,v)<0$}

= Timelike direction
{synonym}

A <timelike vector> $v$ obeys $g(v,v)<0$ in <metric signature> $(-,+,+,+)$. Its orthogonal complement has positive-definite metric; the two timelike cones give the local future and past choices.

= Timelike vector field
{parent=Timelike vector}

A <timelike vector field> assigns a timelike vector continuously or smoothly to every event in its domain. A globally defined such field gives a <time orientation>, but it need not be a <Killing vector field> or a <timelike geodesic vector field>.

= Oblate spheroidal coordinates
{parent=Metric tensor}
{wiki}

With parameter $a\geq0$, oblate spheroidal coordinates on Euclidean three-space are
$$
x=\sqrt{r^2+a^2}\sin\theta\cos\phi,
\quad
y=\sqrt{r^2+a^2}\sin\theta\sin\phi,
\quad
z=r\cos\theta.
$$
Writing $\Sigma=r^2+a^2\cos^2\theta$ and $\Delta=r^2+a^2$, their spatial line element is
$$
dl^2=\frac\Sigma\Delta dr^2+\Sigma d\theta^2
+\Delta\sin^2\theta\,d\phi^2.
$$

= Outgoing null oblate spheroidal coordinates
{parent=Oblate spheroidal coordinates}

Starting from the <Minkowski metric> in <oblate spheroidal coordinates>, set $u=t-r$, $R=r$, and choose an azimuth $\varphi$ satisfying
$$
d\varphi=d\phi-\frac{a}{R^2+a^2}dR.
$$
Then
$$
ds^2=-du^2-2\,du\,dR
+2a\sin^2\theta\,dR\,d\varphi
+(R^2+a^2\cos^2\theta)d\theta^2
+(R^2+a^2)\sin^2\theta\,d\varphi^2,
$$
so the coordinate vector $\partial_R$ is null.

= Inverse metric
{title2=$g^{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Metric tensor}

The inverse metric satisfies $g^{\mu\rho}g_{\rho\nu}=\delta^\mu_\nu$ and raises covariant tensor indices.

= Two-dimensional hyperbolic metric in polar coordinates
{title2=$ds^2=a^2(dr^2+\sinh^2r\,d\phi^2)$}
{parent=General relativity}

This metric is the <hyperbolic plane> with curvature scale $a$. Its nonzero connection coefficients are
$$
\Gamma^r_{\phi\phi}=-\sinh r\cosh r,
\qquad
\Gamma^\phi_{r\phi}=\Gamma^\phi_{\phi r}=\coth r.
$$

= Coordinate geodesics of the hyperbolic polar metric
{parent=Two-dimensional hyperbolic metric in polar coordinates}

Every radial coordinate line $\phi=\text{constant}$ is a geodesic. No nonconstant coordinate circle $r=r_0>0$ is a geodesic.

= Curvature of the two-dimensional hyperbolic polar metric
{parent=Two-dimensional hyperbolic metric in polar coordinates}

For the curvature convention
$$
R^\alpha{}_{\beta\gamma\delta}
=\partial_\gamma\Gamma^\alpha_{\beta\delta}
-\partial_\delta\Gamma^\alpha_{\beta\gamma}
+\Gamma^\mu_{\beta\delta}\Gamma^\alpha_{\mu\gamma}
-\Gamma^\mu_{\beta\gamma}\Gamma^\alpha_{\mu\delta},
$$
the independent nonzero mixed components are
$$
R^r{}_{\phi r\phi}=-\sinh^2r,
\qquad
R^\phi{}_{r\phi r}=-1,
$$
and the Ricci scalar is $R=-2/a^2$.

= Ricci scalar
{title2=$R$}
{parent=General relativity}
{c}

The Ricci scalar is the full metric contraction of the <Ricci tensor>:
$$
R=g^{\mu\nu}R_{\mu\nu}.
$$

\Include[geodesic-congruence]{parent=General relativity}

= Null hypersurface
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

A null hypersurface has a degenerate induced metric. Its normal covector raises to a null vector tangent to the hypersurface, whose integral curves are its null generators.

= Null hypersurface normal generates null geodesics
{parent=Null hypersurface}

If $n_a=\nabla_a u$ is normal to a null level set, then on the hypersurface
$$
n^b\nabla_bn_a=\frac12\nabla_a(n^2)=\kappa n_a.
$$
Thus the normal is tangent to null pregeodesics and can be rescaled to give an affine null tangent.

= Killing horizon
{parent=Null hypersurface}
{wiki}

A Killing horizon is a <null hypersurface> whose null generators are orbits of a <Killing vector field> $K$. On the horizon, $K^b\nabla_bK^a=\kappa K^a$ defines its <surface gravity>.

= Horizon angular velocity
{parent=Killing horizon}
{title2=$\chi=k+\Omega_Hm$}

In an axisymmetric stationary spacetime, the <horizon angular velocity> is the constant coefficient in the null horizon generator $\chi=k+\Omega_Hm$, where $k$ is the stationary Killing vector normalized at infinity and $m$ generates axial orbits with period $2\pi$. It measures rotation relative to the stationary frame at infinity. For a <Kerr black hole>, $\Omega_H=a/(r_+^2+a^2)$. It enters the rotating physical-process first law through the horizon flux of $E-\Omega_HJ$.

= Bifurcate Killing horizon
{parent=Killing horizon}
{title2=$K=\kappa(V\partial_V-U\partial_U)$}

A bifurcate Killing horizon consists of two intersecting null <Killing horizons> of the same <Killing vector field>, which vanishes on their spacelike intersection, the <bifurcation surface>. In regular Kruskal coordinates the horizons are $U=0$ and $V=0$, and their intersection is $U=V=0$. The displayed field is tangent and normal to each horizon branch and zero on the intersection.

= Degenerate Killing horizon
{parent=Killing horizon}
{title2=$\kappa=0$}

A <degenerate Killing horizon> has zero <surface gravity>. In the radial quotient of an <extremal black hole>, a double horizon root produces a pole in the <tortoise coordinate> rather than the logarithm of a simple root. Such a horizon has no bifurcation surface of the usual nondegenerate type.

= Optical scalars on a Killing horizon
{parent=Killing horizon}
{title2=$\theta=\hat\sigma=\hat\omega=0$}

The <null expansion>, <null shear> and <null twist> of a congruence tangent to the generators of a <Killing horizon> all vanish there. If $k$ is its Killing generator and $\ell=fk$ is an affine generator, the <screen-space projector> kills every term proportional to $k$. The symmetric part of the projected derivative of $\ell$ therefore vanishes by the <Killing equation>. The antisymmetric part vanishes by <Frobenius theorem>, since $\ell$ is hypersurface-orthogonal. These conclusions extend through a regular <bifurcation surface> by continuity.

= Cosmological horizon
{parent=Killing horizon}
{wiki}

A cosmological horizon is a causal boundary beyond which events cannot communicate with a specified observer because of the global expansion or geometry of spacetime.

= Surface gravity
{title2=$\kappa$}
{parent=Killing horizon}
{wiki}

The surface gravity $\kappa$ measures the failure of a horizon-generating <Killing vector field> to be affinely parametrized. For a static metric $ds^2=-f(r)dt^2+f(r)^{-1}dr^2+\cdots$ with a simple horizon at $r=r_+$, $\kappa=|f'(r_+)|/2$.

= Signed surface gravity of a de Sitter horizon
{parent=Surface gravity}
{title2=$\kappa_{\rm future}=1/R,\quad\kappa_{\rm past}=-1/R$}

For the future-directed central-time <Killing vector field> in a <static patch of de Sitter spacetime>, the outgoing null chart gives the future branch coefficient $+1/R$ in $\nabla_k k=\kappa k$. The ingoing chart gives $-1/R$ on the past branch. Both follow from $d(k^2)=-2\kappa k^\flat$; the cross-term sign changes the horizon covector. The physical <Hawking temperature> uses $|\kappa|$, not a signed negative temperature.

= Surface gravity of a static spherical horizon
{parent=Surface gravity}
{title2=$\kappa=\tfrac12\sqrt{A'(r_+)B'(r_+)}$}

For a <static spacetime> with $g_{tt}=-A(r)$, $g_{rr}=1/B(r)$ and simple simultaneous horizon zeros, the squared <surface gravity> of $\partial_t$ is $\lim BA'^2/(4A)=A'(r_+)B'(r_+)/4$. Choose the positive value for the outer horizon. A rescaling of the time generator rescales its <surface gravity>.

= Surface gravity of a five-dimensional charged black hole
{parent=Surface gravity of a static spherical horizon}
{title2=$\kappa_\pm=\pm2\sqrt{M^2-Q^2}/r_\pm^3$}

The time translation is normalized at infinity. For the roots $r_\pm^2=M\pm\sqrt{M^2-Q^2}$, evaluate $V'(r)/2$ to get the signed horizon values. The outer <Hawking temperature> in natural units is $\sqrt{M^2-Q^2}/(\pi r_+^3)$. The extremal limit has zero surface gravity; the neutral case has only the outer positive-radius horizon and temperature $1/(2\pi\sqrt{2M})$.

= Surface gravity from the Killing derivative
{parent=Surface gravity}
{title2=$\kappa^2=-\tfrac12(\nabla_aK_b)(\nabla^aK^b)$}

At a regular <Killing horizon>, the generator is hypersurface orthogonal and satisfies $\nabla_KK=\kappa K$. The <Killing derivative contraction identity> gives the displayed squared <surface gravity>, including through a <bifurcation surface> by continuity. The generator's normalization is part of this formula.

= Affine horizon-generator scaling
{parent=Surface gravity}
{title2=$\xi=\kappa\lambda k$}

If a <Killing horizon> generator $\xi$ has constant nonzero <surface gravity> $\kappa$, write $\xi=F(\lambda)k$ with $k=d/d\lambda$ affine. The equation $\nabla_\xi\xi=\kappa\xi$ implies $dF/d\lambda=\kappa$, so shifting the affine origin gives $\xi=\kappa\lambda k$.

= Schwarzschild surface gravity
{parent=Surface gravity}
{c}
{title2=$\kappa=1/(4M)$}

For a <Schwarzschild black hole> of <mass> $M$ in <geometrized units>, $f=1-2M/r$ gives $\kappa=f\prime(2M)/2=1/(4M)$. The <Killing vector field> is normalized at infinity; changing that normalization changes $\kappa$.

= Bifurcation surface
{parent=Killing horizon}

A bifurcation surface is a spacelike codimension-two cross-section on which the Killing generator of a bifurcate Killing horizon vanishes. Affine parameters on the future and past branches can be chosen to vanish there.

= Gaussian null coordinates
{c}
{parent=Null hypersurface}
{wiki}

Gaussian null coordinates adapt one coordinate to the generators of a <null hypersurface>, one affine coordinate to transverse null geodesics, and the remaining coordinates to a spacelike cross-section. If the generator parameter is affine, the metric can locally be written
$$
ds^2=2\,dr\,d\lambda+r^2F\,d\lambda^2+2rh_i\,d\lambda\,dy^i+h_{ij}\,dy^i\,dy^j.
$$

= Trapped surface
{parent=Null hypersurface}
{wiki}

A trapped surface is a closed spacelike codimension-two surface whose two future-directed orthogonal null congruences both have negative expansion.

= Apparent horizon
{parent=Trapped surface}
{wiki}

An <apparent horizon> is the outer boundary of the future-trapped region on a chosen spatial slice, under the usual regularity conditions. Its location depends on the slice; it is not the globally defined <event horizon>. At a regular boundary the outward <null expansion> vanishes while the inward <null expansion> is negative. Its area cannot be substituted into every version of the <Penrose inequality> without the appropriate area and initial-data hypotheses.

= Anti-trapped surface
{parent=Trapped surface}

An anti-trapped surface is the time reverse of a trapped surface: both future-directed orthogonal null expansions are positive.

= Null geodesic completeness
{parent=General relativity}

A spacetime is null-geodesically complete when every inextendible affinely parametrized null geodesic has an affine parameter extending without a finite endpoint in both directions.

A <complete manifold> requires completeness for every geodesic, whereas this condition only tests null ones.

= Null geodesic incompleteness
{parent=Null geodesic completeness}

= Null-geodesically incomplete
{synonym}

<Null geodesic incompleteness> means that some <maximal geodesic> with a <null tangent> has a finite endpoint of its <affine parameter>. This can indicate a removable missing region or a genuine obstruction; it does not alone assert curvature divergence.

= Penrose singularity theorem
{c}
{parent=Null geodesic completeness}
{wiki}

The Penrose singularity theorem states that a globally hyperbolic spacetime with a noncompact Cauchy hypersurface, a closed trapped surface, and the null convergence condition is future null-geodesically incomplete. Time reversal replaces the trapped surface by an anti-trapped surface and gives past incompleteness.

= Penrose theorem at a Cauchy horizon
{c}
{parent=Penrose singularity theorem}

The globally hyperbolic <maximal Cauchy development> of appropriate <Reissner-Nordstrom spacetime> data obeys the <Penrose singularity theorem> when it contains a closed <trapped surface>. Its resulting <null geodesic incompleteness> can include generators reaching a smoothly extendible inner <Cauchy horizon> in finite <affine parameter>. The full analytic extension is not globally hyperbolic and cannot be substituted into that version of the theorem. Incompleteness alone does not establish curvature blowup at every endpoint.

= Compactness of the future horismos of a trapped surface
{parent=Penrose singularity theorem}

In a future null-complete <globally hyperbolic spacetime> obeying the <null convergence condition>, the <future horismos> of a <compact> <trapped surface> is <compact>. Normalize its future null normals continuously using a timelike field. <Compactness> gives a uniform negative bound $\theta\leq-c<0$ on both initial <null expansions>. The <null focusing theorem> bounds all boundary generators by affine length $2/c$. Their endpoints lie in the image under a <continuous map> of the <compact> bundle of normalized null normals times $[0,2/c]$. The <future horismos> is <closed>, so is a <compact> subset of that image.

= Causal hierarchy
{parent=General relativity}

The causal hierarchy orders restrictions on the causal structure of a <spacetime>, from exclusion of <closed timelike curves> through <stable causality> and <global hyperbolicity>.

= Observer event horizon
{parent=Causal hierarchy}
{title2=$H^+_\gamma=\partial I^-(\gamma),\quad H^-_\gamma=\partial I^+(\gamma)$}

An observer's future event horizon bounds the events that can ever signal to its future-inextendible worldline. Its past event horizon bounds the events reachable by signals from its past-inextendible worldline. A complete inertial observer in <Minkowski spacetime> has neither. The uniformly accelerated worldline $t=a^{-1}\sinh(a\tau)$, $x=a^{-1}\cosh(a\tau)$ has $I^-(\gamma)=\{t-x<0\}$ and $I^+(\gamma)=\{t+x>0\}$. Their boundaries are its two <Rindler horizons>. This observer dependence is distinct from a black-hole horizon defined using <future null infinity>.

= Cosmological event horizon
{parent=Observer event horizon}
{title2=$\chi_{\mathrm{eh}}(t)=\int_t^\infty\frac{dt'}{a(t')}$}

For a comoving observer in a spatially flat <Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric>, a finite future <conformal time> interval gives an event horizon at the displayed comoving radius. Its physical radius at time $t$ is $a(t)\chi_{\mathrm{eh}}(t)$.

= Cosmological round-trip communication horizon
{parent=Cosmological event horizon}
{title2=$d_{\mathrm{round}}(t)=\frac{a(t)}2\int_t^\infty\frac{dt'}{a(t')}$}

A light signal sent from a comoving observer to a comoving reflector at separation $\chi$, and immediately returned, consumes <conformal time> $2\chi$. The supremum of the initial physical reflector distances allowing a return is therefore half the <cosmological event horizon> radius. Equality generally requires infinite return time; finite-time reception requires a strictly smaller distance. For a <cosmological domain wall network> with $a\propto t^2$, the supremum is $t/2=1/H$.

= Time orientation
{parent=Causal hierarchy}

A <time orientation> is a continuous choice of future cone throughout a time-orientable <spacetime>. A continuous nonzero <timelike vector field> supplies such a choice. For <metric signature> $(-,+,+,+)$, a nonzero causal vector $Z$ is future-directed relative to a future timelike $W$ exactly when $g(W,Z)<0$.

= Causal curve
{parent=Causal hierarchy}

A <causal curve> has an everywhere nonspacelike tangent of consistent time orientation; its smooth segments are timelike or null. More generally, curves with local <Lipschitz continuity> and future causal tangent almost everywhere are allowed. The <causal future> and <causal past> are defined by reachability along these curves.

= Causal past
{parent=Causal hierarchy}
{title2=$J^-(S)$}

The <causal past> is the time-reversed <causal future>. Relative to a specified component $\mathcal I^+$ of <future null infinity>, the <black hole> region is the complement of its <causal past>: $\mathcal B=\mathcal M\setminus J^-(\mathcal I^+)$.

= Causal future
{parent=Causal hierarchy}
{title2=$J^+(S)$}

The <causal future> of $S$ consists of points reached from $S$ by future-directed causal curves, including points of $S$ itself. In a <globally hyperbolic spacetime>, the <causal future> of a <compact> set is <closed>.

= Future horismos
{parent=Causal future}
{title2=$E^+(S)=J^+(S)\setminus I^+(S)$}

The <future horismos> is the part of the <causal future> outside the <chronological future>. For a <compact> set in a <globally hyperbolic spacetime>, it equals the <boundary> of the <chronological future>. Its normal <null geodesic> generators cannot have passed a <conjugate point to a spacelike surface> when the initial set is that surface.

= Chronological future
{title2=$I^+(S)$}
{parent=Causal hierarchy}

The chronological future $I^+(S)$ of a subset $S$ of a <spacetime> is the set of points reachable from $S$ by future-directed <timelike curves>. The chronological future is an <open set>.

= Chronologically saturated subset
{parent=Chronological future}

A subset $U$ is chronologically saturated in both directions when $I^+(U)=U=I^-(U)$. If the ambient <spacetime> is connected and $U$ is nonempty, then $U$ is the whole spacetime.

= Chronological past
{title2=$I^-(S)$}
{parent=Causal hierarchy}

The chronological past $I^-(S)$ is the set of points from which a future-directed <timelike curve> reaches $S$. Equivalently, it is the time reverse of the <chronological future>.

= Closed timelike curve
{parent=Causal hierarchy}
{wiki}

A closed timelike curve is a closed curve whose tangent is everywhere timelike, so an observer following it returns to the same spacetime event.

= Closed timelike circles in negative-radius Kerr spacetime
{parent=Closed timelike curve}
{title2=$g_{\phi\phi}=r^2+a^2+2Ma^2/r<0\quad(r<0\text{ near }0)$}

On the equatorial plane of the <Kerr metric>, the displayed component is negative for sufficiently small negative $r$. A curve of constant $t,r,\theta=\pi/2$, with $\phi$ increasing through $2\pi$, is therefore a regular <closed timelike curve>. It avoids the ring at $r=0$. Such circles occur on the negative-radius sheet of the exact analytic extension, not in the exterior region accessible from positive-radius infinity.

= Stable causality
{parent=Causal hierarchy}

A spacetime is stably causal when its light cones can be widened slightly without creating a <closed timelike curve>. Equivalently, it admits a continuous time function strictly increasing along every future-directed causal curve.

= Strong causality
{parent=Causal hierarchy}

= Strongly causal
{synonym}

Strong causality means that every event has arbitrarily small neighborhoods which no causal curve can leave and then re-enter. <Stable causality> implies strong causality.

= Future-distinguishing spacetime
{parent=Strong causality}

A spacetime is future-distinguishing when $I^+(p)=I^+(q)$ implies $p=q$. Every <strongly causal> spacetime is future-distinguishing.

= Globally hyperbolic spacetime
{parent=Causal hierarchy}
{wiki}

= Global hyperbolicity
{synonym}

= Globally hyperbolic
{synonym}

A globally hyperbolic spacetime has no causal pathologies and admits a Cauchy hypersurface intersected exactly once by every inextendible causal curve.

= Initial data in general relativity
{parent=Globally hyperbolic spacetime}

= Initial data
{synonym}

Gravitational <initial data> on a spatial manifold consist of a <Riemannian metric> $h$ and an <extrinsic curvature> $K$ satisfying the <Hamiltonian constraint> and <momentum constraint>. Matter theories require their own data and constraints as well. The data determine a <maximal Cauchy development>.

= Maximal Cauchy development
{parent=Initial data in general relativity}

A <maximal Cauchy development> is the largest globally hyperbolic solution, unique up to an appropriate <isometry>, determined by admissible <initial data>, with the initial hypersurface a <Cauchy hypersurface>. It can sometimes be extended as a spacetime across a <Cauchy horizon> even though that extension is no longer globally determined by the initial hypersurface.

= Strong cosmic censorship conjecture
{parent=Maximal Cauchy development}

The <strong cosmic censorship conjecture> asserts that the <maximal Cauchy development> of generic admissible <initial data> is inextendible in a specified regularity class. The matter model, topology on initial data, and extension regularity are part of the assertion. An exceptional exact solution with a smooth <Cauchy horizon> need not violate this generic claim.

= Achronal set
{parent=Globally hyperbolic spacetime}

An achronal set contains no pair of points connected by a timelike curve.

= Achronal boundary
{parent=Achronal set}

An achronal boundary is the boundary of a chronological future or past. Its null generators cease to belong to the boundary after developing a conjugate point or intersecting another generator in the relevant way.

= Cauchy surface
{parent=Globally hyperbolic spacetime}
{wiki}
{c}

= Cauchy hypersurface
{synonym}

= Cauchy slice
{c}
{synonym}

A Cauchy hypersurface is an achronal hypersurface met exactly once by every inextendible timelike curve. Its topology records the global spatial topology of a globally hyperbolic spacetime.

= Future domain of dependence
{parent=Cauchy surface}
{title2=$D^+(\Sigma)$}

The set $D^+(\Sigma)$ of events for which every past-inextendible <causal curve> meets $\Sigma$. This causal characterization permits an initial sign condition on a <null geodesic congruence> to propagate throughout the regular region by integrating a focusing inequality from $\Sigma$.

= Cauchy horizon
{parent=Cauchy surface}
{wiki}

The future or past Cauchy horizon of a partial Cauchy hypersurface is the boundary of the region whose physical evolution is determined by data on that hypersurface. Crossing it can destroy uniqueness of the maximal globally hyperbolic development.

= Dependence of a Cauchy horizon on the initial hypersurface
{parent=Cauchy horizon}
{title2=$H^\pm(\Sigma)$}

A complete <Cauchy hypersurface> in <Minkowski spacetime>, such as $t=0$, has full <domain of dependence> and empty <Cauchy horizons>. This does not forbid horizons for a non-Cauchy initial surface in the same spacetime. The smooth edgeless spacelike hyperboloid $t=\sqrt{a^2+r^2}$, $a>0$, has domain of dependence $t>r$ and a past <Cauchy horizon> on the future light cone of the origin. Every causal curve starting inside that cone crosses the hyperboloid in the direction needed for dependence: $t^2-r^2$ passes through $a^2$, while a null generator of the cone never meets it. The time-reversed hyperboloid gives the analogous future horizon.

= Inner-horizon blueshift coordinate
{parent=Cauchy horizon}
{title2=$V_-\propto-e^{-\kappa_-v}$}

Near a nondegenerate outgoing inner <Cauchy horizon>, a regular null coordinate behaves as $V_-\propto-e^{-\kappa_-v}$, where $\kappa_->0$ is the magnitude of the inner-horizon <surface gravity>. Transforming stress components gives $T_{V_-V_-}\propto e^{2\kappa_-v}T_{vv}$. Even a small power-law tail can therefore cause an unbounded locally measured flux, motivating <mass inflation>.

= Mass inflation
{parent=Cauchy horizon}

<Mass inflation> is the growth of the interior effective mass and curvature near an inner <Cauchy horizon> under suitably perturbed black-hole data. It can obstruct regular spacetime extensions that exist for an exact <Reissner-Nordstrom spacetime>. The extension regularity matters: curvature blowup alone does not rule out every continuous-metric extension.

= Black hole
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

= Black-hole region
{synonym}

In an asymptotically predictable spacetime, a black hole is the region that cannot send a causal signal to future null infinity. Its future boundary is the event horizon.

= Oppenheimer-Snyder model
{parent=Black hole}
{c}
{wiki=Oppenheimer–Snyder_model}

= Oppenheimer-Snyder collapse
{synonym}
{c}

The <Oppenheimer-Snyder model> describes a homogeneous ball of <pressureless matter> collapsing from rest, with a closed <FLRW metric> inside and a <Schwarzschild metric> outside. In geometric units its interior is $ds^2=-d\tau^2+a^2(d\chi^2+\sin^2\chi\,d\Omega^2)$, with $a=a_{\max}(1+\cos\eta)/2$ and $\tau=a_{\max}(\eta+\sin\eta)/2$. The boundary is a fixed comoving angle. <Density> conservation gives $\rho a^3$ constant, and the <Friedmann equation> at the initial turning point gives $8\pi\rho_0a_{\max}^2/3=1$.

= Oppenheimer-Snyder cloud
{parent=Oppenheimer-Snyder model}
{c}

The comoving dust ball $0\le\chi\le\chi_0<\pi/2$ in the <Oppenheimer-Snyder model> is an Oppenheimer-Snyder cloud. Its <pressureless matter> has uniform <density> on each constant-<proper time> slice and reaches a simultaneous final singularity in the ideal homogeneous model.

= Event horizon inside an Oppenheimer-Snyder cloud
{parent=Oppenheimer-Snyder model}
{title2=$\chi_{\mathcal H}(\eta)=\eta-\eta_H+\chi_0$}

Radial outgoing <null geodesics> in the interior of <Oppenheimer-Snyder collapse> obey $d\chi/d\eta=1$. Trace the ray crossing the boundary at $(\eta_H,\chi_0)$ backward: it starts at the centre at $\eta=\eta_H-\chi_0$. This ray is the <event horizon>; it can exist inside the cloud before the surface crosses $R=2M$. An <event horizon> is determined by global escape to <future null infinity>, rather than by a local stopwatch signal.

= Proper-time horizon crossing in homogeneous dust collapse
{parent=Oppenheimer-Snyder model}
{title2=$\eta_H=2\arccos\sqrt{2M/R_0}$}

A boundary clock in <Oppenheimer-Snyder collapse> reads $\tau=a_{\max}(\eta+\sin\eta)/2$. Its <event horizon> crossing occurs at $R=2M$, giving the displayed angle, and the final <curvature singularity> occurs at $\eta=\pi$. The remaining <proper time> is $a_{\max}(\pi-\eta_H-\sin\eta_H)/2$. For $R_0\gg2M$ this approaches $4M/3$, but it is not exactly that value for finite release radius.

= Homogeneous dust-ball matching
{parent=Oppenheimer-Snyder model}
{title2=$2M=a_{\max}\sin^3\chi_0$}

At the boundary of an <Oppenheimer-Snyder cloud>, its <areal radius> is $R=a\sin\chi_0$. Matching to a <Schwarzschild metric> gives $M=4\pi\rho R^3/3=a_{\max}\sin^3\chi_0/2$. Thus $\sin^2\chi_0=2M/R_0$ and $a_{\max}=\sqrt{R_0^3/(2M)}$. The boundary is a radial <timelike geodesic> with specific <Killing energy> $\sqrt{1-2M/R_0}=\cos\chi_0$. Its radial equation is $\dot R^2=2M/R-2M/R_0$. The induced metric and the angular <extrinsic curvature> agree on both sides, so no surface layer is required.

= Schwarzschild-Tangherlini metric
{c}
{parent=Black hole}
{title2=$f(r)=1-\mu/r^{D-3}$}

= Schwarzschild-Tangherlini black hole
{c}
{synonym}

For $D\geq4$, the static spherical vacuum metric $ds^2=-fdt^2+f^{-1}dr^2+r^2d\Omega_{D-2}^2$ has an <event horizon> at $r_h=\mu^{1/(D-3)}$. With time normalized at infinity its <ADM mass> is $M=(D-2)\Omega_{D-2}\mu/(16\pi G_D)$, where $\Omega_{D-2}$ is the unit-sphere area. For $D=5$ and $\mu=2m$, $r_h=\sqrt{2m}$ and $M=3\pi m/(4G_5)$. The spherical orbits are three-spheres, even though the radial-time <Penrose diagram> has the same causal pattern as its four-dimensional counterpart.

= Kruskal extension of the five-dimensional Schwarzschild-Tangherlini metric
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild-Tangherlini metric}
{title2=$-UV=e^{2r/r_h}(r-r_h)/(r+r_h)$}

The <tortoise coordinate> is $r_*=r+(r_h/2)\log|(r-r_h)/(r+r_h)|$. Set $u=t-r_*$ and $v=t+r_*$, then $U=-e^{-u/r_h}$ and $V=e^{v/r_h}$ in the right exterior. The extended radial metric is $-r_h^2(r+r_h)^2e^{-2r/r_h}dU\,dV/r^2$, regular at $r=r_h$. The horizons are $UV=0$, while $r=0$ gives $UV=1$ and is a spacelike curvature singularity. Compactification by $\arctan U,\arctan V$ displays two exteriors, a future <black hole> and a past <white hole>.

= Penrose inequality
{c}
{parent=Black hole}
{title2=$E\geq\sqrt{A/(16\pi)}$}

The <Penrose inequality> compares asymptotic energy and an appropriate horizon or enclosing area in <geometrized units>. Its physical motivation uses censorship, positive radiated energy, settling to a <Kerr black hole>, and <Hawking's area theorem>. The area variable requires care: the area of an arbitrary <apparent horizon> on non-time-symmetric data does not give a universally valid inequality. The <Riemannian Penrose inequality> uses the relevant outermost <minimal surface> under nonnegative <scalar curvature>.

= Riemannian Penrose inequality
{c}
{parent=Penrose inequality}
{wiki}
{title2=$m_{\rm ADM}\geq\sqrt{A/(16\pi)}$}

For complete asymptotically flat three-dimensional Riemannian initial data with nonnegative <scalar curvature> and an outermost compact <minimal surface> boundary of area $A$, the <Riemannian Penrose inequality> gives the displayed bound. Equality is realized by the spatial exterior of <Schwarzschild spacetime>. Time-symmetric gravitational initial data have this form when the <Einstein field equations> and energy hypothesis give the required nonnegative curvature. This theorem does not justify substituting arbitrary non-time-symmetric <apparent horizon> area into the same formula.

= Apparent-horizon area comparison
{parent=Penrose inequality}
{title2=$A_{\rm app}\leq A_{\rm EH,initial}$}

The heuristic derivation of the <Penrose inequality> from <Hawking's area theorem> needs an initial area comparison, in addition to enclosure of the <apparent horizon> by the <event horizon>. Set inclusion alone is not an area inequality. An <outer area-minimizing surface> or appropriate enclosing-area hypotheses supply the needed comparison in formulations where it is valid; arbitrary slice-dependent apparent-horizon area need not do so.

= Reissner-Nordstrom spacetime
{parent=Black hole}
{c}
{title2=$r_\pm=M\pm\sqrt{M^2-Q^2}$}

= Reissner-Nordstrom metric
{synonym}
{c}

= Reissner-Nordstrom black hole
{synonym}
{c}

The four-dimensional charged spherically symmetric electrovacuum solution has radial function $f=1-2M/r+Q^2/r^2$. For $0<|Q|<M$, its outer and inner horizon radii are $r_\pm=M\pm\sqrt{M^2-Q^2}$. A complete two-ended spatial bridge has a <maximal Cauchy development> ending at smoothly extendible inner <Cauchy horizons> in the exact solution.

= Reissner-Nordstrom horizon surface gravity
{c}
{parent=Reissner-Nordstrom spacetime}
{title2=$\kappa_+=(r_+-r_-)/(2r_+^2)$}

For $M>0$ and $M\geq|Q|$, put $r_\pm=M\pm\sqrt{M^2-Q^2}$. The stationary <Killing vector field> normalized at infinity generates the outer <Killing horizon>. In an ingoing null chart its covector is $dr$ on that horizon; the <Killing equation> then gives $\nabla_k k=\tfrac12 f'(r_+)k$, where $f=(r-r_+)(r-r_-)/r^2$. Thus the positive nonextremal <surface gravity> is $(r_+-r_-)/(2r_+^2)$. It tends to zero when $M=|Q|$, where the horizon becomes a <degenerate Killing horizon>. The past horizon branch has the opposite signed nonaffinity for the same time-translation field.

= Global structure of a charged spherical black hole
{parent=Reissner-Nordstrom spacetime}
{title2=$r_\pm=M\pm\sqrt{M^2-Q^2}$}

For $0<|Q|<M$, a <Reissner-Nordstrom spacetime> has an outer <event horizon> and an inner <Cauchy horizon>. Between them $r$ is timelike; inside the inner horizon $r$ is spacelike and the $r=0$ <curvature singularity> is timelike. The exact analytic extension contains repeated exterior blocks beyond the <Cauchy horizons>. Small perturbations produce severe inner-horizon blueshift and <mass inflation>, so that exact continuation is not a robust prediction for collapse. At $|Q|=M$ the horizons merge into a <degenerate Killing horizon>; for $|Q|>M$ the spherical electrovacuum solution instead has a <naked singularity>.

= Reissner-Nordstrom trapped spheres
{c}
{parent=Reissner-Nordstrom spacetime}
{title2=$r_-<r<r_+$}

In ingoing <Reissner-Nordstrom spacetime>, orient $-\partial_r$ to the future and normalize the other radial <null vector> to $\partial_v+(f/2)\partial_r$. The <spherical null expansion in ingoing coordinates> gives $-2/r$ and $f/r$. Hence the strictly future <trapped surfaces> among the round spheres occur exactly between the distinct horizons. At either horizon the outgoing <null expansion> vanishes. Inside the inner horizon the same round spheres are not future trapped.

= White hole
{parent=Black hole}

A white hole is the time reverse of a <black hole>: causal trajectories can emerge from its past singularity, while exterior future-directed trajectories cannot enter through its past horizon. The maximal <Schwarzschild spacetime> contains both a future black-hole interior and a past white-hole interior. Both have the same areal-radius range, illustrating why a global time orientation and choice of extension are essential in causal statements.

= Black-hole uniqueness theorem
{parent=Black hole}

In four-dimensional asymptotically flat <general relativity>, a regular stationary vacuum black hole is a <Kerr black hole> under the standard connectedness and analyticity hypotheses. The electrovacuum extension is the Kerr--Newman family.

= Black-hole rigidity theorem
{parent=Black-hole uniqueness theorem}

For a regular analytic stationary asymptotically flat vacuum black hole with a connected nondegenerate horizon, the stationary <Killing vector field> either generates the horizon or the spacetime admits an additional commuting axial <Killing vector field>. In the rotating case the horizon generator is $K+\Omega_H\Phi$, where $\Phi$ has closed rotational orbits. Together with the appropriate staticity and <black-hole uniqueness theorem>, this identifies an equilibrium uncharged collapse remnant with the <Kerr metric>. The theorem concerns an equilibrium geometry; it does not itself prove that arbitrary dynamical collapse settles to equilibrium.

= Black-hole no-hair theorem
{parent=Black-hole uniqueness theorem}

The black-hole no-hair principle says that radiation carries away the higher multipole structure of a collapsing isolated body, leaving a stationary black hole specified by conserved asymptotic charges such as <mass>, <angular momentum>, and <electric charge>.

= Extremal black hole
{parent=Black hole}
{wiki}

An extremal black hole lies at the parameter bound where its inner and outer horizons coincide. Its <surface gravity> and hence its semiclassical <Hawking temperature> vanish.

= Extremal Euclidean throat
{parent=Extremal black hole}
{title2=$V(r)\sim a(r-r_h)^2,\quad\int dr/\sqrt V\to\infty$}

At a degenerate static horizon the Euclidean proper radial distance diverges logarithmically, so the horizon is an infinite end rather than a finite polar origin. Its time circle is not contracted at a conical tip, and local cone regularity imposes no finite period. The vanishing limiting <surface gravity> determines zero Hawking temperature; the simple-horizon periodicity argument must not be applied unchanged at the degenerate root.

= Naked singularity
{parent=Black hole}
{wiki}

A naked singularity is a spacetime singularity visible to distant observers because it is not hidden behind an <event horizon>.

= Weak cosmic censorship conjecture
{parent=Naked singularity}

The <weak cosmic censorship conjecture> predicts, under appropriate generic isolated initial-data and matter assumptions, that collapse singularities are hidden from distant observers by an <event horizon>. It motivates exterior predictability in the physical argument for the <Penrose inequality>. It is distinct from the <strong cosmic censorship conjecture>, which concerns inextendibility of the <maximal Cauchy development>.

= Five-dimensional Reissner--Nordstrom black hole
{title2=Five-dimensional Reissner–Nordström black hole}
{c}
{parent=Black hole}

The static spherically symmetric electrically charged black hole in five spacetime dimensions has $f(r)=1-2M/r^2+Q^2/r^4$. When $M>Q$ it has outer and inner horizon radii $r_\pm^2=M\pm\sqrt{M^2-Q^2}$; for $M=Q$ it is an <extremal black hole>, while $M<Q$ exposes a <naked singularity>.

= Kretschmann scalar of a five-dimensional charged black hole
{parent=Five-dimensional Reissner--Nordstrom black hole}
{c}
{title2=$R_{abcd}R^{abcd}=288M^2/r^8-720MQ^2/r^{10}+508Q^4/r^{12}$}

For $V=1-2M/r^2+Q^2/r^4$, an orthonormal-frame curvature calculation gives $(V'')^2+6(V'/r)^2+12((1-V)/r^2)^2$. Expansion gives the displayed invariant, diverging at zero radius. It is finite at every positive root of V. Thus horizon coordinate singularities are distinct from the central <curvature singularity>.

= Causal trapping between two spherical horizons
{parent=Five-dimensional Reissner--Nordstrom black hole}
{title2=$\dot r\leq\frac f2\dot v-\frac{r^2|\dot\Omega|^2}{2\dot v}$}

In the regular metric $ds^2=-f\,dv^2+2\,dv\,dr+r^2d\Omega^2$, a future causal tangent has $\dot v\geq0$. For $\dot v>0$, causality gives $\dot r\leq f\dot v/2-r^2|\dot\Omega|^2/(2\dot v)$. If $f<0$ between two horizons, the areal radius must decrease. An inner event cannot reach the original exterior <future null infinity> without traversing that band in the forbidden direction.

= Tortoise coordinate of a five-dimensional charged black hole
{parent=Five-dimensional Reissner--Nordstrom black hole}
{title2=$\frac{dr_\star}{dr}=\frac1f$}

For distinct horizon radii $a>b>0$, integrating $dr_\star/dr=f^{-1}$ gives $r_\star=r+\frac{a^3}{2(a^2-b^2)}\log|(r-a)/(r+a)|-\frac{b^3}{2(a^2-b^2)}\log|(r-b)/(r+b)|+C$. The <tortoise coordinate> defines constant retarded or advanced coordinates on radial <null geodesics>.

= Komar integral of a five-dimensional Reissner--Nordstrom metric
{parent=Five-dimensional Reissner--Nordstrom black hole}
{c}
{title2=$\int_{S^3_\infty}\star dk^\flat=4\pi^2s$}

For $f=1-s/r^2+q/r^4$ and the spacetime orientation $-dt\wedge dr\wedge d\Omega_3$, the <Hodge star operator> gives $\int_{S^3}\star dk^\flat=2\pi^2r^3f^{\prime}\to4\pi^2s$. A prefactor $1/(16\pi)$ produces $\pi s/4$, whereas the dimension-adjusted ADM mass normalization produces $3\pi s/8$. The normalization and orientation must be specified rather than imported from four dimensions.

= Event horizon
{parent=Black hole}
{wiki}

An event horizon is the null boundary of the causal past of future null infinity. Its global definition makes it respond teleologically to matter that falls in later.

= Horizon-free Minkowski spacetime
{parent=Event horizon}

Every event in complete <Minkowski spacetime> emits a future-directed <null geodesic> ending on <future null infinity>. Therefore its black-hole region $M\setminus J^-(\mathcal I^+)$ is empty and there is no intrinsic event horizon. The null edges of the <Minkowski conformal compactification> represent infinity, not hidden regions inside the physical spacetime. An accelerated observer can instead have an observer-dependent <Rindler horizon>.

= Strong asymptotic predictability
{parent=Black hole}

Strong asymptotic predictability requires the exterior region visible from future null infinity to lie inside a globally hyperbolic neighborhood of the conformal completion. It is the cosmic-censorship-type hypothesis used in classical black-hole area and topology arguments.

= Black-hole non-splitting theorem
{parent=Black hole}

In a strongly asymptotically predictable spacetime, the causal future on a later Cauchy surface of one connected component of an earlier black-hole region lies inside one connected component of the later black-hole region. A classical black hole can merge with another component, but it cannot bifurcate into causally disconnected black holes.

= Myers-Perry black hole
{c}
{parent=Black hole}
{wiki}

A Myers-Perry black hole is the higher-dimensional asymptotically flat rotating vacuum generalization of the <Kerr black hole>. In six dimensions, a solution with one nonzero rotation parameter $a$ and horizon radius $r_+$ has mass parameter $\mu=r_+(r_+^2+a^2)$.

= Five-dimensional equal-spin Myers-Perry black hole
{parent=Myers-Perry black hole}

A five-dimensional <Myers-Perry black hole> with equal rotational parameters has a metric depending on a single radial coordinate. In a fibered coordinate system, its radial functions can be written $f=1-r_0^2/r^2+\alpha^2r_0^2/r^4$, $h=1+\alpha^2r_0^2/r^4$, and $\Omega=\alpha r_0^2/(r^4h)$. For $0<\alpha<r_0/2$ there are outer and inner <Killing horizons>; at equality they form a <degenerate Killing horizon>. With the standard sphere identifications and $G_5=1$, its <Komar mass> is $3\pi r_0^2/8$.

= Singly rotating six-dimensional Myers-Perry black hole
{c}
{parent=Myers-Perry black hole}

For the singly rotating six-dimensional Myers-Perry solution with $G=1$ and $\mu=r_+(r_+^2+a^2)$,
$$
M=\frac{2\pi}{3}\mu,
\qquad
\Omega_H=\frac{a}{r_+^2+a^2},
\qquad
\kappa=\frac{3r_+^2+a^2}{2r_+(r_+^2+a^2)},
$$
and the horizon area is
$$
A_H=\frac{8\pi^2}{3}r_+^2(r_+^2+a^2).
$$

= BTZ black hole
{c}
{parent=Black hole}
{wiki}

The BTZ black hole is a quotient of three-dimensional <anti-de Sitter spacetime>. In lapse-and-shift coordinates its outer and inner horizon radii are $r_+$ and $r_-$, and its outer-horizon surface gravity is
$$
\kappa=\frac{r_+^2-r_-^2}{L^2r_+}.
$$

= Conical singularity of negative-mass BTZ geometry
{parent=BTZ black hole}
{title2=$\delta=2\pi(1-\sqrt{-M})$}

For the nonrotating <BTZ black hole> family with $M<0$ and angular period $2\pi$, the circumference of a small circle divided by its proper radius tends to $2\pi\sqrt{-M}$. A smooth axis requires this ratio to be $2\pi$, so only $M=-1$ gives regular <anti-de Sitter spacetime>. Other values have a <conical singularity>, with deficit angle $\delta$ or an angular excess when $\delta<0$. The lapse $r^2/\ell^2-M$ is strictly positive, so outward <radial null geodesics> escape from arbitrarily near the axis: the defect is a <naked singularity>, despite finite local curvature away from it.

= Ingoing BTZ coordinates
{c}
{parent=BTZ black hole}

For
$$
ds^2=-fdt^2+\frac{dr^2}{f}+r^2(d\phi-\Omega dt)^2,
$$
the transformations $dv=dt+dr/f$ and $d\chi=d\phi+\Omega\,dr/f$ give
$$
ds^2=-f\,dv^2+2\,dv\,dr+r^2(d\chi-\Omega\,dv)^2,
$$
which is regular at every horizon where $f$ vanishes while $\Omega$ remains finite.

= Trapped circle inside a nonrotating BTZ black hole
{c}
{parent=BTZ black hole}

For the nonrotating BTZ metric in ingoing coordinates, future null normals to a circle of fixed $v,r$ may be chosen as $\ell=\partial_v+(f/2)\partial_r$ and $n=-\partial_r$. Their expansions are $\theta_{(\ell)}=f/(2r)$ and $\theta_{(n)}=-1/r$, so both are negative inside the outer horizon.

= BTZ causal singularity
{c}
{parent=BTZ black hole}

The BTZ black hole can be null-geodesically incomplete at $r=0$ even though every local curvature invariant is finite there. The endpoint comes from the global anti-de Sitter quotient rather than curvature blowup.

= Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter black hole
{title2=Schwarzschild-AdS black hole}
{c}
{parent=Black hole}

In four dimensions the Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter black hole has
$$
f(r)=1+\frac{r^2}{L^2}-\frac{2M}{r}.
$$
Writing the horizon radius as $r_+$ gives $M=r_+(1+r_+^2/L^2)/2$ and
$$
T_H=\frac{1+3r_+^2/L^2}{4\pi r_+}.
$$

= Five-dimensional Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter black hole
{title2=Five-dimensional Schwarzschild-AdS black hole}
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter black hole}

The spherical five-dimensional Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter metric has $f(r)=1+r^2/L^2-\mu/r^2$. Its horizon satisfies $\mu=r_H^2(1+r_H^2/L^2)$ and has area $2\pi^2r_H^3$.

= Canonical stability of a Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter black hole
{title2=Canonical stability of a Schwarzschild-AdS black hole}
{c}
{parent=Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter black hole}

The Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter heat capacity has the sign of $dT_H/dr_+$. It is positive precisely for $r_+>L/\sqrt3$, so these large black holes are locally stable in a fixed-temperature reservoir.

= Black string
{parent=Black hole}
{wiki}

A black string is a higher-dimensional black object whose horizon extends along one spatial direction. The direct product of a $d$-dimensional static black-hole metric with a line or circle gives a uniform black string in $d+1$ spacetime dimensions.

= Penrose diagram
{c}
{parent=Black hole}
{wiki}

= CP diagram
{c}
{synonym}

= Carter-Penrose diagram
{c}
{synonym}

A Penrose diagram conformally compactifies a spacetime while preserving causal directions, placing null infinity, timelike infinity, spacelike infinity, horizons, and singularities at finite coordinate locations.

= Schwarzschild conformal compactification
{c}
{parent=Penrose diagram}
{title2=$T=\tfrac12(\arctan V+\arctan U),\quad X=\tfrac12(\arctan V-\arctan U)$}

For the <Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates> of a positive-mass <Schwarzschild spacetime>, $UV=(1-r/(2M))e^{r/(2M)}$. The displayed <conformal compactification> makes radial <null geodesics> lines of slope $\pm1$. The <Killing horizons> are $T=\pm X$, and the spacelike <curvature singularities> $r=0$ lie at $T=\pm\pi/4$, with $|X|<\pi/4$. Two asymptotically flat exterior regions each have <past null infinity>, <null infinity> to the future, <timelike infinity> and <spacelike infinity>. The two exterior regions cannot communicate by any <causal curve>; future-directed curves that cross a future horizon instead end at the black-hole singularity.

= Spacelike infinity
{parent=Penrose diagram}
{title2=$i^0$}

<Spacelike infinity> is the ideal endpoint of spatial directions in the <Minkowski conformal compactification>, represented by $(T,R)=(0,\pi)$. Unlike <null infinity>, it is a vertex of the radial <Penrose diagram>.

= Timelike infinity
{parent=Penrose diagram}
{title2=$i^\pm$}

Future and past <timelike infinity> are ideal endpoints of asymptotically escaping timelike trajectories in a <conformal completion>. For the <Minkowski conformal compactification>, they are the vertices $(T,R)=(\pi,0)$ and $(-\pi,0)$; these vertices are not physical spacetime events.

= Past timelike infinity
{parent=Timelike infinity}
{title2=$i^-$}

This is the past endpoint of complete inertial <timelike geodesics> in the <Minkowski conformal compactification>, at $(T,R)=(-\pi,0)$. It is the time reversal of <future timelike infinity> and is reached at infinite physical <proper time> to the past.

= Future timelike infinity
{parent=Timelike infinity}
{title2=$i^+$}

This is the future endpoint of complete inertial <timelike geodesics> in the <Minkowski conformal compactification>, at $(T,R)=(\pi,0)$. The endpoint has finite unphysical coordinate time but infinite physical <proper time>. Accelerated timelike observers need not have this endpoint; a <Rindler horizon> can accompany an observer asymptotic to a null direction instead.

= Null infinity
{parent=Penrose diagram}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathscr I^\pm$}

<Null infinity> is the ideal boundary reached by asymptotically escaping <null geodesics> in a suitable conformal completion. It has future and past components. A finite location on a <Penrose diagram> need not represent a finite <affine parameter>.

= Past null infinity
{parent=Null infinity}
{title2=$\mathscr I^-$}

<Past null infinity> is the ideal boundary from which incoming <null geodesics> arrive in an <asymptotically flat spacetime>. In the <Minkowski conformal compactification> it is $T-R=-\pi$, with the retarded coordinate tending to $-\infty$ and the advanced coordinate finite.

= Causal projection along a spacelike circular fiber
{parent=Penrose diagram}

For $g=q+C(d\psi+A)^2$ with $C>0$, projection of every causal curve is causal for $q$, since $q(\dot\gamma,\dot\gamma)\leq g(\dot\gamma,\dot\gamma)\leq0$. Conversely, the lift defined by $d\psi=-A$ along any given base curve has the same norm as that curve. Thus the orbit-space metric $q$ describes the projected causal structure exactly. A <Penrose diagram> suppressing that circle is a quotient diagram, not the metric of a constant-$\psi$ slice.

= Tortoise coordinate
{parent=Penrose diagram}
{title2=$r_*$}

A <tortoise coordinate> straightens radial null directions. For a static radial metric $-F(r)dt^2+G(r)dr^2$ outside a horizon, choose $dr_*/dr=\sqrt{G/F}$; then $t\pm r_*$ are constant along the corresponding radial <null geodesics>. Continuation across horizons requires choosing branches consistently rather than replacing an analytic derivative by its absolute value.

= Black-hole thermodynamics
{parent=Black hole}
{wiki}

Black-hole thermodynamics relates horizon geometry to thermodynamic laws, assigning temperature and entropy to a stationary black hole.

= Black-hole energy extraction
{parent=Black-hole thermodynamics}

Black-hole energy extraction transfers part of a black hole's mass, charge, or rotational energy to its exterior. A reversible limiting process preserves the horizon area and saturates the maximum extraction permitted by the <first law of black-hole mechanics> and <Hawking's area theorem>.

= Laws of black-hole mechanics
{parent=Black-hole thermodynamics}

The laws of black-hole mechanics relate <surface gravity>, horizon area, mass, angular momentum, and charge in close analogy with the laws of thermodynamics. Quantum theory identifies the analogy physically through <Hawking temperature> and <Bekenstein-Hawking entropy>.

= Zeroth law of black-hole mechanics
{c}
{parent=Black-hole thermodynamics}

The zeroth law states that the <surface gravity> is constant over a stationary Killing horizon under the standard field-equation and energy assumptions.

= Hawking temperature
{title2=$T_H$}
{c}
{parent=Black-hole thermodynamics}
{wiki}

In units $G=\hbar=c=k_B=1$, a stationary black hole with surface gravity $\kappa$ has Hawking temperature $T_H=\kappa/(2\pi)$.

= Euclidean black-hole regularity condition
{parent=Hawking temperature}

Near a nonextremal Euclidean horizon, the radial--time plane is smooth only when Euclidean time has period $\beta_H=4\pi/f'(r_H)$. Any other period produces a conical singularity at the horizon.

= Euclidean metric near a simple static horizon
{parent=Euclidean black-hole regularity condition}
{c}
{title2=$ds_E^2\simeq d\rho^2+\tfrac14V'(r_0)^2\rho^2d\tau^2$}

On the side where V is positive, set $r-r_0=V'(r_0)\rho^2/4$. Its radial Euclidean metric becomes the displayed plane in polar coordinates. The would-be singularity is conical unless the angular variable $|V'(r_0)|\tau/2$ has period exactly $2\pi$. This fixes $\beta=4\pi/|V'(r_0)|$; a nontrivial integer multiple still has a cone excess at the tip.

= Euclidean temperature and signed horizon surface gravity
{parent=Euclidean black-hole regularity condition}
{c}
{title2=$T=|\kappa|/(2\pi)$}

For a normalized static metric $ds^2=-Vdt^2+dr^2/V+r^2d\Omega^2$ with a simple outer zero at $r_0$, set $b=V'(r_0)>0$. The <Wick rotation> and $r-r_0=b\rho^2/4$ give $d\rho^2+(b\rho/2)^2dt_E^2$ to leading order, so smoothness fixes period $4\pi/b$. In an ingoing null chart, $\nabla_k k=(b/2)k$ on the future horizon; the outgoing chart gives the opposite sign on the past branch. The <Hawking temperature> is $b/(4\pi)=|\kappa|/(2\pi)$ and uses the time generator normalized at infinity.

= Euclidean horizon period under a regular radial conformal factor
{c}
{parent=Euclidean black-hole regularity condition}
{title2=$\beta=4\pi/|f'(r_H)|$}

For a Euclidean radial-time metric $C(r)(f(r)dt_E^2+dr^2/f(r))$, assume $f$ has a simple horizon zero and $C$ is smooth, finite and positive there. A proper radial coordinate $\rho=2\sqrt{C(r_H)(r-r_H)/f'(r_H)}$ puts the leading metric in the form $d\rho^2+f'(r_H)^2\rho^2dt_E^2/4$. Smoothness fixes the displayed period independently of $C(r_H)$. When the Lorentzian time is normalized at infinity, the period is the inverse <Hawking temperature> in natural units. A conformal factor singular at the horizon need not preserve this argument.

// Target: general-relativity.bigb

= Hawking radiation
{c}
{parent=Hawking temperature}
{wiki}

Hawking radiation is the approximately thermal flux seen at future infinity when outgoing quantum-field modes are traced backward through a collapsing black-hole geometry. The exponential near-horizon redshift produces nonzero Bogoliubov beta coefficients at temperature $T_H=\kappa/(2\pi)$.

= Collapse vacuum for Hawking radiation
{parent=Hawking radiation}

In a collapse spacetime, choose the quantum field's incoming state to have no incoming particles at <past null infinity> and to be regular in freely falling coordinates near the forming <event horizon>. The late outgoing modes then have the <Hawking temperature>, while there is no incoming thermal bath. This collapse-state boundary condition differs from an equilibrium state on an eternal <black hole>. The <Hawking exponential ray map> and the <thermal ratio of Hawking Bogoliubov coefficients> determine the leading late-time particle spectrum; <greybody factors> describe scattering outside the <event horizon>.

= Black-hole evaporation
{parent=Hawking radiation}

<Black-hole evaporation> is mass loss through <Hawking radiation>. For a large isolated <Schwarzschild black hole>, the leading mass dependence is $dM/dt\propto-M^{-2}$ and the evaporation timescale is proportional to $M^3$. This semiclassical scaling cannot determine the endpoint when the curvature reaches quantum-gravitational scales.

= Semiclassical cubic mass law for Schwarzschild evaporation
{parent=Black-hole evaporation}
{title2=$M(t)^3=M_0^3-3\alpha t$}

For a large isolated <Schwarzschild black hole> with a fixed effective set of radiated species, <Hawking radiation> has $T\propto M^{-1}$ and luminosity $P=\alpha/M^2$ in natural units. Integrating $dM/dt=-\alpha/M^2$ gives the displayed law. A horizon-area <blackbody radiation> estimate gives $\alpha=1/(15360\pi)$; <greybody factors> and species change it. With mass-dependent $\alpha$, integrate $t=\int_{M(t)}^{M_0}m^2\,dm/\alpha(m)$ instead. The <semiclassical gravity> approximation fails near <Planck mass>, so a formal zero of the cubic law does not settle the final state.

= Stefan-Boltzmann estimate of Schwarzschild evaporation time
{c}
{parent=Black-hole evaporation}
{title2=$t_{\mathrm{evap}}=5120\pi G^2M_0^3/(\hbar c^4)$}

Approximate an isolated <Schwarzschild black hole> as emitting photon <blackbody radiation> from an area $16\pi G^2M^2/c^4$ at <Hawking temperature> $\hbar c^3/(8\pi Gk_BM)$. The <Stefan-Boltzmann law> gives $P=\hbar c^6/(15360\pi G^2M^2)$. Integrating $c^2dM/dt=-P$ gives the displayed lifetime, about $2\times10^{67}$ years for a solar mass. <Greybody factors> and additional emitted species modify the coefficient; the robust large-mass scaling is $M_0^3$, and the semiclassical calculation does not determine the final quantum-gravity endpoint.

// Target: general-relativity.bigb

= Hawking exponential ray map
{c}
{parent=Hawking radiation}
{title2=$U_H-U=Ae^{-\kappa u}$}

The late outgoing rays from a nonextremal collapsing <black hole> have an exponential relation between a regular early affine null coordinate $U$ and late retarded time $u$. Logarithmic divergence of the <tortoise coordinate> at the horizon gives $U_H-U=Ae^{-\kappa u}$ up to subleading terms. Backward propagation turns $e^{-i\omega u}$ into $(U_H-U)^{i\omega/\kappa}$; its positive- and negative-frequency Fourier parts give a thermal <Bogoliubov transformation>.

= Thermal ratio of Hawking Bogoliubov coefficients
{parent=Hawking exponential ray map}
{title2=$|\beta|^2/|\alpha|^2=e^{-2\pi\omega/\kappa}$}

For the <Hawking exponential ray map>, regulated integrals $\int_0^\infty x^{ia}e^{-(\epsilon\pm i\omega')x}\,dx=\Gamma(1+ia)(\epsilon\pm i\omega')^{-1-ia}$ have squared-modulus ratio $e^{-2\pi a}$ as $\epsilon\downarrow0$, where $a=\omega/\kappa$. Combining this with the <canonical identities for a bosonic Bogoliubov transformation> gives the bosonic occupation $[e^{2\pi\omega/\kappa}-1]^{-1}$. Continuum modes require wave-packet normalization to interpret finite particle counts.

= Greybody factor
{parent=Hawking radiation}
{title2=$\Gamma_\ell(\omega)$}

For a <Schwarzschild black hole>, a <greybody factor> is a mode's transmission probability through the exterior scattering potential. The spectrum of <Hawking radiation> at <future null infinity> is the thermal horizon occupation multiplied by this factor, with a sum over angular modes.

= Black hole information paradox
{parent=Hawking radiation}
{wiki}

The black hole information paradox is the apparent conflict between unitary quantum evolution and the semiclassical prediction that complete black-hole evaporation leaves only thermal <Hawking radiation>. A resolution must explain how information is preserved or why one of the assumptions behind the conflict fails.

= Bekenstein-Hawking entropy
{title2=$S_{BH}$}
{c}
{parent=Black-hole thermodynamics}
{wiki}

In units $G=\hbar=c=k_B=1$, a black hole of horizon area $A$ has entropy $S_{BH}=A/4$.

= Conical-singularity derivation of black-hole entropy
{parent=Bekenstein-Hawking entropy}

Changing the Euclidean time period away from its smooth value creates a curvature delta function at the horizon. Applying $(1-\beta\partial_\beta)$ to the Euclidean gravitational partition function isolates this term and gives $S=A/(4G_N)$.

= Gibbons–Hawking–York boundary term
{c}
{parent=Black-hole thermodynamics}
{wiki}

The Gibbons–Hawking–York boundary term makes the Einstein--Hilbert variational problem well posed when the induced boundary metric is fixed. It is the integral of the trace of the boundary's extrinsic curvature with the conventional normalization and sign.

= First law of black-hole mechanics
{c}
{parent=Black-hole thermodynamics}

For a nonrotating uncharged stationary black hole, the first law is $\delta M=\kappa\delta A/(8\pi)=T_H\delta S_{BH}$.

= Physical-process first law of black-hole mechanics
{c}
{parent=First law of black-hole mechanics}

The physical-process first law derives the area change caused by a small flux of matter through an initially and finally stationary horizon. Linearized <null Raychaudhuri equation> evolution converts the Killing-energy flux into $\Delta M=\kappa\Delta A/(8\pi)$.

= First-order horizon-area response to matter flux
{parent=Physical-process first law of black-hole mechanics}
{title2=$\delta A=8\pi\int\lambda\delta T_{kk}\,d\lambda\,dA_0$}

For a small perturbation of a stationary horizon, the linearized <null Raychaudhuri equation> gives $\delta\theta^{\prime}=-8\pi\delta T_{kk}$. With vanishing boundary term $[\lambda\delta\theta]$, integration by parts gives $\delta A=8\pi\int\lambda\delta T_{kk}\,d\lambda\,dA_0$. Combining this with <affine horizon-generator scaling> converts area response to horizon Killing-energy flux.

= Physical-process first law for a rotating black hole
{c}
{parent=Physical-process first law of black-hole mechanics}

For horizon generator $\xi=k+\Omega_Hm$, a small matter flux into an initially and finally stationary rotating black hole satisfies
$$
\Delta M-\Omega_H\Delta J=\frac{\kappa}{8\pi}\Delta A.
$$

= Second law of black-hole mechanics
{parent=Black-hole thermodynamics}

Under the <null energy condition> and the usual global assumptions excluding naked pathologies, the area of every cross-section of a future <event horizon> is nondecreasing toward the future. The <null Raychaudhuri equation> and future completeness rule out negative expansion of its generators.

= Hawking's area theorem
{c}
{parent=Second law of black-hole mechanics}
{wiki}

= Black-hole area theorem
{synonym}

Hawking's area theorem states that the total area of classical future event horizons cannot decrease when the <null energy condition> and the required global assumptions hold. It is the geometric content of the <second law of black-hole mechanics>.

= Future-complete horizon focusing proof of the area theorem
{parent=Hawking's area theorem}

For future-complete generators of an <event horizon>, the <null convergence condition> and hypersurface orthogonality reduce the <null Raychaudhuri equation> to $\theta'\leq-\theta^2/2$. If $\theta_0<0$, integration gives focusing within affine distance $2/|\theta_0|$. The <null focusing theorem> identifies this with a <conjugate point to a spacelike surface>. A null generator cannot remain on an <achronal boundary> beyond such a focal point, contradicting future completeness on the horizon. Thus $\theta\geq0$, and $dA/d\lambda=\theta A\geq0$ gives the <second law of black-hole mechanics>. New horizon generators may join toward the future at crease sets, adding area, but generators have no future endpoints on a regular event horizon under these assumptions.

// Target: quantum-field-theory.bigb

= Third law of black-hole mechanics
{parent=Black-hole thermodynamics}

The third law of black-hole mechanics says that no finite physical process can reduce the <surface gravity> of a regular black-hole horizon to zero.

= Generalized second law
{parent=Black-hole thermodynamics}

The generalized second law states that the sum of <Bekenstein-Hawking entropy> and the entropy of matter and radiation outside black holes does not decrease.

= Kerr black hole
{c}
{parent=Black hole}

= Kerr spacetime
{c}
{synonym}

The Kerr black hole is the asymptotically flat stationary axisymmetric vacuum black hole with mass $M$ and angular momentum $J=Ma$.

A Kerr black hole is the <Kerr metric> with mass $M>0$ and spin parameter satisfying $|a|\leq M$ in geometrized units; its outer horizon is $r_+=M+\sqrt{M^2-a^2}$.

= Lense-Thirring precession
{c}
{parent=Kerr black hole}
{wiki=Lense–Thirring_precession}
{title2=$\dot{\mathbf L}=\boldsymbol\Omega_{\rm LT}\times\mathbf L$}

Lense-Thirring precession is the rotation of orbital angular momentum caused by the gravitational field of a spinning mass. In the secular weak-field picture, the precession vector is parallel to the central spin. Thus an annulus has <torque> proportional to $\mathbf J_h\times\delta\mathbf J_d$, perpendicular to $\mathbf J_h$. Summing such torques preserves this orthogonality even if their radial coefficients differ. Dissipation and the internal response of a warped <accretion disk> can supplement precession with an alignment torque.

= Frame dragging inside a slowly rotating spherical shell
{parent=Lense-Thirring precession}
{title2=$\omega=\frac{4GM}{3R}\Omega$}

For a weakly gravitating thin spherical shell of mass $M$, radius $R$ and uniform angular velocity $\Omega$, its interior <linearized gravity> metric has constant scalar potentials $A=-GM/R$, $C=GM/R$ and $g_{0i}=\omega(\widehat{\mathbf z}\times\mathbf x)_i$. The scalar mass shell is a monopole. Its mass current is a dipole: regular interior and decaying exterior radial solutions are proportional to $r$ and $r^{-2}$, and their derivative jump fixes the factor $4/3$. Local inertial axes co-rotate with the shell at $\omega$ relative to asymptotic nonrotating axes. This is the leading interior <Lense-Thirring precession>, neglecting quadratic rotation and higher gravitational orders.

= Inertial coordinates inside a slowly rotating spherical shell
{parent=Frame dragging inside a slowly rotating spherical shell}
{title2=$T=(1-\epsilon)t,\quad\mathbf X=(1+\epsilon)\mathcal R_z(-\omega t)\mathbf x$}

At linear order in $\epsilon=GM/R$ and the leading rotation term, this coordinate change takes the interior shell metric to <Minkowski spacetime>. Consequently the retained interior <Riemann curvature tensor> vanishes although inertial axes rotate relative to distant reference axes. A freely moving particle has $\mathbf X=\mathbf X_0+\mathbf V T$, while its original coordinates obey $d^2\mathbf x/dt^2=2\boldsymbol\omega\times d\mathbf x/dt$ to first rotation order. Terms of order $\omega^2$ would include centrifugal acceleration and are outside this approximation. A <gyroscope> at rest to the retained order has its parallel-transported spin precessing at $\boldsymbol\omega$ in the original coordinates.

= Black-hole and disk alignment under internal torque
{parent=Lense-Thirring precession}
{title2=$\dot{\mathbf J}_h=-K_1\mathbf J_h\times\mathbf J_d-K_2\mathbf J_h\times(\mathbf J_h\times\mathbf J_d)$}

For the opposite internal torques on a <black hole> and its <accretion disk>, the total <angular momentum> $\mathbf J_t$ and the black-hole spin magnitude $J_h$ are constant. If $A=K_2|\mathbf J_h\times\mathbf J_d|^2$, then $d(\mathbf J_h\cdot\mathbf J_t)/dt=A$ and $dJ_d^2/dt=-2A$. The $K_1$ term produces <precession>; the positive $K_2$ term increases the alignment of the hole with the total vector while decreasing the disk angular-momentum magnitude. The exact direction equation is $\dot c=K_2J_hJ_t(1-c^2)$ for $c=\cos\angle(\mathbf J_h,\mathbf J_t)$.

= Counteralignment of a black hole and accretion disk
{parent=Black-hole and disk alignment under internal torque}
{title2=$J_t<J_h\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\cos\theta_0<-J_{d,0}/(2J_h)$}

After persistent dissipative alignment of the hole with the conserved total <angular momentum>, the final vectors are $\mathbf J_{h,f}=J_h\widehat{\mathbf J}_t$ and $\mathbf J_{d,f}=(J_t-J_h)\widehat{\mathbf J}_t$. The disk is therefore antiparallel to the hole if $J_t<J_h$. Squaring $J_t^2=J_h^2+J_{d,0}^2+2J_hJ_{d,0}\cos\theta_0$ gives the displayed initial-angle criterion. The disk and hole need not finish aligned with each other even though the hole aligns with the total. Equality $J_t=J_h$ leaves zero final disk angular momentum rather than a defined disk spin direction.

= Persistent-torque criterion for asymptotic spin alignment
{parent=Black-hole and disk alignment under internal torque}
{title2=$\int_0^\infty K_2(t)\,dt=\infty$}

For nonzero $J_h,J_t$ and a noncollinear initial configuration, the alignment equation integrates to $\operatorname{artanh}c(t)=\operatorname{artanh}c(0)+J_hJ_t\int_0^tK_2(s)\,ds$. Hence the positive dissipative torque aligns the hole with the total vector if its integrated strength diverges. A fixed positive coefficient is sufficient. Pointwise positivity alone need not suffice for time-dependent coefficients: with $J_h=J_t=1$, $K_1=0$, $K_2=e^{-t}$ and $c(0)=0$, the limiting cosine is $\tanh1<1$. Exactly antiparallel initial spin and total angular momentum form a stationary, nongeneric exception.

= Kerr axial analytic extension
{c}
{parent=Kerr black hole}
{title2=$ds^2=-Fdt^2+dr^2/F,\quad F=1-2Mr/(r^2+a^2)$}

The two-dimensional symmetry-axis restriction of the <Kerr metric> has simple horizons at $r_\pm$ for $0<|a|<M$, but is regular at $r=0$ because the ring is off-axis. Its static region below the inner horizon continues to an asymptotically flat end at negative infinity. The horizon blocks repeat in the maximal analytic extension. This ideal continuation is distinct from a claim of physical stability at the <Cauchy horizon>.

= Kerr axial timelike effective potential
{c}
{parent=Kerr axial analytic extension}
{title2=$\dot r^2+1-2Mr/(r^2+a^2)=\varepsilon^2$}

Conserved stationary energy and unit timelike normalization give this equation in the <Kerr axial analytic extension>. For $a>0$, the negative-sheet maximum of the potential is $1+M/a$ at $r=-a$. Energy squared above this maximum permits an inward orbit to pass through regular $r=0$ and continue to negative infinity without a turning point. At energy squared one, zero is a turning point. For negative spin the threshold is expressed with $|a|$.

= Kerr ring singularity
{c}
{parent=Kerr black hole}
{title2=$r=0,\quad\theta=\pi/2$}

For nonzero spin, the curvature singularity of the <Kerr metric> is the ring $x^2+y^2=a^2$, $z=0$ in oblate Cartesian coordinates. The rest of the $r=0$ disk is regular and allows continuation to negative $r$. The entire disk must not be mistaken for a curvature singularity, and axial trajectories do not hit the ring.

= Scalar wave separation in Kerr spacetime
{parent=Kerr black hole}
{title2=$\Psi=e^{-i\omega t+i m\phi}R(r)\Theta(\theta)$}

For the massless <Klein-Gordon equation> on a <Kerr black hole>, the <covariant wave operator> separates into radial and angular <ordinary differential equations>. With $K=(r^2+a^2)\omega-am$ and angular <separation constant> $\Lambda$, the radial equation is $(\Delta R')'+[K^2/\Delta-a^2\omega^2+2am\omega-\Lambda]R=0$. The angular equation is $(\sin\theta\,\Theta')'/\sin\theta+[a^2\omega^2\cos^2\theta-m^2/\sin^2\theta+\Lambda]\Theta=0$. No extra radial factor is included in the displayed ansatz.

= Scalar spheroidal harmonic
{parent=Scalar wave separation in Kerr spacetime}
{title2=$S_{\ell m}(\theta;c),\quad c=a\omega$}

= Spin-zero spheroidal harmonic
{synonym}

A <scalar spheroidal harmonic> is a regular angular solution of $(\sin\theta\,S')'/\sin\theta+[c^2\cos^2\theta-m^2/\sin^2\theta+\Lambda]S=0$; with $e^{im\phi}$ it is smooth at both axes. For real $c$, regularity gives discrete eigenvalues $\Lambda_{\ell m}(c)$. At $c=0$ it reduces to the angular part of a <spherical harmonic>, with $\Lambda=\ell(\ell+1)$. The sign of the $c^2$ term distinguishes the convention used here.

= Kerr horizon angular velocity
{c}
{parent=Kerr black hole}
{title2=$\Omega_H=a/(r_+^2+a^2)$}

The outer <Killing horizon> of a <Kerr black hole> is generated by $k+\Omega_Hm$, with the stationary <Killing vector field> $k$ normalized at infinity and the axial generator $m$ having $2\pi$-periodic orbits. In <ingoing Kerr coordinates> its generators satisfy $d\chi/dv=\Omega_H$. This is the horizon's angular velocity relative to the nonrotating asymptotic stationary frame.

= Ingoing Kerr coordinates
{parent=Kerr black hole}
{title2=$dv=dt+(r^2+a^2)\,dr/\Delta,\quad d\chi=d\phi+a\,dr/\Delta$}

= Advanced Kerr coordinates
{synonym}

<Ingoing Kerr coordinates> cancel the explicit inverse-$\Delta$ singularities of the <Boyer-Lindquist coordinates> metric. They give a smooth, nondegenerate <Kerr metric> at the future outer horizon when $M>|a|$. Its stationary and axial <Killing vector fields> are $\partial_v$ and $\partial_\chi$. The advanced chart need not cover every horizon branch of the maximal extension.

= Irreducible mass
{parent=Kerr black hole}
{title2=$M_{\mathrm{irr}}=\sqrt{A/(16\pi)}$}

The <irreducible mass> of a four-dimensional uncharged <Kerr black hole> is $M_{\mathrm{irr}}=\sqrt{A/(16\pi)}$. Its <mass> satisfies $M^2=M_{\mathrm{irr}}^2+J^2/(4M_{\mathrm{irr}}^2)$. <Hawking's area theorem> prevents a classical process from decreasing $M_{\mathrm{irr}}$ under the theorem's energy and global hypotheses.

= Area bound for an axisymmetric Kerr merger
{parent=Irreducible mass}

For initially well-separated <Kerr black holes> with no orbital <angular momentum>, exact <axisymmetry> gives $J_f=J_1+J_2$. Put $X=(A_1+A_2)/(8\pi)$. The <black-hole area theorem> and the <irreducible mass> identity imply
$$
M_f^2\geq\frac12\left(X+\frac{(J_1+J_2)^2}{X}\right).
$$
Indeed, $X\geq|J_1|+|J_2|\geq|J_f|$, and $\tfrac12(Y+J_f^2/Y)$ is nondecreasing for $Y\geq X$. Opposite spin signs lower $J_f^2$ at fixed initial areas and hence raise the allowed radiated-energy bound; this need not be achievable in an actual merger.

= Boyer-Lindquist coordinates
{c}
{parent=Kerr black hole}
{wiki}

Boyer-Lindquist coordinates adapt the Kerr metric to its stationary and axial symmetries. The horizon radii are the roots $r_\pm=M\pm\sqrt{M^2-a^2}$ of $\Delta=r^2-2Mr+a^2$.

= Kerr ergoregion
{c}
{parent=Kerr black hole}

= Ergoregion
{synonym}

The Kerr ergoregion is the region where the asymptotically timelike stationary Killing field becomes spacelike. Its outer boundary is $r=M+\sqrt{M^2-a^2\cos^2\theta}$.

The Kerr realization of an <ergosphere> lies outside the outer horizon where the stationary Killing vector is spacelike; its outer boundary is $r=M+\sqrt{M^2-a^2\cos^2\theta}$.

= Kerr stationary-limit surface is generically timelike
{c}
{parent=Kerr ergoregion}
{title2=$g^{ab}\partial_as\partial_bs=(\Delta+r_e'(\theta)^2)/\Sigma>0$}

For subextreme <Kerr spacetime>, the outer stationary-limit surface is $s=r-r_e(\theta)=0$, where $r_e=M+\sqrt{M^2-a^2\cos^2\theta}$. Away from the rotation axis, $r_e>r_+$ and $\Delta(r_e)=a^2\sin^2\theta>0$. Its normal is therefore spacelike and the surface timelike. Although the stationary <Killing vector field> has zero norm on it, it is not normal to it, and the surface is not a <Killing horizon>. The actual outer Killing horizon is $r=r_+$, generated by $K+\Omega_H\Phi$.

= Superradiance
{parent=Kerr ergoregion}

<Superradiance> is amplification of a scattered wave by extracting rotational or other available conserved energy from a background. For a rotating <Kerr black hole>, a bosonic mode of frequency $\omega$ and azimuthal integer $m$ can have negative horizon Killing-energy flux when $0<\omega<m\Omega_H$. The reflected energy can then exceed the incident energy. A stationary Killing flow need not define a globally positive particle energy.

= Causal angular velocity bounds in Kerr spacetime
{parent=Kerr ergoregion}

Outside a subextremal <Kerr black hole>, regular <causal curves> obey $(-g_{t\phi}-\sqrt D)/g_{\phi\phi}\le d\phi/dt\le(-g_{t\phi}+\sqrt D)/g_{\phi\phi}$, where $D=g_{t\phi}^2-g_{tt}g_{\phi\phi}>0$. Positive radial and polar metric terms establish this necessary bound. In the open <Kerr ergoregion> both endpoints are positive for $a>0$, forcing co-rotation.

= Penrose process
{c}
{parent=Kerr ergoregion}
{wiki}

The Penrose process extracts rotational energy by allowing a fragment with negative stationary Killing energy to enter the black hole while another fragment escapes with more energy than the original body carried.

= Horizon flux inequality for causal matter
{parent=Penrose process}
{title2=$E-\Omega_HL\ge0$}

For a future-directed causal four-momentum $p$ crossing a future <Killing horizon> with generator $\xi=t+\Omega_H\varphi$, the Lorentzian inner product obeys $p\cdot\xi\le0$. With $E=-p\cdot t$ and $L=p\cdot\varphi$, this is $E\ge\Omega_HL$. A massive particle has strict inequality. The same bound follows in the nonextremal quasistationary regime from the <physical-process first law for a rotating black hole> and the <second law of black-hole mechanics>. Negative Killing energy in a <Penrose process> is allowed, but its angular momentum must satisfy this bound.

= Kerr polar tortoise coordinate
{c}
{parent=Kerr black hole}

On the Kerr symmetry axis, the radial metric has $F=\Delta/(r^2+a^2)$ and the tortoise coordinate satisfies $dr_*/dr=F^{-1}=(r^2+a^2)/\Delta$.

= Kerr black-hole heat capacity at fixed angular momentum
{title2=$C_J$}
{c}
{parent=Kerr black hole}

Writing $s=\sqrt{1-J^2/M^4}=\sqrt{1-a^2/M^2}$, the heat capacity of a <Kerr black hole> at fixed angular momentum is
$$
C_J=4\pi M^2\frac{s(1+s)}{2-2s-s^2}.
$$
It is positive for $\sqrt{2\sqrt3-3}<|a|/M<1$, diverges at the lower endpoint, and tends to zero in the extremal limit.

= Stationary spacetime
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

A stationary spacetime admits a Killing vector field that is timelike in the region representing stationary observers, in particular near infinity for an asymptotically flat stationary black hole.

= Ergoregion of a stationary spacetime
{parent=Stationary spacetime}
{title2=$g(k,k)>0$}

For a chosen stationary time-translation <Killing vector field> $k$, this is the region where $k$ is spacelike and observers following its orbits cannot be timelike. In an asymptotically flat geometry, the usual choice is the nonrotating time translation at infinity. The stationary-limit boundary has $g(k,k)=0$, but need not be an <event horizon>. The <Kerr ergoregion> is a particular rotating-black-hole example: its outer boundary touches the horizon only on the axis for nonzero subextremal spin.

= Nonstationary spacetime
{parent=Stationary spacetime}

A <nonstationary spacetime> lacks the stationary time-translation symmetry in the domain being considered. Without a preferred suitable timelike <Killing vector field>, the classical geometry alone does not determine a unique <positive-frequency solution> subspace for quantization.

= Strictly stationary spacetime
{parent=Stationary spacetime}

A strictly stationary spacetime has a globally defined Killing vector that is timelike everywhere.

= Static spacetime
{parent=Stationary spacetime}
{wiki}

A static spacetime has a timelike Killing vector that is hypersurface orthogonal. Coordinates adapted to it can be chosen with no time-space cross terms.

= Static Killing field is a Ricci eigenvector
{parent=Static spacetime}
{title2=$R_{ab}k^b\ \parallel\ k_a$}

A timelike <Killing vector> with <hypersurface orthogonality> admits local coordinates with $k=\partial_t$ and $ds^2=N(\mathbf x)^2dt^2-h_{ij}(\mathbf x)dx^idx^j$. The only connection coefficients with time indices that can be nonzero are $\Gamma^0_{0i}=\partial_i\log N$ and $\Gamma^i_{00}=Nh^{ij}\partial_jN$. Substitution into the <Ricci tensor> gives $R_{0i}=0$. Thus $R_{ab}k^b$ has only its time component and is proportional to $k_a$. Equivalently $k_dR^d{}_{[a}k_{b]}=0$. This local statement does not require the <vacuum Einstein equations> and is independent of the overall curvature-sign convention.

= Static vacuum conformal spatial metric
{parent=Static spacetime}
{title2=$ds^2=-e^{2U}dt^2+e^{-2U}\gamma_{ij}dx^idx^j$}

Rescaling the spatial metric by the squared static <lapse function> gives a useful three-dimensional quotient metric $\gamma$. In <vacuum Einstein equations>, its <Ricci tensor> obeys $R_{ij}(\gamma)=2\partial_iU\,\partial_jU$, while $U$ is <harmonic>. The <contracted Bianchi identity> recovers the harmonic equation without dividing at critical points.

= Horizonless static vacuum rigidity
{parent=Static vacuum conformal spatial metric}
{title2=$U=0,\quad \gamma\cong\mathbb R^3$}

On a complete nonsingular spatial quotient with no inner boundary and standard <asymptotic flatness>, a bounded <harmonic function> tending to zero on all ends is zero. The <static vacuum conformal spatial metric> is then <Ricci-flat>; <three-dimensional curvature from the Ricci tensor> makes it flat. Completeness and an ordinary Euclidean asymptotic end exclude nontrivial flat quotients, yielding <Minkowski spacetime> with the standard global time coordinate.

= Conserved scalar-field energy in a static spacetime
{parent=Static spacetime}

The <stress-energy current from a Killing vector> associated with the static time translation gives a positive conserved energy for a massive real scalar. Its hypersurface independence follows from current conservation and the divergence theorem.

= Stationary axisymmetric spacetime
{parent=Stationary spacetime}

A stationary axisymmetric spacetime has commuting stationary and axial Killing vector fields, with the axial field having closed spacelike orbits.

= Arnowitt-Deser-Misner energy
{title2=$E_{ADM}$}
{c}
{parent=General relativity}

= ADM energy
{c}
{synonym}

The Arnowitt-Deser-Misner energy is the asymptotic surface charge generating time translations on an asymptotically flat spatial hypersurface. In asymptotically Cartesian coordinates,
$$
E_{ADM}=\frac1{16\pi}\lim_{r\to\infty}\int_{S_r^2}n^i(\partial_jh_{ij}-\partial_ih_{jj})\,dA.
$$

The Arnowitt-Deser-Misner energy is the asymptotic Hamiltonian boundary charge in the <ADM formalism> for an asymptotically flat spacetime.

= Arnowitt-Deser-Misner mass
{c}
{parent=Arnowitt-Deser-Misner energy}
{title2=$M_{\rm ADM}$}

= ADM mass
{c}
{synonym}

For an asymptotically flat stationary solution in its asymptotic rest frame, the <ADM mass> is the gravitational mass measured by the <Arnowitt-Deser-Misner energy>. It is fixed by the asymptotic metric falloff, not by a coordinate parameter's name. If spatial ADM momentum is present, distinguish the asymptotic energy component from the invariant mass obtained from the energy-momentum norm. For a static <Schwarzschild-Tangherlini metric> in $D$ dimensions with $f=1-\mu/r^{D-3}$, the mass is $(D-2)\Omega_{D-2}\mu/(16\pi G_D)$; the dimensional normalization is also reproduced by the <Komar mass normalization in higher dimensions>.

= ADM momentum
{c}
{parent=Arnowitt-Deser-Misner energy}
{title2=$P_i=(8\pi G)^{-1}\lim\int(k_{ij}-kh_{ij})s^j\,dS$}

For an asymptotically flat spatial metric $h$ and $k_{ij}=-g(\nabla_i n,e_j)$, the displayed surface integral gives ADM spatial momentum. Together with <ADM energy> it forms the asymptotic energy-momentum four-vector. Reversing the sign convention for extrinsic curvature reverses the corresponding surface-integral sign.

= Positive energy theorem
{parent=Arnowitt-Deser-Misner energy}
{title2=$E\ge|\mathbf P|$}

Complete asymptotically flat <spin> initial data obeying the Einstein constraints and the <dominant energy condition> have future-causal ADM four-momentum, under the appropriate decay and boundary hypotheses. A spinorial proof solves the <Witten spinor equation> and integrates the <Nester two-form>. Its divergence is a sum of a nonnegative spinor-gradient term and a matter-energy contraction with a future-causal <Dirac current>. Positivity for every asymptotic constant spinor yields the full inequality, rather than merely nonnegative energy in one frame.

= Nester two-form
{parent=Positive energy theorem}
{c}
{title2=$B^{\mu\nu}=\bar\epsilon\gamma^{\mu\nu\rho}\nabla_\rho\epsilon-\overline{\nabla_\rho\epsilon}\gamma^{\mu\nu\rho}\epsilon$}

This antisymmetric spinor bilinear converts the <positive energy theorem> into a boundary-charge identity. Its divergence is $2(\nabla_\nu\bar\epsilon)\gamma^{\mu\nu\rho}\nabla_\rho\epsilon+G^{\mu\nu}\bar\epsilon\gamma_\nu\epsilon$ in the mostly-plus conventions specified here. On a spacelike hypersurface the first term is twice the difference between the squared spatial <covariant derivative> and the squared spatial Dirac operator. The <Witten spinor equation> removes the negative square, while the <dominant energy condition> controls the <curvature> term through the Einstein constraints.

= ADM boundary term of the Nester two-form
{c}
{parent=Nester two-form}
{title2=$(8\pi G)^{-1}\lim\int b^is_i\,dS=EK^0_\infty-P_iK^i_\infty$}

For asymptotically constant spinors, the leading intrinsic <spin connection> term gives half the ADM metric integrand times $K^0_\infty$. The <Sen spinor connection> correction gives $-(k_{ij}-kh_{ij})K^j_\infty$. The constant-spinor derivative term integrates to zero by antisymmetry of the gamma matrices, and the remaining quadratic terms vanish under the standard decay. This identifies the <Nester two-form> flux with the ADM energy-momentum contraction, including momentum rather than just energy.

= Witten-Nester identity
{c}
{parent=Nester two-form}
{title2=$D_ib^i=2(|\nabla_i\epsilon|^2-|\gamma^i\nabla_i\epsilon|^2)+G_{\mu\nu}n^\mu K^\nu$}

In mostly-plus signature orient the commuting <Dirac current> to the future by $\bar\epsilon=-\epsilon^\dagger\gamma^{\hat0}$. For the <Nester two-form>, put $b^i=n_\mu B^{\mu i}$. Its covariant divergence has a spinor-gradient term and the <Einstein tensor contraction of spin curvature>. Projecting onto the hypersurface gives the displayed identity with positive spinor norm. The <Witten spinor equation> removes the negative square, and the <dominant energy condition> makes the matter term nonnegative after the Einstein constraints are imposed.

= Witten spinor equation
{parent=Nester two-form}
{c}
{title2=$\gamma^i\nabla_i\epsilon=0$}

This elliptic equation on a spacelike <spin> hypersurface uses the restriction of the spacetime <spin connection>, including <extrinsic curvature>. The spinor tends to a prescribed constant at spatial infinity. It removes the negative derivative square in the divergence of the <Nester two-form>. It is weaker than the spacetime <Killing spinor> equation; generic positive-energy initial data need not admit any <Killing spinor>.

= Elliptic existence of a Witten spinor
{parent=Witten spinor equation}

On complete smooth asymptotically flat <spin> initial data without inner boundary, satisfying the Einstein constraints and the <dominant energy condition>, the <Witten spinor equation> admits a spinor approaching each prescribed asymptotic constant, under the standard weighted decay assumptions. The integrated <Witten-Nester identity> gives a coercive estimate and excludes a decaying homogeneous kernel. Weighted elliptic solvability then supplies the desired correction to a spinor extended from infinity. Positivity of the boundary integral is a conditional identity until this analytic existence step is supplied; see https://users.math.msu.edu/users/parker/witten.pdf[the rigorous analytic proof].

= Sen spinor connection
{c}
{parent=Witten spinor equation}
{title2=$\nabla_i=D_i^{(3)}-\tfrac12 k_{ij}\gamma^j\gamma^{\hat0}$}

Restrict the spacetime <spin connection> to a spacelike hypersurface. With future unit normal $n$ and convention $k_{ij}=-g(\nabla_i n,e_j)$, it differs from the intrinsic spin connection by the displayed <extrinsic curvature> term. Its spatial <Dirac operator> is the operator in the <Witten spinor equation>; replacing it by the intrinsic operator would omit the ADM momentum contribution.

= Differentiability of the gravitational Hamiltonian at spatial infinity
{parent=Arnowitt-Deser-Misner energy}

For asymptotically Cartesian spatial metrics $h_{ij}=\delta_{ij}+O(r^{-1})$, unit asymptotic lapse and vanishing asymptotic shift, the curvature term in the bulk gravitational <Hamiltonian> has variation
$$
\delta H_{\rm bulk}=\text{bulk functional derivatives}-\frac1{16\pi G}\lim_{r\to\infty}\int_{S_r}n^i(\partial_j\delta h_{ij}-\partial_i\delta h_{jj})\,dA.
$$
The surface variation is finite under the usual differentiable asymptotic falloff and is not removed by simply taking the boundary to infinity. Adding <Arnowitt-Deser-Misner energy> cancels it and makes <Hamilton's equations> well defined for allowed variations that need not fix the energy. On the constraint surface, the total Hamiltonian for that time translation equals the boundary energy.

\Include[numerical-relativity]{parent=General relativity}

= Kasner metric
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

The Kasner metric is the anisotropic vacuum cosmology
$$
ds^2=-dt^2+\sum_{i=1}^3t^{2p_i}(dx^i)^2,
$$
where the vacuum Einstein equations require $\sum_i p_i=\sum_i p_i^2=1$.

= Bondi--Sachs formalism
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

The Bondi--Sachs formalism uses outgoing null hypersurfaces to describe asymptotically flat radiating spacetimes.

= Bondi news function
{c}
{parent=Bondi--Sachs formalism}
{wiki}

The Bondi news is the retarded-time derivative of the leading asymptotic shear and measures outgoing gravitational radiation at null infinity.

= Asymptotically flat spacetime
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

= Asymptotic flatness
{synonym}

An asymptotically flat spacetime approaches <Minkowski spacetime> sufficiently far from isolated sources. At null infinity this is stated invariantly by requiring a smooth <conformal completion> whose boundary contains future and past null infinity.

= Asymptotic simplicity
{parent=Asymptotically flat spacetime}

= Asymptotically simple
{synonym}

<Asymptotic simplicity> requires a suitable smooth conformal completion with $\widetilde g=\Omega^2g$, $\Omega>0$ in the physical spacetime, and $\Omega=0$, $d\Omega\ne0$ on null infinity, such that every inextendible null geodesic has both past and future endpoints there. It is stronger than merely possessing an asymptotically flat exterior. Null geodesics ending on singularities or on omitted finite-affine-distance horizons prevent this global condition.

= Conformal completion
{parent=Asymptotically flat spacetime}

A conformal completion embeds a physical spacetime $(\mathcal M,g)$ into a manifold with boundary carrying a regular metric $\bar g=\Omega^2g$. Infinity is represented by $\Omega=0$ with $d\Omega\ne0$; the causal character of each boundary component is determined by $\bar g^{ab}\bar\nabla_a\Omega\bar\nabla_b\Omega$.

This spacetime boundary construction is a <conformal compactification>, not an arbitrary topological compactification.

= Minkowski conformal compactification
{c}
{parent=Conformal completion}
{title2=$\bar g=-dT^2+dR^2+\sin^2R\,d\Omega^2$}

For four-dimensional <Minkowski spacetime>, set $p=\arctan((t-r)/L)$ and $q=\arctan((t+r)/L)$, with $L>0$, then $T=p+q$, $R=q-p$. Multiplication by the square of the <conformal factor> $2\cos p\cos q/L$ yields the displayed metric on $R\geq0$, $|T|+R<\pi$. Its radial <Penrose diagram> is a triangle: the timelike edge $R=0$ is the ordinary centre, while the two sloping edges are <future null infinity> and <past null infinity>. A signed Cartesian spatial coordinate in two dimensions gives a full diamond instead.

= Geodesic endpoints in Minkowski conformal compactification
{parent=Minkowski conformal compactification}

A complete inertial <timelike geodesic> in <Minkowski spacetime> has $\mathbf x=\mathbf b+\mathbf vt$ with $|\mathbf v|<1$. Under $p=\arctan(t-r)$, $q=\arctan(t+r)$, both $p,q$ tend to the same endpoint, giving <past timelike infinity> and <future timelike infinity>. A complete <null geodesic> has one finite limiting null coordinate at each end and reaches <past null infinity> and <future null infinity>. Complete <spacelike geodesics> have $r>|t|$ asymptotically and both ends approach <spacelike infinity>. The endpoints have finite compactified coordinates but occur at infinite physical <affine parameter>. A <conformal transformation> preserves null paths as unparametrized geodesics; it does not generally preserve timelike or spacelike geodesic paths.

= Projective Minkowski compactification
{c}
{parent=Minkowski conformal compactification}
{title2=$\overline M_L=(S^1\times S^3)/\mathbb Z_2$}

The Lorentzian real form of the <Klein quadric> is the projectivized real null cone of signature $(2,4)$. Scaling a nonzero null vector makes its positive two-dimensional and negative four-dimensional parts both unit vectors. Real projectivization then identifies $(u,v)$ with $(-u,-v)$, yielding the displayed quotient. Its universal cover is $\mathbb R\times S^3$, and its fundamental group is infinite cyclic. An affine Minkowski chart omits a null cone at infinity. This projective compact space is distinct from an unwrapped causal cover used for a <Penrose diagram>.

= Future null infinity
{title2=$\mathcal I^+$}
{parent=Conformal completion}

Future null infinity is the boundary reached by future-directed outgoing null geodesics in an <asymptotically flat spacetime>. In four dimensions it has topology $\mathbb R\times S^2$ under the standard asymptotic-flatness assumptions.

It is the future component of <null infinity>; the past component is $\mathcal I^-$.

= Gravitational lensing
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

Gravitational lensing is the deflection and focusing of light by spacetime curvature along the line of sight.

= Mass-sheet degeneracy
{parent=Gravitational lensing}
{title2=$\kappa\mapsto\lambda\kappa+(1-\lambda),\quad\gamma\mapsto\lambda\gamma$}

For a single source plane, adding a uniform sheet and rescaling the original projected lens mass as shown leaves the reduced shear $g=\gamma/(1-\kappa)$ unchanged. Rescaling source coordinates by $\lambda$ also preserves the image positions and shapes. Thus image geometry alone need not determine absolute lens mass normalization. Absolute magnification, independently known source sizes, time-delay information with a fixed distance scale, or additional geometric/dynamical constraints can break or restrict this <mass-sheet degeneracy>.

= Gravitational lens
{parent=Gravitational lensing}
{wiki}

A gravitational lens is a foreground distribution of matter whose gravitational field deflects light from a background source. Its <projected surface mass density> determines the mapping in the thin-lens approximation. The lens is the deflecting object; <gravitational lensing> is the resulting optical process.

= Lensing magnification
{parent=Gravitational lensing}
{title2=$\mu=1/[(1-\kappa)^2-|\gamma|^2]$}

The signed magnification is the reciprocal determinant of the <Jacobian matrix> of the <thin gravitational lens equation>. Its absolute value gives the flux and solid-angle amplification; its sign gives image parity. <Gravitational lensing> preserves <surface brightness> in the geometric-optics approximation.

= Thin gravitational lens equation
{parent=Gravitational lensing}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\beta=\boldsymbol\theta-\boldsymbol\alpha$}

A thin lens maps apparent image angle $\boldsymbol\theta$ to unlensed source angle $\boldsymbol\beta$. In the usual outward reduced-deflection convention, $\boldsymbol\alpha=\nabla_\theta\psi$ for a projected lensing potential. For an axisymmetric lens its magnitude is $4GM_{\rm projected}(<D_d\theta)D_{ds}/(c^2D_dD_s\theta)$. The mass here is projected inside a cylinder, not the three-dimensional mass inside a sphere.

= Softened isothermal lensing potential
{parent=Thin gravitational lens equation}
{title2=$\psi(r)=\theta_E\sqrt{r^2+\theta_c^2}$}

This circular lensing potential has reduced deflection $\alpha(r)=\theta_Er/\sqrt{r^2+\theta_c^2}$, mean <lensing convergence> $\bar\kappa=\theta_E/\sqrt{r^2+\theta_c^2}$ and local convergence $\kappa=\theta_E(\theta_c^2+r^2/2)/(r^2+\theta_c^2)^{3/2}$. Its central convergence is finite for $\theta_c>0$. As the core shrinks to zero, the deflection tends to that of a <singular isothermal sphere lens>. This softening of the projected lensing potential must not be confused with replacing a three-dimensional density by a different cored profile.

= Multiple-image caustic of a softened isothermal lensing potential
{parent=Softened isothermal lensing potential}
{title2=$\beta_c=\theta_c[(\theta_E/\theta_c)^{2/3}-1]^{3/2}$}

For $0<\theta_c<\theta_E$, the signed circular lens mapping $\beta=\theta[1-\theta_E/\sqrt{\theta^2+\theta_c^2}]$ has radial <stationary points> at $\theta_r^2=(\theta_E\theta_c^2)^{2/3}-\theta_c^2$. Substituting them gives the displayed caustic radius. An offset <point-like astronomical source> has three images when $0<|\beta|<\beta_c$, a merging pair plus a separate outer image on the fold, and one image outside. At alignment, the two off-centre images form a ring while a central image remains. For $\theta_c\geq\theta_E$ the mapping is monotone and there is no finite multiple-image region. The zero-core singular limit loses the central image and has a different image-count exception.

= Einstein radius
{c}
{parent=Gravitational lensing}
{title2=$R_E=D_l\theta_E$}

For a point lens of <mass> $m$ at observer distance $D_l$ and source distance $D_s$ in a nearby Euclidean geometry, the <Einstein radius> in the lens plane is $R_E=\sqrt{4GmD_l(D_s-D_l)/(c^2D_s)}$. Its angular version is $\theta_E=R_E/D_l$. This is the ring radius for perfect alignment and the characteristic transverse scale of a <gravitational microlensing> event. A crossing timescale is $t_E=R_E/v_\perp$, proportional to the square root of lens mass at fixed geometry and speed.

= Weak gravitational lensing
{parent=Gravitational lensing}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\beta=\boldsymbol\theta-\boldsymbol\alpha$}

When <gravitational lensing> gives small angular distortions without generic multiple imaging, its deflection field changes source positions, magnification and shear. Angular derivatives of the lensing potential determine these distortions. Ensemble galaxy-shape distortions and <cosmic microwave background> remapping probe the foreground gravitational field.

= Cosmic shear
{parent=Weak gravitational lensing}
{wiki}

Cosmic shear is the statistically correlated <lensing shear> of distant <galaxies> produced by the intervening large-scale matter distribution. Correlations across angle and source redshift constrain clustering, the growth of structure and distance geometry, thereby testing dark matter and dark energy. Intrinsic galaxy alignments and measurement errors must be separated from the lensing signal.

= Lensing shear
{parent=Weak gravitational lensing}
{title2=$\gamma_1=(\psi_{,11}-\psi_{,22})/2,\quad\gamma_2=\psi_{,12}$}

The trace-free part of the angular lensing-potential Hessian stretches an image anisotropically. The source-image <Jacobian matrix> has eigenvalues $1-\kappa\pm|\gamma|$. Thus a circular source becomes an ellipse in the local linear approximation; the orientation follows the shear axes.

= Lensing convergence
{parent=Weak gravitational lensing}
{title2=$\kappa=\tfrac12\nabla_\theta\cdot\boldsymbol\alpha$}

The trace of the angular derivative of the reduced deflection defines convergence in the standard <thin gravitational lens equation> convention. It equals projected <surface mass density> divided by the critical surface density, $\Sigma_{\rm crit}=c^2D_s/(4\pi GD_dD_{ds})$. If deflection is instead defined as source minus image angle, the divergence relation has a minus sign.

= Angular deflection kernel for cosmological lensing
{parent=Weak gravitational lensing}
{title2=$\alpha^i=2\int_0^{\chi_s}\frac{\chi_s-\chi}{\chi_s}\partial_\perp^i\Psi\,d\chi$}

For a source at comoving distance $\chi_s$ in a spatially flat universe, define deflection as apparent minus unlensed source direction. The transverse <Weyl lensing potential> gradient along the ray receives the distance weight $(\chi_s-\chi)/\chi_s$. Integrating the transverse photon acceleration twice and dividing the source displacement by $\chi_s$ gives the formula. Reversing the definition of the deflection reverses its sign. Its angular lensing potential is $2\int_0^{\chi_s}(\chi_s-\chi)\Psi/(\chi_s\chi)\,d\chi$.

= Born approximation in cosmological lensing
{parent=Gravitational lensing}
{c}
{title2=$\Psi(\eta,\mathbf x)\simeq\Psi(\eta,\mathbf x_{\rm background})$}

In a first-order light-deflection calculation, evaluate the <Weyl lensing potential> and its gradients on the unperturbed <null geodesic>. The position correction is first order, so inserting it into an already first-order potential produces a second-order correction. This approximation omits repeated deflection and lens-lens coupling.

= Strong gravitational lensing
{parent=Gravitational lensing}
{wiki}

Strong gravitational lensing produces multiple resolved images, arcs, or rings when a sufficiently massive foreground lens lies close to the source's line of sight.

= Gravitational-lensing critical curve
{parent=Strong gravitational lensing}
{title2=$\det A(\boldsymbol\theta)=0$}

A critical curve is a locus in the image plane where the <Jacobian matrix> of the <thin gravitational lens equation> becomes singular. Its formal point-source <lensing magnification> diverges, while finite source size regularizes the measured flux. Its mapped locus in the source plane is a <gravitational-lensing caustic>.

= Radial critical curve of an axisymmetric lens
{parent=Gravitational-lensing critical curve}
{title2=$\lambda_r=1-2\kappa+\bar\kappa=0$}

The local and mean <lensing convergence> obey $2\kappa=2\bar\kappa+r\bar\kappa'$. Hence the radial <derivative> of the lens mapping is $1-\alpha'(r)=1-2\kappa+\bar\kappa$. Its zeros are radial <gravitational-lensing critical curves>, where radial stretching diverges and images merge on crossing the mapped <gravitational-lensing caustic>.

= Tangential critical curve of an axisymmetric lens
{parent=Gravitational-lensing critical curve}
{title2=$\lambda_t=1-\bar\kappa=0$}

For circular reduced deflection $\alpha(r)=r\bar\kappa(r)$, a displacement perpendicular to the radial direction is multiplied by $1-\bar\kappa$. Vanishing of this <eigenvalue> gives a tangential <gravitational-lensing critical curve>. Its source position is $\beta=r-\alpha(r)=0$, so its caustic is the origin in the circular model. Near it, images are stretched tangentially into arcs or an <Einstein ring>.

= Gravitational-lensing caustic
{parent=Gravitational-lensing critical curve}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\beta(\boldsymbol\theta_{\rm crit})$}

A caustic is the source-plane image of a <gravitational-lensing critical curve>. Crossing a generic fold caustic creates or destroys a pair of images of opposite parity. For a circular lens, a tangential critical circle maps to an aligned-source point, while a radial critical circle can map to a finite source-plane circle. Critical curves and caustics consequently occupy different planes and must not be identified as the same angular locus.

= Einstein ring
{c}
{parent=Strong gravitational lensing}
{wiki}

An aligned source behind an axisymmetric <gravitational lens> produces a circular image at an <Einstein radius>. Its angular radius satisfies $\theta_E^2=4GM_{\rm projected}(<D_d\theta_E)D_{ds}/(c^2D_dD_s)$. Exact alignment gives a ring; displaced sources generally give arcs or distinct images rather than a complete ring.

= Gravitational microlensing
{parent=Gravitational lensing}
{wiki}

Gravitational microlensing is lensing by compact masses whose individual image splitting is unresolved, so their motion is observed through time-dependent magnification.

= Microlensing optical depth
{parent=Gravitational microlensing}
{title2=$\tau=\int n_l\pi R_E^2\,dD_l$}

The <microlensing optical depth> is the probability, when small, that a source lies within one <Einstein radius> of a foreground lens. For point lenses and compact-object <mass density> $\rho_l$ in nearby Euclidean geometry,
$$
\tau=\frac{4\pi G}{c^2}\int_0^{D_s}\rho_l(D_l)\frac{D_l(D_s-D_l)}{D_s}\,dD_l.
$$
The individual lens mass cancels because $n_l=\rho_l/m$ and $R_E^2\propto m$. Detection efficiencies and event durations still depend on individual masses and survey cadence.

= Perfect fluid
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

A perfect fluid has no viscosity or heat flux in its local rest frame; its stress is specified by energy density and isotropic pressure. Its relativistic realization is the <perfect fluid in general relativity>.

= Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki=Eddington–Finkelstein_coordinates}

The advanced time $v=t+r_*$ or retarded time $u=t-r_*$ combines Schwarzschild time with the <Schwarzschild tortoise coordinate> to remove one horizon-coordinate singularity. The advanced choice gives <ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates>; the retarded choice is outgoing.

= Conformally flat manifold
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

A (pseudo-)<Riemannian manifold> is conformally flat if every point has a neighborhood on which the metric is $g=\Omega^2\eta$ for a flat metric $\eta$ and a positive function $\Omega$. This is a local property and does not imply global flatness.

= Kerr metric
{c}
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

The Kerr metric is the stationary axisymmetric vacuum metric of a rotating mass. Parameters $M$ and $a$ determine whether its geometry has the horizons of a <Kerr black hole>; the metric also admits over-extremal parameters with no horizon.

= Ergosphere
{parent=General relativity}
{wiki}

An ergosphere is the region outside a rotating black hole horizon in which the stationary time-translation <Killing vector> is spacelike. Observers cannot remain stationary relative to infinity there. The <Kerr ergoregion> is its Kerr-metric instance.
